window.EXPLAINED={"B1":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Blood banking (immunohematology) is about giving blood safely. The core idea is simple: <b>antigens<\/b> sit on red cells, and <b>antibodies<\/b> in plasma attack red cells carrying antigens the body doesn't recognise. Every test in the blood bank looks for one or the other. The ABO system matters most, because its antibodies are already present without any previous transfusion and can destroy incompatible blood within minutes.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The 8 big ideas, explained<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Group O red cells are the universal donor; group AB plasma is the universal plasma.<\/b> O red cells have no A or B antigen for anyone's antibodies to attack. AB plasma has no anti-A or anti-B to attack anyone's red cells. Forward and reverse typing must agree before you report a group.<\/li>\n<li><b>ABO antibodies are IgM and react at room temperature<\/b>; Rh, Kell, Kidd and Duffy antibodies are <b>IgG<\/b> and react at <b>37 °C / AHG<\/b>. IgM is a big pentamer that can bridge cells directly; IgG is small and usually needs help (AHG) to show agglutination.<\/li>\n<li><b>After A and B, D is the most immunogenic antigen<\/b>: a D-negative person exposed to D-positive blood is very likely to make anti-D.<\/li>\n<li><b>DAT vs IAT.<\/b> The DAT asks \"is the patient's own red cell coated <i>in the body<\/i>?\" The IAT asks \"is there antibody in the patient's <i>serum<\/i>?\" (tested in the tube). A negative antiglobulin test needs <b>check cells<\/b> to prove it was valid.<\/li>\n<li><b>Kidd antibodies cause delayed hemolytic reactions and fade quickly. Duffy antigens are destroyed by enzymes.<\/b><\/li>\n<li><b>Suspected transfusion reaction:<\/b> stop the transfusion, keep the IV line open with saline, check all identification, and send a post-transfusion sample.<\/li>\n<li><b>RhIG<\/b> protects D-negative mothers from making anti-D. Give it within <b>72 hours<\/b> of delivery of a D-positive baby; the <b>Kleihauer-Betke<\/b> test measures how much fetal blood entered the mother.<\/li>\n<li><b>Shelf lives:<\/b> red cells 42 days (in additive solution), platelets 5 days at room temperature with agitation, plasma 1 year frozen.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>How the ABO system works<\/h3>\n<p>All four blood groups start with the same building block, the <b>H antigen<\/b>. Genes then add one sugar to it:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>A gene<\/b> adds N-acetylgalactosamine → <b>A antigen<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>The <b>B gene<\/b> adds galactose → <b>B antigen<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>The <b>O gene<\/b> adds nothing, so group O cells carry <b>lots of unchanged H<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Early in life, gut bacteria carry sugars that look like A and B antigens. The immune system makes antibodies against whichever of these it <i>doesn't<\/i> have itself. That's why ABO antibodies are \"naturally occurring\" and present without any transfusion or pregnancy:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Group <b>A<\/b>: A antigen, <b>anti-B<\/b> in the plasma.<\/li>\n<li>Group <b>B<\/b>: B antigen, <b>anti-A<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Group <b>AB<\/b>: both antigens, <b>no ABO antibodies<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Group <b>O<\/b>: neither (only H), <b>anti-A, anti-B and anti-A,B<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why ABO mistakes are so dangerous<\/b>Anti-A and anti-B are mostly IgM and strongly activate complement. If group A blood is given to a group O patient, the patient's anti-A coats the donor cells and complement punches holes in them inside the blood vessels: <b>acute intravascular hemolysis<\/b>, which can cause shock, kidney failure, DIC and death. Most of these errors are <b>clerical<\/b> (wrong patient, wrong label).<\/div>\n\n<h3>Forward and reverse typing: two tests that check each other<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Forward typing<\/b> tests the patient's <b>red cells<\/b> with reagent anti-A and anti-B. Which antigens are on the cells?<\/li>\n<li><b>Reverse typing<\/b> tests the patient's <b>serum/plasma<\/b> with reagent <b>A1 and B cells<\/b>. Which antibodies are in the plasma?<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The two must match what's expected. A group A person should show A on the cells <i>and<\/i> anti-B in the serum. If they disagree, you have an <b>ABO discrepancy<\/b>, and you must resolve it before giving group-specific blood (give group O red cells meanwhile if needed).<\/p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Forward: anti-A 4+, anti-B 0. Reverse: A1 cells 0, B cells 4+. Cells carry A; serum has anti-B → <b>group A<\/b>.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Solving ABO discrepancies<\/h3>\n<p>Ask: <b>is something missing, or is something extra?<\/b> Then match the clue to the cause:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Missing reverse reaction<\/b> (weak or absent antibodies): newborns (who don't make their own ABO antibodies until about 3-6 months, so <b>cord blood is forward-typed only<\/b>), elderly people, and patients with low immunoglobulins. Fix: incubate longer or at 4 °C (with controls) to strengthen weak IgM.<\/li>\n<li><b>Extra reverse reaction with A1 cells only<\/b> in a group A person: an <b>A2 subgroup with anti-A1<\/b>. A2 cells have less A antigen and some A2 people make anti-A1. Confirm by testing their cells with <b>anti-A1 lectin<\/b> (Dolichos biflorus): A2 cells don't react.<\/li>\n<li><b>Extra reactions at room temperature everywhere<\/b>: a <b>cold autoantibody<\/b> (often anti-I). Fix: prewarm to 37 °C, or wash the cells with warm saline.<\/li>\n<li><b>Stacked cells in forward and reverse<\/b>: <b>rouleaux<\/b> from high protein (myeloma). Fix: <b>saline replacement<\/b>, which disperses rouleaux but not true agglutination.<\/li>\n<li><b>Weak extra B on a group A patient<\/b>: <b>acquired B<\/b>. Bacterial enzymes in colon cancer or gut infection convert A sugar into something that looks like B. Fix: acidified anti-B or a different reagent clone; the patient's own serum still contains anti-B but doesn't react with their own cells.<\/li>\n<li><b>Mixed-field<\/b> (a mix of clumps and free cells): two red cell populations, as after transfusion of group O cells to an A patient, or after a stem cell transplant. Fix: review the history.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>The Bombay phenotype<\/h3>\n<p>Bombay people (genotype <b>hh<\/b>) can't make the H antigen at all, so they can't make A or B either (there's no H to build on). Their cells <b>type as group O<\/b>. But because they lack H completely, they also make <b>anti-H<\/b>, which reacts strongly with group O cells (which are full of H). So a Bombay patient's serum reacts with <b>every<\/b> group O cell, including screening cells. They can only receive <b>Bombay blood<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>ABO genetics<\/h3>\n<p>A and B are <b>codominant<\/b> (both show if both present) and both are dominant over O, which is silent. Each person has two genes, one from each parent:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Group A can be <b>AA or AO<\/b>; group B can be <b>BB or BO<\/b>; group AB is <b>AB<\/b>; group O is <b>OO<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Parents <b>AO × BO<\/b> can have children of <b>all four groups<\/b> (AB, AO, BO, OO).<\/li>\n<li>Parents <b>AB × OO<\/b> can only have <b>A (AO) or B (BO)<\/b> children; never O or AB.<\/li>\n<li>Two group <b>O<\/b> parents can only have <b>O<\/b> children.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For Rh: D-negative people are <b>dd<\/b>, so two D-negative parents can only have D-negative children. A D-positive parent may be DD or Dd.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Forward: no reaction with anti-A or anti-B. Reverse: 4+ with A1 and B cells. Group?<\/summary><p>Group O.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is reverse typing not done on cord blood?<\/summary><p>Newborns haven't made their own anti-A or anti-B yet; any antibody present is maternal IgG, so the reverse type would be misleading.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient types as group O but their serum reacts with all group O screening and donor cells. What do you suspect?<\/summary><p>Bombay phenotype (anti-H). Only Bombay donor blood is compatible.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A group A patient's serum reacts 2+ with A1 cells, and their cells don't react with anti-A1 lectin. Explanation?<\/summary><p>A2 (or weaker A) subgroup with anti-A1.<\/p><\/details>"},"B2":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">After ABO, the most important blood group is Rh, and then a handful of others: Kell, Duffy, Kidd, MNS, Lewis, P and I. You don't need to memorise hundreds of antigens. For each system you need to know: is the antibody <b>IgM or IgG<\/b> (harmless cold reactor, or dangerous warm reactor)? What do <b>enzymes and DTT<\/b> do to it? And does it cause <b>hemolysis or HDFN<\/b>?<\/p>\n\n<h3>The Rh system<\/h3>\n<p>\"Rh positive\" and \"Rh negative\" refer only to the <b>D antigen<\/b>. D is the most immunogenic antigen after A and B: most D-negative people exposed to D-positive red cells (by transfusion or pregnancy) make anti-D. Unlike ABO, there are <b>no naturally occurring<\/b> Rh antibodies; they appear only after exposure.<\/p>\n<p>Rh antibodies are <b>IgG<\/b>, react at <b>37 °C / AHG<\/b>, and usually <b>don't activate complement<\/b>. Coated cells are removed by macrophages in the spleen (<b>extravascular hemolysis<\/b>). IgG crosses the placenta, which is why anti-D is the classic cause of severe <b>HDFN<\/b>.<\/p>\n<p>The Rh system has five main antigens: <b>D, C, c, E, e<\/b>. They are inherited together as a set (haplotype) on one chromosome. The shorthand (Wiener) names, worth learning:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>R1 = DCe<\/b> (most common in people of European descent), <b>R2 = DcE<\/b>, <b>R0 = Dce<\/b> (common in people of African descent), <b>Rz = DCE<\/b> (rare).<\/li>\n<li><b>r = dce<\/b> (the usual D-negative set), <b>r' = dCe<\/b>, <b>r'' = dcE<\/b>, <b>ry = dCE<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: reading the shorthand<\/b>Capital <b>R<\/b> = D present; small <b>r<\/b> = no D. <b>1<\/b> (or a single prime ') means C; <b>2<\/b> (or a double prime \") means E; <b>0<\/b> means neither C nor E; <b>z / y<\/b> means both.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Cells react with anti-D, anti-C and anti-e, but not anti-c or anti-E. The person has D, C and e only, so most probably <b>R1R1 (DCe/DCe)<\/b>.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Weak D<\/h3>\n<p>Some people have D antigen in such small amounts (or slightly altered) that reagent anti-D only reacts after the AHG step. This is <b>weak D<\/b>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Donors<\/b> are tested for weak D, and weak-D donors are <b>labeled Rh positive<\/b>, because their cells could still immunise a D-negative recipient.<\/li>\n<li><b>Patients<\/b>: practice depends on lab policy. Many patients with serologic weak D are given D-negative blood and RhIG when pregnant until RHD genotyping shows a weak D type (types 1, 2 or 3) that can safely be treated as D-positive.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Rh null<\/b> cells lack all Rh antigens. The Rh proteins help hold the membrane together, so Rh null cells are <b>stomatocytes<\/b> with chronic hemolysis.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Enzymes and DTT: chemical tools for antibody work<\/h3>\n<p>Treating red cells with enzymes (<b>ficin, papain<\/b>) strips off some surface structures. That makes some antigens <b>easier to reach<\/b> and destroys others:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Enhanced<\/b> (react more strongly): <b>Rh, Kidd, Lewis, I, P1<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Destroyed<\/b> (no longer react): <b>Duffy (Fya, Fyb), M, N, S<\/b> (S is variable) and Xga.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>DTT<\/b> (and AET) break disulfide bonds. They <b>destroy Kell antigens<\/b> (and others such as Cartwright, Dombrock, Knops), and in serum they break up IgM pentamers. Useful for removing interference from anti-CD38 drugs (daratumumab) in myeloma patients.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: enzymes<\/b>\"<b>Enzymes<\/b> make the <b>Duffy MNS<\/b> go away.\" Everything in Rh, Kidd, Lewis, I and P1 gets stronger.<\/div>\n\n<h3>The other blood groups<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Kell (K, k)<\/b>: <b>IgG<\/b>, very immunogenic (second only to D). Anti-K causes hemolytic reactions and severe HDFN, because it also attacks red cell precursors in the fetal marrow. About 91% of people are K-negative. Destroyed by DTT and AET.<\/li>\n<li><b>Duffy (Fya, Fyb)<\/b>: <b>IgG<\/b>, destroyed by enzymes. The <b>Fy(a−b−)<\/b> phenotype, common in people of African ancestry, lacks the receptor <b>P. vivax<\/b> malaria uses to enter red cells, so it protects against vivax.<\/li>\n<li><b>Kidd (Jka, Jkb)<\/b>: <b>IgG<\/b> that <b>binds complement<\/b>. Kidd antibodies are famous for two dangerous habits: their level <b>falls quickly<\/b> after an exposure (so they can be undetectable on a later screen), then they bounce back rapidly when the antigen is seen again (anamnestic response), causing a <b>delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction<\/b> days later. Always honour a past history of anti-Jka or anti-Jkb.<\/li>\n<li><b>MNS<\/b>: <b>M and N<\/b> antibodies are usually <b>IgM<\/b> (cold, often harmless); <b>S and s<\/b> antibodies are <b>IgG<\/b> and clinically significant. Show dosage.<\/li>\n<li><b>Lewis (Lea, Leb)<\/b>: <b>IgM<\/b>. Lewis antigens aren't made by red cells; they are made in tissues, float in the plasma, and are <b>adsorbed onto red cells<\/b>. Anti-Lewis <b>doesn't cause HDFN<\/b> (IgM can't cross the placenta, and fetal cells have little Lewis antigen). Lewis antigens may weaken in pregnancy.<\/li>\n<li><b>P1<\/b>: <b>IgM<\/b>, usually insignificant. Anti-P1 can be <b>neutralised<\/b> with hydatid cyst fluid or pigeon egg white.<\/li>\n<li><b>I / i<\/b>: <b>IgM cold autoantibodies<\/b>. Adult cells carry I, and cord cells carry i. <b>Anti-I<\/b> is seen in <b>cold agglutinin disease<\/b> and after <b>Mycoplasma<\/b> pneumonia; <b>anti-i<\/b> after <b>infectious mononucleosis<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: the clinically significant warm antibodies<\/b>The IgG antibodies that cause hemolysis and HDFN: \"<b>Rh, Kell, Duffy, Kidd, Ss<\/b>\". The IgM ones that are usually harmless and cold: \"<b>Lewis, I, P1, M, N<\/b>\" (\"<b>Lie<\/b> <b>P<\/b>atiently in the <b>MN<\/b> cold\").<\/div>\n\n<h3>Dosage<\/h3>\n<p>Some antibodies react more strongly with cells from people who inherited <b>two copies<\/b> of the antigen gene (homozygous) than with cells that have only one copy (heterozygous). A Jk(a+b−) cell has a double dose of Jka and reacts more strongly with anti-Jka than a Jk(a+b+) cell. Dosage is seen in <b>Rh (except D), Kidd, Duffy and MNS<\/b>. That's why antibodies like these should be ruled out only using <b>homozygous<\/b> cells.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Finding compatible blood<\/h3>\n<p>Once a patient has an antibody, you need donors <b>negative for that antigen<\/b>. To estimate how many units to screen, multiply the frequencies of antigen-negative donors.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>The patient has anti-K and anti-Fya. About 91% of donors are K-negative and 34% are Fy(a−). Compatible = 0.91 × 0.34 ≈ <b>0.31 (31%)<\/b>. To find 2 units, expect to screen about 2 ÷ 0.31 ≈ 7 units.<\/div>\n\n<h3>High- and low-prevalence antigens<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>An antibody to a <b>high-prevalence<\/b> antigen (one almost everyone has, like k, Kpb, Jsb, Vel) reacts with <b>nearly every panel cell<\/b>, but the <b>autocontrol is negative<\/b> because the patient lacks the antigen. Compatible blood is rare: test family members or use the rare donor registry.<\/li>\n<li>An antibody to a <b>low-prevalence<\/b> antigen (Kpa, Wra) reacts with only one odd panel cell; nearly every donor unit is compatible.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A donor's cells are negative with anti-D at immediate spin but positive after AHG. How is the unit labeled?<\/summary><p>Rh positive (weak D). It could immunise a D-negative recipient.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which antibodies would be destroyed if the panel cells were enzyme-treated?<\/summary><p>Anti-Fya, anti-Fyb, anti-M, anti-N (and often anti-S). Rh, Kidd and Lewis reactions would be stronger.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why can anti-Jka be missed and then cause a reaction a week after transfusion?<\/summary><p>Kidd antibodies drop below detectable levels quickly, then rise rapidly after re-exposure (anamnestic response), causing a delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>All panel cells react 2+ at AHG, the autocontrol and DAT are negative. Most likely explanation?<\/summary><p>An alloantibody to a high-prevalence antigen.<\/p><\/details>"},"B3":{"min":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Before any red cells are given, the blood bank checks three things: the patient's ABO and Rh type, whether the patient has any unexpected antibodies (the antibody screen), and whether the chosen unit is compatible (the crossmatch). The tool behind most of this is the <b>antiglobulin (Coombs) test<\/b>. Understand how it works and the rest of the lesson falls into place.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Why we need antihuman globulin (AHG)<\/h3>\n<p>An IgM antibody is a big, five-armed molecule that can grab two red cells at once and clump them on its own. An <b>IgG<\/b> antibody is small. It sticks to red cells but can't bridge the gap between them (red cells repel each other with their negative surface charge). So IgG-coated cells look normal in the tube.<\/p>\n<p><b>AHG<\/b> is an antibody made against human antibody. It links one IgG on one cell to an IgG on the next cell, building a bridge that makes visible clumps. So AHG reveals IgG (and complement) that would otherwise be invisible.<\/p>\n\n<h3>DAT and IAT: same reagent, different question<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>DAT (direct antiglobulin test)<\/b>: patient's red cells are washed and AHG is added straight away. It asks: <b>are these cells already coated in the body (in vivo)?<\/b> Positive in <b>HDFN<\/b> (baby's cells coated with mother's antibody), <b>hemolytic transfusion reactions<\/b> (donor cells coated), <b>autoimmune hemolytic anemia<\/b>, and <b>drug-induced hemolysis<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>IAT (indirect antiglobulin test)<\/b>: patient's serum is first incubated with reagent or donor red cells at 37 °C (in vitro), then washed and tested with AHG. It asks: <b>is there antibody in the serum that will coat these cells?<\/b> Used for the <b>antibody screen<\/b>, <b>antibody identification<\/b>, the <b>AHG crossmatch<\/b> and weak D testing.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Polyspecific AHG<\/b> contains <b>anti-IgG<\/b> and <b>anti-C3d<\/b> (complement). Monospecific reagents show which one is present: IgG-only points to warm antibodies; C3d-only points to cold agglutinins and some drug reactions.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Making sure a negative is truly negative: check cells<\/h3>\n<p>After every negative AHG test, add <b>check cells<\/b> (red cells pre-coated with IgG). They should clump, proving the AHG was present and active. If they <b>don't<\/b> clump, the test is <b>invalid<\/b> and must be repeated. The most common reason is <b>poor washing<\/b>: leftover free serum IgG soaks up (neutralises) the AHG before it can react.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: AHG errors<\/b><b>False negatives<\/b>: inadequate washing (AHG neutralised), a delay before reading or adding AHG (antibody falls off the cells), AHG forgotten, under-centrifuging. <b>False positives<\/b>: over-centrifuging, contaminated reagents or glassware, dirty tubes, a positive DAT on the cells being used.<\/div>\n<p><b>Enhancement media<\/b> help IgG antibodies attach faster and more strongly: <b>LISS<\/b> (low ionic strength saline, which reduces the shielding cloud of ions around cells), <b>PEG<\/b> (removes water so cells and antibody meet more often), and <b>albumin<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The antibody screen<\/h3>\n<p>The patient's serum is tested by IAT against <b>2 or 3 group O screening cells<\/b> whose antigens are known. Group O is used so that ABO antibodies don't react. Together, the cells carry all the common, clinically important antigens. If any cell reacts, the patient has an unexpected antibody and needs <b>identification<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Identifying the antibody with a panel<\/h3>\n<p>A panel is a set of about 10-16 group O cells, each with a known antigen profile (written on the antigram sheet). Work through it logically:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Look at the autocontrol<\/b> (patient's serum with their own cells). Negative = <b>alloantibody<\/b> (to an antigen the patient lacks). Positive = <b>autoantibody<\/b> or a recent transfusion.<\/li>\n<li><b>Look at the phase.<\/b> Reacting at immediate spin or room temperature suggests IgM (cold); at AHG suggests IgG (warm, usually significant).<\/li>\n<li><b>Rule out.<\/b> Take each <b>non-reactive<\/b> cell. Any antigen it carries is unlikely to be the target, so cross it off. Use <b>homozygous<\/b> cells for antibodies that show dosage (Rh, Kidd, Duffy, MNS), because a weak antibody may miss a heterozygous cell.<\/li>\n<li><b>Rule in.<\/b> The remaining antibody should fit every reactive cell.<\/li>\n<li><b>Confirm with the rule of three.<\/b> To be 95% confident, have at least <b>3 antigen-positive cells that react<\/b> and <b>3 antigen-negative cells that don't<\/b>. Then confirm the patient's own cells <b>lack<\/b> that antigen.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>An Fy(a+b−) cell is non-reactive at AHG, while an Fy(a−b+) cell reacts. The non-reactive cell is homozygous for Fya, so <b>anti-Fya can be ruled out<\/b>. The reactive Fy(a−b+) cell points toward something else, such as anti-Fyb.<\/div>\n<p><b>Autoantibodies<\/b> complicate things. A <b>warm autoantibody<\/b> (IgG) reacts with every panel cell <i>and<\/i> the autocontrol, and the DAT is positive. It can hide a real alloantibody, so the lab removes the autoantibody by <b>adsorption<\/b> (see below). A <b>cold autoantibody<\/b> (IgM, often anti-I) reacts at room temperature; <b>prewarming<\/b> serum and cells to 37 °C before mixing avoids it.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The crossmatch and pretransfusion rules<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sample age<\/b>: if the patient was <b>transfused or pregnant in the last 3 months<\/b>, the sample must be <b>no more than 3 days old<\/b>, because new antibodies can appear quickly.<\/li>\n<li><b>Immediate-spin crossmatch<\/b>: donor cells and patient serum spun right away. It only checks <b>ABO compatibility<\/b>. Allowed when the <b>antibody screen is negative and there is no history<\/b> of clinically significant antibodies.<\/li>\n<li><b>AHG crossmatch<\/b>: required when the screen is positive or there is an antibody history. Also give <b>antigen-negative<\/b> units.<\/li>\n<li><b>Electronic (computer) crossmatch<\/b>: no tube test, just a validated computer check. Needs a negative screen, no antibody history, and <b>two ABO types on record<\/b> (one from a separate sample).<\/li>\n<li><b>Emergency release<\/b>: if there is no time, give <b>group O red cells<\/b> (D-negative for girls and women of childbearing age), with the physician's signed statement. Testing continues afterward.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>RhIG dose: worked out step by step<\/h3>\n<p>One 300 µg vial covers <b>30 mL of fetal whole blood<\/b> (15 mL of fetal red cells). After delivery, a <b>rosette test<\/b> screens for a large fetomaternal bleed; if positive, the <b>Kleihauer-Betke<\/b> test (or flow cytometry) measures it.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fetal blood (mL) = KB % fetal cells × <b>50<\/b> (the 50 assumes about 5,000 mL of maternal blood volume).<\/li>\n<li>Vials = fetal blood ÷ 30.<\/li>\n<li>Round: decimal <b>0.5 or more → round up<\/b>; <b>below 0.5 → round down<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Add one extra vial<\/b> as a safety margin.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>KB shows 1.2% fetal cells. Fetal blood = 1.2 × 50 = 60 mL. 60 ÷ 30 = 2.0 → 2 vials, + 1 = <b>3 vials<\/b>.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Reading and grading reactions<\/h3>\n<p>Agglutination is graded from <b>4+<\/b> (one solid clump, clear background) through 3+, 2+, 1+, to <b>W+<\/b> (weak, tiny clumps) and <b>0<\/b> (no clumps). <b>Hemolysis<\/b> counts as a positive reaction (the antibody bound complement and lysed the cells). Centrifuging correctly matters: too little lowers sensitivity; too much packs cells so hard they look clumped.<\/p>\n<p><b>Gel (column) and solid-phase<\/b> methods are automated and standardised: agglutinated cells are trapped at the top of a gel column, and unagglutinated cells pass to the bottom.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Drug-induced hemolysis: three mechanisms<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Drug adsorption (penicillin)<\/b>: the drug sticks to the red cell, and antibodies to the drug attack the cell. DAT positive for IgG.<\/li>\n<li><b>Autoantibody (methyldopa)<\/b>: the drug triggers a true warm autoantibody against red cells, which looks just like warm AIHA.<\/li>\n<li><b>Immune complex (quinidine)<\/b>: drug-antibody complexes land on red cells and activate complement, causing intravascular hemolysis. DAT positive for C3d.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Special serologic techniques<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Titration<\/b>: serial two-fold dilutions measure antibody strength. The titer is the reciprocal of the highest dilution that still reacts. In pregnancy, an anti-D titer of <b>16 or more<\/b> is considered critical.<\/li>\n<li><b>Adsorption<\/b>: incubating serum with red cells pulls an antibody out of the serum. Used to remove a <b>warm autoantibody<\/b> (with the patient's own cells or selected donor cells) so hidden alloantibodies can be seen.<\/li>\n<li><b>Elution<\/b>: frees antibody <b>off<\/b> red cells (with acid, heat or chemicals) so it can be identified. Used in <b>HDFN<\/b>, <b>delayed transfusion reactions<\/b> and <b>AIHA<\/b> workups.<\/li>\n<li><b>Neutralisation<\/b>: soluble antigen blocks an antibody, for example Lewis substance (from saliva) for anti-Lewis, hydatid fluid for anti-P1.<\/li>\n<li><b>Lectins<\/b> (plant extracts that act like antibodies): <b>Dolichos biflorus = anti-A1<\/b>; <b>Ulex europaeus = anti-H<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>DTT / 2-ME<\/b>: break IgM (to see if an antibody is IgM or IgG) and destroy Kell antigens. <b>Chloroquine<\/b> or <b>EDTA-glycine acid<\/b> strip IgG off DAT-positive cells so the cells can be antigen typed.<\/li>\n<li><b>Genotyping<\/b> predicts red cell antigens from DNA, useful when the patient was recently transfused or has a strongly positive DAT.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: Wharton's jelly<\/b>Cord blood samples can be contaminated with Wharton's jelly from the umbilical cord, which causes false clumping. Wash the cells at least 6 times and retest.<\/div>\n<p><b>HLA and platelets<\/b>: HLA class I (A, B, C) is on all nucleated cells and platelets. Patients who don't respond to platelet transfusions (low post-transfusion count) often have HLA antibodies; they need <b>HLA-matched or crossmatched platelets<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>An antibody screen is negative, but check cells added afterward don't agglutinate. What do you do?<\/summary><p>The test is invalid, most often from poor washing. Repeat the test.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>When can an immediate-spin crossmatch be used?<\/summary><p>When the antibody screen is negative and the patient has no history of clinically significant antibodies.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>KB shows 0.8% fetal cells. How many RhIG vials?<\/summary><p>0.8 × 50 = 40 mL. 40 ÷ 30 = 1.33 → round down to 1, then add 1 = 2 vials.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which technique would you use to identify an antibody coating a newborn's red cells in HDFN?<\/summary><p>Elution, then test the eluate against panel cells.<\/p><\/details>"},"B4":{"min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A donated unit of whole blood is rarely given as it is. It is spun and split into <b>components<\/b> (red cells, plasma, platelets, cryoprecipitate), and each one is stored differently because each one survives differently. This lesson explains the reasoning behind storage rules, donor rules, quality checks, and when each product is actually used.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Making components from one donation<\/h3>\n<p>About <b>450 mL (± 10%)<\/b> of blood is collected into a bag with about <b>63 mL of anticoagulant-preservative<\/b>. The arm is scrubbed (chlorhexidine or iodine), and the <b>first 30-40 mL is diverted<\/b> into a side pouch, because that first portion carries skin bacteria that the needle may have picked up.<\/p>\n<p>A gentle spin separates <b>red cells<\/b> from <b>platelet-rich plasma<\/b>; a hard spin then separates <b>platelets<\/b> from <b>plasma<\/b>. Plasma frozen quickly becomes <b>fresh frozen plasma (FFP)<\/b>. Thawing FFP slowly in the cold leaves a cold-insoluble precipitate: <b>cryoprecipitate<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Storage: each component has its own rules<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Red cells<\/b>: <b>1-6 °C<\/b>. Cold slows red cell metabolism and bacterial growth. Shelf life depends on the preservative: <b>CPD 21 days<\/b>, <b>CPDA-1 35 days<\/b> (adenine helps cells keep making ATP), and <b>additive solutions such as AS-1 42 days<\/b>. One unit raises an adult's hemoglobin about <b>1 g/dL<\/b> (Hct about 3%).<\/li>\n<li><b>Frozen red cells<\/b>: glycerol protects cells from ice crystals; stored <b>≤ −65 °C for 10 years<\/b>. Used for rare blood types. After thawing and washing out the glycerol, use within <b>24 hours<\/b> (the system is opened).<\/li>\n<li><b>Platelets<\/b>: <b>20-24 °C<\/b> with <b>constant gentle agitation<\/b>, for <b>5 days<\/b> (up to 7 with extra bacterial testing). Platelets stored cold are quickly cleared from the circulation, and agitation keeps gas exchange going (stagnant platelets produce lactic acid and the pH drops). The catch: room temperature also lets bacteria grow, so <b>platelets carry the highest risk of bacterial contamination<\/b>. One random (whole-blood-derived) unit raises the count about 5,000-10,000/µL; one apheresis unit 30,000-60,000/µL.<\/li>\n<li><b>FFP<\/b>: <b>≤ −18 °C for 1 year<\/b>. It contains <b>all the clotting factors<\/b>. Thawed at 30-37 °C; once thawed, keep at 1-6 °C and use within <b>24 hours<\/b> (or relabel as \"thawed plasma\" for up to 5 days; factors V and VIII slowly fall).<\/li>\n<li><b>Cryoprecipitate<\/b>: <b>≤ −18 °C for 1 year<\/b>. Rich in <b>fibrinogen, factor VIII, vWF, factor XIII and fibronectin<\/b>. After thawing, a single unit must be used within <b>6 hours<\/b>; a pool (open system) within <b>4 hours<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Granulocytes<\/b>: 20-24 °C, no agitation, <b>24 hours<\/b> at most. Must be <b>irradiated<\/b> and given as soon as possible.<\/li>\n<li><b>Washed red cells<\/b>: washing removes plasma proteins, but opens the bag, so use within <b>24 hours<\/b>. For patients with severe allergic reactions or <b>IgA deficiency<\/b> with anti-IgA.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>The storage lesion: why old red cells aren't perfect<\/b>As red cells sit in the fridge, they lose <b>2,3-DPG<\/b> (the molecule that helps hemoglobin release oxygen), so the oxygen curve shifts left and old cells hold on to oxygen tightly at first. The cells' sodium-potassium pumps slow down, so <b>potassium leaks out<\/b> into the plasma; that matters for newborns and massive transfusion. pH also falls and some cells hemolyse.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Modifications: leukoreduced and irradiated<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Leukoreduced<\/b> (fewer than <b>5 × 10⁶<\/b> white cells per unit, usually by filter): white cells cause many problems. Removing them prevents <b>febrile non-hemolytic reactions<\/b> (cytokines from white cells), reduces <b>HLA alloimmunisation<\/b> (white cells carry HLA), and lowers <b>CMV transmission<\/b> (CMV lives in white cells).<\/li>\n<li><b>Irradiated<\/b> (at least <b>25 Gy<\/b> to the centre of the bag, 15 Gy minimum anywhere): radiation stops donor lymphocytes from dividing. It is the only reliable way to prevent <b>transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease (TA-GVHD)<\/b>, which is almost always fatal. Needed for fetuses (intrauterine transfusion), newborns receiving exchange transfusion, immunocompromised patients (stem cell transplants, some cancers), <b>blood donated by relatives<\/b>, and <b>HLA-matched<\/b> products.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: leukoreduction vs irradiation<\/b>Leukoreduction does <b>not<\/b> prevent TA-GVHD; too many lymphocytes still remain. Only irradiation does. Irradiation shortens red cell shelf life to <b>28 days<\/b> (or the original expiry, whichever is sooner), because it damages the membrane and raises potassium.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Donor rules: protecting the donor and the patient<\/h3>\n<p>Every donor rule protects either the <b>donor<\/b> (safe to give blood?) or the <b>recipient<\/b> (blood safe to receive?):<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hemoglobin<\/b>: women at least <b>12.5 g/dL<\/b> (Hct 38%), men at least <b>13.0 g/dL<\/b> (Hct 39%). Protects the donor from becoming anemic.<\/li>\n<li><b>Weight ≥ 110 lb<\/b>: 450 mL is safe to remove only from a large enough person.<\/li>\n<li><b>Temperature ≤ 99.5 °F<\/b>, <b>pulse 50-100<\/b>, <b>blood pressure ≤ 180/100<\/b>: signs of infection or poor health.<\/li>\n<li><b>Intervals<\/b>: whole blood every <b>56 days (8 weeks)<\/b>; double red cells every <b>16 weeks<\/b>; platelet apheresis as often as every <b>7 days<\/b>, up to 24 times a year.<\/li>\n<li><b>Autologous<\/b> donors (giving for themselves before surgery) have relaxed rules: Hgb at least <b>11 g/dL<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Required donor tests<\/b>: ABO and Rh, antibody screen, <b>HIV-1/2<\/b> (antibody and NAT), <b>HCV<\/b> (antibody and NAT), <b>HBsAg<\/b>, <b>anti-HBc<\/b> and HBV NAT, <b>HTLV-I/II<\/b>, <b>West Nile virus NAT<\/b>, <b>syphilis<\/b>, and <b>Chagas<\/b> (T. cruzi, once per donor). NAT (nucleic acid testing) finds the virus itself, shortening the \"window period\" before antibodies appear.<\/p>\n<p><b>Deferrals<\/b>: malaria travel (3 months after visiting, 3 years after living in an endemic area), recent pregnancy (6 weeks after delivery), behaviours that raise HIV or hepatitis risk, and many medicines.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Component quality control<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Red cells<\/b>: hematocrit no higher than 80%; leukoreduced units below 5 × 10⁶ WBCs.<\/li>\n<li><b>Random platelets<\/b>: at least <b>5.5 × 10¹⁰<\/b> platelets and <b>pH at least 6.2<\/b> at the end of storage. Low pH means the platelets have been damaged.<\/li>\n<li><b>Apheresis platelets<\/b>: at least <b>3.0 × 10¹¹<\/b> (equal to about 6 random units).<\/li>\n<li><b>Cryoprecipitate<\/b>: at least <b>80 IU factor VIII<\/b> and <b>150 mg fibrinogen<\/b> per unit.<\/li>\n<li><b>Equipment<\/b>: blood bank refrigerators at 1-6 °C with continuous recording and an alarm set to sound before temperatures go out of range; platelet agitators checked.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Plasma derivatives and apheresis<\/h3>\n<p><b>Plasma derivatives<\/b> are made by pooling plasma from thousands of donors and purifying single proteins (fractionation), with steps that kill viruses. Examples: <b>albumin<\/b>, <b>IVIG<\/b>, <b>Rh immune globulin<\/b>, hepatitis B and tetanus immune globulins, and <b>factor VIII and IX concentrates<\/b>.<\/p>\n<p><b>Therapeutic apheresis<\/b> removes something harmful from a patient's blood and returns the rest:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Plasma exchange<\/b>: removes harmful antibodies or proteins. Used for <b>TTP<\/b> (also replaces ADAMTS13), Guillain-Barré, myasthenia gravis and hyperviscosity.<\/li>\n<li><b>Red cell exchange<\/b>: replaces sickle cells in sickle cell crises or stroke.<\/li>\n<li><b>Leukapheresis<\/b>: removes extreme white counts in leukemia. <b>Photopheresis<\/b>: treats white cells with UV light.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Patient blood management<\/b> reduces the need for donor blood: cell salvage during surgery, autologous donation, and treating anemia before surgery.<\/p>\n<p><b>Donor reactions<\/b>: <b>vasovagal<\/b> fainting is the most common (lie the donor down, raise the legs, cool cloth, fluids). Apheresis donors can get a <b>citrate reaction<\/b>, because citrate anticoagulant binds their calcium (tingling lips and fingers); slow the return or give calcium.<\/p>\n\n<h3>When each product is actually needed<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Red cells<\/b>: usually when hemoglobin is <b>below about 7 g/dL<\/b> (below 8 with heart disease or orthopedic surgery), or with symptoms of poor oxygen delivery. Not for volume replacement alone.<\/li>\n<li><b>Platelets<\/b>: to prevent bleeding when the count is <b>below 10,000/µL<\/b>; below <b>50,000<\/b> with active bleeding or before surgery.<\/li>\n<li><b>FFP<\/b>: <b>multiple factor deficiencies<\/b> (liver disease, DIC), urgent <b>warfarin reversal<\/b> with bleeding, and plasma exchange for TTP.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cryoprecipitate<\/b>: <b>fibrinogen below about 100-150 mg/dL<\/b> (DIC, massive bleeding) and factor XIII deficiency.<\/li>\n<li><b>Granulocytes<\/b>: severe neutropenia with infection that isn't responding to antibiotics.<\/li>\n<li><b>Massive bleeding<\/b>: a <b>balanced ratio<\/b> of red cells, plasma and platelets (about <b>1:1:1<\/b>), so clotting factors and platelets aren't diluted out.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Giving blood safely at the bedside<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Only <b>0.9% saline<\/b> in the same line. <b>Dextrose<\/b> makes red cells swell and burst; <b>Ringer's lactate<\/b> contains calcium, which overcomes the citrate and lets the unit clot; <b>drugs<\/b> may hemolyse cells.<\/li>\n<li>Use a standard <b>170-260 micron<\/b> filter.<\/li>\n<li>Start <b>slowly for the first 15 minutes<\/b> and watch the patient closely, because the most severe reactions start early.<\/li>\n<li>Finish each unit within <b>4 hours<\/b> (bacterial growth risk).<\/li>\n<li>Use a <b>blood warmer<\/b> for rapid or massive transfusion and for patients with cold agglutinins.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why are platelets stored at room temperature, and what is the downside?<\/summary><p>Cold-stored platelets are cleared quickly after transfusion. Room temperature keeps them working, but it also allows bacterial growth, so platelets have the highest bacterial contamination risk.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A stem cell transplant patient needs red cells. Which modification is essential?<\/summary><p>Irradiation, to prevent TA-GVHD. (Leukoreduction is also usual, but it doesn't prevent GVHD.)<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A bleeding patient has fibrinogen of 80 mg/dL. Which product?<\/summary><p>Cryoprecipitate, the concentrated source of fibrinogen.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why can't lactated Ringer's be run in the same line as red cells?<\/summary><p>It contains calcium, which overcomes the citrate anticoagulant and can cause the unit to clot.<\/p><\/details>"},"B5":{"min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Transfusion is safe, but reactions still happen, and the lab plays a central role in working them out. This lesson covers the types of transfusion reaction (and how to tell them apart by timing and symptoms), hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn, how Rh immune globulin prevents it, and the autoimmune hemolytic anemias the blood bank investigates.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The first response to any suspected reaction<\/h3>\n<p>Whatever the cause, the first steps are the same, because the most dangerous possibility (an ABO mismatch) gets worse with every extra millilitre:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Stop the transfusion<\/b>, but <b>keep the IV line open with saline<\/b> (the patient may need fluids or drugs fast).<\/li>\n<li><b>Check the identification<\/b>: patient wristband against the unit label and paperwork, at the bedside. Most fatal reactions come from <b>clerical errors<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Notify<\/b> the physician and the blood bank.<\/li>\n<li>Send a <b>post-transfusion blood sample<\/b> (and the bag and tubing) to the blood bank.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The lab then does three quick checks: a <b>clerical check<\/b>, a <b>visual check<\/b> of the post-transfusion plasma for <b>pink/red color<\/b> (free hemoglobin), and a <b>DAT<\/b> on the post-transfusion sample. It repeats ABO and Rh on the pre- and post-transfusion samples and the unit.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The reactions, sorted by timing<\/h3>\n<p><b>Acute (during or within 24 hours):<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Acute hemolytic<\/b>: almost always <b>ABO incompatibility<\/b> from a clerical error. ABO antibodies fix complement and burst the donor cells inside the vessels. Fever, chills, back or flank pain, a feeling of doom, low blood pressure, <b>red plasma and red urine<\/b> (hemoglobinuria), positive DAT, possibly DIC and kidney failure.<\/li>\n<li><b>Febrile non-hemolytic (FNHTR)<\/b>: temperature rise of <b>1 °C or more<\/b>, from <b>cytokines<\/b> released by white cells in the unit (or the patient's antibodies against donor white cells). It is a <b>diagnosis of exclusion<\/b>: you must first rule out hemolysis and bacterial contamination, which also cause fever. Prevented by leukoreduction.<\/li>\n<li><b>Allergic (urticarial)<\/b>: hives and itching, from the patient reacting to proteins in donor plasma. If hives are the only symptom, stop, give an antihistamine, and the transfusion may continue when they settle.<\/li>\n<li><b>Anaphylactic<\/b>: within minutes: wheezing, low blood pressure, <b>no fever<\/b>. Classic cause: an <b>IgA-deficient patient with anti-IgA<\/b> receiving normal plasma containing IgA. Use <b>washed<\/b> red cells or products from IgA-deficient donors.<\/li>\n<li><b>TRALI<\/b> (transfusion-related acute lung injury): within <b>6 hours<\/b>. <b>Donor antibodies<\/b> (anti-HLA or anti-HNA, often from multiparous female donors) attack the recipient's white cells in the lungs, causing leaky lung vessels. <b>Non-cardiogenic<\/b> pulmonary edema: breathing difficulty, often <b>low<\/b> blood pressure and fever, normal heart pressures.<\/li>\n<li><b>TACO<\/b> (transfusion-associated circulatory overload): within 6 hours. Simply <b>too much volume, too fast<\/b> for the heart (elderly, heart failure, small patients). <b>Cardiogenic<\/b> edema with <b>high<\/b> blood pressure and a <b>high BNP<\/b>. Treat with diuretics; prevent by transfusing slowly.<\/li>\n<li><b>Septic<\/b>: bacteria in the unit, usually <b>platelets<\/b> (stored warm). High fever, shock. Gram stain and culture the bag and the patient.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: TRALI vs TACO<\/b>Both cause breathing problems within 6 hours. <b>TACO<\/b> = too much fluid: <b>high<\/b> blood pressure, high BNP, responds to diuretics. <b>TRALI<\/b> = antibody lung injury: often <b>low<\/b> blood pressure, normal BNP, doesn't respond to diuretics.<\/div>\n<p><b>Delayed (days to weeks):<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Delayed hemolytic<\/b>, <b>3-14 days<\/b> later: the patient was sensitised before (pregnancy or transfusion), but the antibody had faded below detection. The new transfusion triggers a rapid <b>memory (anamnestic)<\/b> response. Classic culprits: <b>Kidd<\/b>, Rh, Duffy, Kell. Signs: falling hemoglobin, mild jaundice, a <b>new positive antibody screen<\/b>, and a positive DAT (often mixed-field). Destruction is mostly extravascular.<\/li>\n<li><b>Transfusion-associated GVHD<\/b>, <b>4-30 days<\/b>: donor lymphocytes attack the recipient's tissues (skin rash, diarrhoea, liver damage, and marrow failure with pancytopenia). Almost always fatal. Prevented only by <b>irradiation<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Post-transfusion purpura<\/b>, <b>5-10 days<\/b>: a sudden severe drop in platelets, usually in a woman sensitised by pregnancy who lacks the platelet antigen <b>HPA-1a<\/b>. Her anti-HPA-1a destroys her own platelets too.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn (HDFN)<\/h3>\n<p>HDFN happens when the mother has <b>IgG<\/b> antibodies against an antigen on her baby's red cells (inherited from the father). IgG crosses the placenta, coats the baby's red cells, and the baby's spleen destroys them. The baby becomes anemic and jaundiced, and in severe cases develops heart failure and swelling (hydrops fetalis).<\/p>\n<p><b>ABO HDFN vs Rh HDFN:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>ABO HDFN<\/b>: mother usually <b>group O<\/b> (her anti-A,B is partly IgG), baby group A or B. It can happen in a <b>first pregnancy<\/b>. Usually <b>mild<\/b>, because the A and B antigens are weak on fetal cells and also found on other tissues that soak up the antibody. DAT <b>weak or negative<\/b>; <b>spherocytes<\/b> on the smear. There is no way to prevent it.<\/li>\n<li><b>Rh (anti-D) HDFN<\/b>: D-negative mother sensitised by a previous D-positive baby (or transfusion). The first pregnancy is usually spared because sensitisation mostly happens at delivery. Can be <b>severe<\/b>: strongly <b>positive DAT<\/b>, many <b>nucleated red cells<\/b> (the fetal marrow pours out immature cells). Prevented by <b>RhIG<\/b>. Anti-K is also severe, because it destroys red cell precursors.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Cord blood testing<\/b>: ABO (forward only), Rh and <b>DAT<\/b>. If the mother is D-negative and the baby appears D-negative, test the baby for <b>weak D<\/b>, because the result decides whether she needs RhIG.<\/p>\n<p><b>Exchange transfusion<\/b> removes coated cells and bilirubin. The blood should be <b>group O<\/b>, <b>negative for the antigen<\/b> (D-negative for anti-D), <b>compatible with the mother's serum<\/b> (the antibody comes from her), <b>fresh (under 7 days<\/b>, so potassium is low and 2,3-DPG is high), <b>irradiated<\/b> and <b>CMV-safe<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Rh immune globulin (RhIG): how it works and when to give it<\/h3>\n<p>RhIG is concentrated anti-D. When given to a D-negative mother, it coats any D-positive fetal cells in her circulation, and they are cleared <b>before her immune system notices them<\/b>. So she never makes her own anti-D.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Give at about <b>28 weeks<\/b> of pregnancy and within <b>72 hours<\/b> after delivery of a <b>D-positive<\/b> baby.<\/li>\n<li>Also after events that can mix fetal and maternal blood: <b>miscarriage, abortion, ectopic pregnancy, amniocentesis, abdominal trauma<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Not<\/b> given if the mother has <b>already made her own anti-D<\/b> (it's too late), or if she is D-positive. Practice for mothers with weak D depends on lab policy and RHD genotyping.<\/li>\n<li>After an antenatal dose, a weak anti-D (low titer) in the mother is usually <b>passive RhIG<\/b>, not true sensitisation.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Immune hemolytic anemias<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia (WAIHA)<\/b>: <b>IgG<\/b> autoantibody active at body temperature, usually <b>panreactive<\/b> (reacts with every cell). Spherocytes; <b>DAT positive for IgG<\/b> (with or without C3). Associated with lupus, CLL and drugs. Crossmatching is difficult; <b>adsorption<\/b> is used to look for hidden alloantibodies.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cold agglutinin disease<\/b>: <b>IgM anti-I<\/b> that binds at cold temperatures (fingers, ears) and fixes complement, then falls off as the blood warms, leaving <b>C3 only<\/b> on the cells. Red cells clump in the tube at room temperature (falsely high MCV and MCHC). Follows <b>Mycoplasma<\/b> (anti-I) and <b>infectious mononucleosis<\/b> (anti-i), or lymphoma.<\/li>\n<li><b>Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria (PCH)<\/b>: an <b>IgG anti-P<\/b> \"<b>biphasic<\/b>\" hemolysin. It binds red cells in the cold and then, when warmed, activates complement and bursts them. Usually children after a viral infection. Diagnosed by the <b>Donath-Landsteiner test<\/b> (blood is chilled then warmed; hemolysis appears).<\/li>\n<li><b>Drug-induced<\/b>: penicillin (drug on the cell), methyldopa (true autoantibody), quinidine (immune complexes).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Twenty minutes into a transfusion, the patient has fever, back pain and red urine. Most likely cause, and the first action?<\/summary><p>Acute hemolytic reaction, most likely an ABO error. Stop the transfusion, keep the line open with saline, check identification, notify, and send a post-transfusion sample.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Hives only, normal vital signs, normal clerical check. What happens next?<\/summary><p>Mild allergic reaction: pause, give an antihistamine, and resume if the hives resolve (per policy).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is ABO HDFN usually milder than Rh HDFN?<\/summary><p>A and B antigens are weakly developed on fetal red cells and are also present on other tissues and in fluids, which absorb much of the maternal antibody.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why does cold agglutinin disease give a DAT positive for C3 only?<\/summary><p>The IgM antibody binds in the cold and fixes complement, then detaches as the blood warms, leaving only C3 on the cells.<\/p><\/details>"},"B6":{"min":3,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">This short lesson ties the blood bank to two bigger topics: <b>transplantation<\/b> (where blood groups and HLA decide what is safe) and <b>quality assurance<\/b> (the daily checks and records that keep transfusion safe). Both are tested as practical \"what would you do?\" questions.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Solid organ transplants<\/h3>\n<p>For kidney, heart, liver and other organs, two compatibility layers matter:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>ABO compatibility<\/b>: A and B antigens are on blood vessel walls in organs too, not just on red cells. A recipient's anti-A or anti-B would attack an ABO-incompatible organ's blood vessels within minutes (hyperacute rejection).<\/li>\n<li><b>HLA<\/b>: the recipient may already have antibodies against the donor's HLA (from pregnancy, transfusion or a previous transplant). An <b>HLA crossmatch<\/b> (recipient serum against donor lymphocytes) detects these <b>preformed antibodies<\/b>. A positive crossmatch predicts hyperacute rejection, so that organ isn't used. Matching at <b>HLA-DR<\/b> matters most for kidney survival.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Stem cell (HPC) transplants: the blood type can change<\/h3>\n<p>In a stem cell transplant, the patient's marrow is replaced by the donor's, so the patient's <b>blood group gradually becomes the donor's<\/b>. Because ABO is not used to choose stem cell donors (HLA matters more), ABO mismatches are common and create two kinds of problem:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Major mismatch<\/b>: the <b>recipient has antibodies against the donor's red cells<\/b> (example: recipient group O, donor group A; the recipient's anti-A attacks the new A cells). Risks: hemolysis of red cells in the graft, <b>delayed red cell recovery<\/b> or <b>pure red cell aplasia<\/b> (the antibodies keep destroying new red cell precursors).<\/li>\n<li><b>Minor mismatch<\/b>: the <b>donor's plasma or lymphocytes carry antibodies against the recipient's cells<\/b> (example: recipient A, donor O). Lymphocytes transferred with the graft (\"<b>passenger lymphocytes<\/b>\") make anti-A and can cause sudden hemolysis 1-2 weeks later.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Choosing blood products during the switch<\/b> follows one rule: <b>red cells must be compatible with both the recipient's and the donor's plasma, and plasma must be compatible with both the recipient's and the donor's red cells.<\/b> <b>Group O red cells<\/b> and <b>group AB plasma and platelets<\/b> always satisfy it. All cellular products are <b>irradiated<\/b>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Recipient group A, donor group O. Red cells: must suit the donor's anti-A and anti-B → <b>group O<\/b>. Plasma: must not attack the recipient's A cells → <b>group A or AB<\/b>.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Quality assurance in the blood bank<\/h3>\n<p>Blood bank errors can kill, so quality checks are strict and documented:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Reagent QC on each day of use<\/b>: reagent red cells, antisera (anti-A, anti-B, anti-D) and AHG are tested with <b>positive and negative controls<\/b> every day they are used. A reagent that fails isn't used for patients.<\/li>\n<li><b>Storage equipment<\/b>: refrigerators (1-6 °C), freezers and platelet incubators/agitators are monitored <b>continuously<\/b> with <b>alarms<\/b>, and checked and recorded daily. The alarm must sound <b>before<\/b> products reach an unacceptable temperature. If a unit has been out of range, <b>quarantine<\/b> it, investigate, and document; the medical director decides its fate.<\/li>\n<li><b>Keeping samples<\/b>: the patient's pretransfusion sample and a <b>segment<\/b> from every unit given are kept, refrigerated, for at least <b>7 days<\/b> after transfusion. If a reaction occurs later, they can be retested.<\/li>\n<li><b>Records<\/b>: donor, patient and transfusion records are kept for the required time (often <b>5 years or longer<\/b>; some records much longer), and every unit must be traceable from donor to recipient (\"vein to vein\").<\/li>\n<li><b>Errors and incidents<\/b> are reported, investigated for root cause, and corrected. Serious events may be reported to the FDA.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A group O recipient receives group A stem cells. What kind of mismatch is it, and what risk does it carry?<\/summary><p>A major mismatch. The recipient's anti-A can destroy donor red cells and red cell precursors, causing hemolysis, delayed red cell recovery or pure red cell aplasia.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which red cell and plasma groups are always safe during an ABO-mismatched stem cell transplant?<\/summary><p>Group O red cells and group AB plasma (and platelets).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>How long are patient samples and unit segments kept after transfusion, and why?<\/summary><p>At least 7 days, so they can be retested if a delayed reaction is investigated.<\/p><\/details>"},"B7":{"min":3,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Blood bank traps usually test whether you know an antibody's <b>class<\/b> (IgM or IgG), how it reacts to <b>chemical treatments<\/b>, and whether a test is <b>valid<\/b>. Each listed trap is explained below, followed by more classics.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 1: \"every antibody can cause HDFN\"<\/h3>\n<p>Only <b>IgG<\/b> crosses the placenta, so only IgG antibodies can coat fetal red cells. <b>Lewis and P1<\/b> antibodies are <b>IgM<\/b>: they can't cross. Lewis antigens are also poorly developed on fetal cells. So anti-Lewis and anti-P1 don't cause HDFN. <b>Rh antibodies<\/b> (anti-D, anti-c, anti-E) are IgG and do. So do Kell, Duffy and Kidd.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 2: \"enzymes enhance everything\"<\/h3>\n<p>Ficin and papain strip proteins and sugars from the red cell surface. That <b>enhances<\/b> antigens that were partly hidden (<b>Rh, Kidd<\/b>, Lewis, I, P1) but <b>destroys<\/b> antigens that sit on the stripped-away parts (<b>Duffy<\/b>, M, N, S). So a panel run with enzymes makes anti-Fya disappear while anti-E gets stronger. This is a useful way to split mixtures of antibodies.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 3: \"a negative AHG test is negative\"<\/h3>\n<p>Only if the <b>check cells<\/b> work. IgG-coated check cells are added to every negative AHG tube; if they don't clump, the AHG was neutralised (usually by poor washing) or never added, and the \"negative\" result is <b>invalid<\/b>. Repeat the test.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 4: universal donors<\/h3>\n<p>The two \"universal\" products are opposites, and the exam swaps them on purpose:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Red cells: group O<\/b> is universal (no A or B antigen on the cells).<\/li>\n<li><b>Plasma: group AB<\/b> is universal (no anti-A or anti-B in the plasma).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For emergency red cells, give O <b>D-negative<\/b> to girls and women who may become pregnant; O D-positive is often used for men and older women when O-negative stock is low.<\/p>\n\n<h3>More blood bank traps<\/h3>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Kidd antibodies vanish<\/b>A negative screen doesn't rule out a past anti-Jka. Always check the patient's history; the antibody may be below detection and come back after transfusion.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>DTT and Kell<\/b>DTT destroys Kell antigens. If a panel was DTT-treated, anti-K can't be detected. DTT is used on purpose for patients on daratumumab (anti-CD38).<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Leukoreduction doesn't prevent GVHD<\/b>Only irradiation prevents transfusion-associated GVHD.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Febrile reaction is a diagnosis of exclusion<\/b>A fever during transfusion must be investigated as possible hemolysis or bacterial contamination before calling it a febrile non-hemolytic reaction.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Cord blood reverse typing<\/b>Never reverse-type a newborn. Antibodies present are the mother's.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>RhIG after sensitisation<\/b>RhIG is useless once the mother has made her own anti-D. A low-titer anti-D after an antenatal RhIG dose is usually passive, not sensitisation.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Weak D donors<\/b>Donors with weak D are labeled Rh positive (their cells can immunise D-negative recipients).<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Rouleaux vs agglutination<\/b>Saline replacement disperses rouleaux; true agglutination stays clumped.<\/div>\n\n<h3>How to approach a blood bank trap<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the antibody <b>IgM or IgG<\/b>? That predicts the phase it reacts in, whether it matters clinically, and whether it crosses the placenta.<\/li>\n<li>What do <b>enzymes, DTT, temperature and dosage<\/b> do to it?<\/li>\n<li>Is the test <b>valid<\/b>? Check cells, controls, sample age and identification come first.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A pregnant woman has anti-Lea. Is her baby at risk of HDFN?<\/summary><p>No. Anti-Lewis is IgM (doesn't cross the placenta) and Lewis antigens are poorly expressed on fetal cells.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>An antibody reacts with untreated panel cells but not with ficin-treated cells. Which specificities fit?<\/summary><p>Duffy (anti-Fya, anti-Fyb), anti-M, anti-N (and often anti-S).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which blood group is the universal donor for plasma?<\/summary><p>Group AB.<\/p><\/details>"},"C1":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Clinical chemistry measures substances dissolved in blood and body fluids: sugars, proteins, enzymes, salts, hormones and drugs. Almost every chemistry result comes from a handful of instruments, and most calculation questions come from a handful of formulas. Learn how the instruments \"see\" a substance and you can predict their errors, which is what the exam asks about most.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The 8 big ideas, explained<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Beer-Lambert law: absorbance is proportional to concentration.<\/b> The more colored molecules in the path of light, the more light they absorb. Double the concentration, double the absorbance. That is why one standard of known concentration lets you calculate an unknown.<\/li>\n<li><b>Glucose falls in an unspun tube.<\/b> Red and white cells keep eating glucose (glycolysis) at about 5-7% per hour. A gray-top tube contains <b>sodium fluoride<\/b>, which poisons the enzyme enolase and stops glycolysis. Otherwise, separate the serum within an hour.<\/li>\n<li><b>Diabetes cut-offs:<\/b> fasting glucose ≥126 mg/dL, 2-hour OGTT ≥200, or HbA1c ≥6.5%.<\/li>\n<li><b>Troponin is the most specific marker of heart muscle damage<\/b>, because its cardiac forms (I and T) are found only in heart muscle.<\/li>\n<li><b>Each enzyme points to an organ:<\/b> ALT = liver, AST = liver plus heart and muscle, ALP and GGT = bile ducts, lipase = pancreas.<\/li>\n<li><b>Anion gap = Na − (Cl + HCO3)<\/b>, normally about 8-16. A high gap means an unmeasured acid is present.<\/li>\n<li><b>Bilirubin type tells you where the problem is:<\/b> high direct (conjugated) = the bile can't get out (blockage); high indirect = too much made (hemolysis) or poor processing (Gilbert).<\/li>\n<li><b>High TSH with low free T4 = primary hypothyroidism.<\/b> The thyroid is failing, so the pituitary shouts louder.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Spectrophotometry: the workhorse<\/h3>\n<p>Most chemistry tests turn the substance into something colored, then measure how much light the color absorbs. The path of light is: <b>lamp → monochromator<\/b> (picks one wavelength) <b>→ cuvette<\/b> (holds the sample) <b>→ detector<\/b> (photodetector) <b>→ readout<\/b>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Transmittance (%T)<\/b> is the fraction of light that gets through. <b>Absorbance (A)<\/b> is how much was absorbed. They are linked by <b>A = 2 − log %T<\/b>. So 100% T means A = 0; 10% T means A = 1; 1% T means A = 2.<\/li>\n<li>Beer's law in full is <b>A = a × b × c<\/b> (absorptivity × light path length × concentration). In practice: <b>C<sub>unknown<\/sub> = (A<sub>unknown<\/sub> ÷ A<sub>standard<\/sub>) × C<sub>standard<\/sub><\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>A 100 mg/dL standard reads A = 0.250. A patient reads A = 0.500. Patient = (0.500 ÷ 0.250) × 100 = <b>200 mg/dL<\/b>.<\/div>\n<p><b>Common errors:<\/b> <b>stray light<\/b> (light of the wrong wavelength reaching the detector) makes high absorbances read too low and bends the curve at the top. Dirty or scratched cuvettes, bubbles and fingerprints add false absorbance. Wavelength accuracy is checked with <b>holmium oxide or didymium filters<\/b>, which have sharp known peaks.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Other instruments and what each one is for<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Ion-selective electrodes (ISE)<\/b> measure Na, K and Cl. A membrane lets only one ion through and produces a voltage that depends on that ion's activity. <b>Indirect ISE<\/b> dilutes the sample first and assumes normal water content. If the plasma is full of lipid or protein, there is less water, so sodium reads <b>falsely low (pseudohyponatremia)<\/b>. <b>Direct ISE<\/b> (undiluted, used in blood gas machines) avoids this.<\/li>\n<li><b>Flame emission photometry<\/b> (older method for Na, K, Li): the flame excites atoms and they <i>emit<\/i> light of their own color.<\/li>\n<li><b>Atomic absorption<\/b> (metals like lead, zinc, calcium, magnesium): a hollow-cathode lamp made of the metal being tested shines light that only that metal's atoms <i>absorb<\/i>. Very specific.<\/li>\n<li><b>Fluorometry<\/b> measures light emitted by a molecule after it absorbs light. It is more sensitive than absorbance because it measures light against a dark background.<\/li>\n<li><b>Nephelometry<\/b> measures light <b>scattered<\/b> at an angle by particles such as antigen-antibody complexes (used for immunoglobulins, CRP). <b>Turbidimetry<\/b> measures the light <i>blocked<\/i> straight ahead.<\/li>\n<li><b>Osmometry<\/b> measures osmolality by <b>freezing point depression<\/b>: more dissolved particles = lower freezing point.<\/li>\n<li><b>GC-MS (gas chromatography-mass spectrometry)<\/b> separates compounds and identifies them by their mass \"fingerprint\". It is the <b>gold standard to confirm a positive drug screen<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Electrophoresis<\/b> separates proteins by charge in an electric field at pH 8.6. Serum proteins line up from the anode: <b>albumin, alpha-1, alpha-2, beta, gamma<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: absorb, emit or scatter?<\/b>Spectrophotometry and atomic absorption <b>absorb<\/b>. Flame photometry and fluorometry <b>emit<\/b>. Nephelometry <b>scatters<\/b>. Knowing which one a method uses tells you what can interfere with it.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Standard curves and calibration<\/h3>\n<p>To turn absorbance into concentration, the analyzer runs <b>standards<\/b> (known concentrations) and draws a line of absorbance against concentration. If the line is straight, Beer's law holds and the patient result is read from the line.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>reagent blank<\/b> (reagents without sample) sets zero, so the reagent's own color isn't counted.<\/li>\n<li>The line is only trusted between the lowest and highest standards (the <b>linear or reportable range<\/b>). A result above the top standard must be <b>diluted, rerun, and multiplied by the dilution factor<\/b>. Never extend the line by guessing.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Quick calculations<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Dilution factor<\/b> = total volume ÷ sample volume. 0.5 mL serum + 4.5 mL diluent = 5.0 mL total → 1:10. Multiply the result by 10.<\/li>\n<li><b>Molarity<\/b> (mol/L) = grams per liter ÷ molecular weight.<\/li>\n<li><b>Percent solution (w/v)<\/b> = grams per 100 mL. A 10% NaCl solution has 10 g per 100 mL, so 250 mL needs 25 g.<\/li>\n<li><b>Unit conversions:<\/b> glucose mg/dL ÷ 18 = mmol/L (so 90 mg/dL = 5 mmol/L). Cholesterol mg/dL × 0.0259 = mmol/L.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Glucose is over the linear limit. A 1:4 dilution reads 150 mg/dL. Report 150 × 4 = <b>600 mg/dL<\/b>.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A solution transmits 1% of the light. What is its absorbance?<\/summary><p>A = 2 − log 1 = 2 − 0 = 2.0.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why does a very lipemic sample give a falsely low sodium on indirect ISE but not on direct ISE?<\/summary><p>Indirect ISE dilutes a fixed volume and assumes it is about 93% water. Lipid takes up part of that volume, so less sodium-containing water is sampled. Direct ISE measures sodium activity in the water phase without dilution.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which method confirms a positive urine drug screen?<\/summary><p>Gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (or LC-MS/MS).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Convert 180 mg/dL glucose to mmol/L.<\/summary><p>180 ÷ 18 = 10 mmol/L.<\/p><\/details>"},"C2":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Glucose is the body's main fuel, and its level is held in a tight range by two hormones with opposite jobs: <b>insulin<\/b> lowers it, <b>glucagon<\/b> (plus cortisol, adrenaline and growth hormone) raises it. Diabetes is what happens when insulin is missing or doesn't work. This lesson covers how glucose is measured, how diabetes is diagnosed, and the long-term markers.<\/p>\n\n<h3>How the body controls glucose<\/h3>\n<p>After a meal, glucose rises and the pancreas's beta cells release <b>insulin<\/b>. Insulin acts like a key that lets glucose into muscle and fat cells and tells the liver to store glucose as glycogen. Between meals, glucose falls and <b>glucagon<\/b> tells the liver to release its stored glucose.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Type 1 diabetes<\/b>: the immune system destroys the beta cells, so there is <b>no insulin<\/b>. Usually young, often presents in <b>diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)<\/b>. Autoantibodies (anti-GAD, islet cell, anti-insulin) and a low C-peptide support it.<\/li>\n<li><b>Type 2 diabetes<\/b>: insulin is made but the body <b>resists<\/b> it, and production slowly falls behind. Usually linked to obesity and age.<\/li>\n<li><b>Gestational diabetes<\/b>: insulin resistance caused by pregnancy hormones.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why DKA happens<\/b>With no insulin, cells starve even while glucose is high in the blood. The body burns fat instead, and the liver turns fatty acids into <b>ketone bodies<\/b> (acetoacetate, beta-hydroxybutyrate, acetone). These are acids, so the result is a <b>high anion gap metabolic acidosis<\/b> with high glucose. Potassium often reads high (it shifts out of cells) even though the body's total potassium is low.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Measuring glucose<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hexokinase<\/b> is the <b>reference method<\/b>. Hexokinase converts glucose to glucose-6-phosphate, and a second enzyme (G6PD) uses it to produce NADPH. The rise in absorbance at <b>340 nm<\/b> is proportional to glucose. It is very accurate and has few interferences.<\/li>\n<li><b>Glucose oxidase<\/b> is <b>specific for glucose<\/b> (it ignores other sugars). It produces hydrogen peroxide, which is measured with a color reaction. Reducing substances such as <b>ascorbic acid and uric acid<\/b> can interfere with that peroxide step.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Specimen matters: glucose in an unspun tube falls by about 5-7% per hour because cells keep using it. Use a <b>gray-top (sodium fluoride)<\/b> tube or separate promptly. Whole blood glucose (like a fingerstick) reads about 10-15% lower than plasma glucose.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Diagnosing diabetes and prediabetes<\/h3>\n<p>Any one of these confirms diabetes (repeated to be sure, unless the patient has obvious symptoms):<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fasting plasma glucose <b>≥126 mg/dL<\/b> (fasting = no calories for at least 8 hours).<\/li>\n<li>2-hour glucose <b>≥200 mg/dL<\/b> during a 75 g OGTT.<\/li>\n<li>HbA1c <b>≥6.5%<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Random glucose ≥200 with classic symptoms (thirst, frequent urination, weight loss).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Prediabetes<\/b> is the zone in between: fasting 100-125 (impaired fasting glucose), 2-hour 140-199 (impaired glucose tolerance), or A1c 5.7-6.4%.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: the numbers<\/b>Fasting: <b>100 / 126<\/b>. Two-hour: <b>140 / 200<\/b>. A1c: <b>5.7 / 6.5<\/b>. In each pair, the first number starts prediabetes and the second starts diabetes.<\/div>\n\n<h3>The oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT)<\/h3>\n<p>The patient eats normal amounts of carbohydrate for 3 days (so the body isn't in \"starvation mode\"), fasts 8-12 hours, then drinks <b>75 g of glucose<\/b>. Blood is drawn fasting and at 2 hours. A healthy pancreas brings glucose back down by 2 hours; in diabetes it stays high.<\/p>\n<p><b>Gestational diabetes<\/b> is tested at 24-28 weeks. In the <b>one-step<\/b> 75 g test, any one value at or above <b>fasting 92, 1 hour 180, or 2 hours 153 mg/dL<\/b> is positive. The <b>two-step<\/b> method starts with a 50 g non-fasting 1-hour screen, and only abnormal screens get a full 100 g test.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Long-term markers: HbA1c and fructosamine<\/h3>\n<p>Glucose sticks to proteins slowly and permanently (glycation). The more glucose around, the more sticks.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>HbA1c<\/b> is glucose attached to hemoglobin. Red cells live about 120 days, so A1c reflects the average glucose over the last <b>2-3 months<\/b>. Estimated average glucose (eAG) = 28.7 × A1c − 46.7; an A1c of 7% ≈ 154 mg/dL.<\/li>\n<li><b>Fructosamine<\/b> is glucose attached to serum proteins (mostly albumin, which lasts about 20 days), so it reflects the last <b>2-3 weeks<\/b>. It is useful in pregnancy or when A1c is unreliable.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: when A1c lies<\/b>A1c depends on red cell lifespan. If red cells die early (<b>hemolytic anemia, bleeding<\/b>), they have less time to collect glucose, so A1c reads <b>falsely low<\/b>. Some hemoglobin variants (HbS, HbC) can interfere with certain methods. Use fructosamine in those patients.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Hypoglycemia: insulin and C-peptide<\/h3>\n<p>The pancreas makes insulin as one long molecule, <b>proinsulin<\/b>, and cuts it into insulin plus a spare piece called <b>C-peptide<\/b>, in equal amounts. Injected insulin has no C-peptide. That gives a neat way to find the cause of low glucose:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>High insulin + high C-peptide<\/b> → the pancreas is overproducing: <b>insulinoma<\/b> or a <b>sulfonylurea<\/b> drug (which squeezes the pancreas).<\/li>\n<li><b>High insulin + low C-peptide<\/b> → insulin was <b>injected<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Ketones and kidney damage<\/h3>\n<p>The urine dipstick ketone pad uses <b>nitroprusside<\/b>, which reacts with <b>acetoacetate<\/b> (and acetone, weakly) but <b>not with beta-hydroxybutyrate<\/b>. In severe DKA, most ketone is beta-hydroxybutyrate, so the dipstick can underestimate how sick the patient is. A specific blood beta-hydroxybutyrate test fixes this.<\/p>\n<p>High glucose damages the kidney's filters over years. The earliest sign is a little albumin leaking into the urine: a urine <b>albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 30-300 mg/g<\/b> (microalbuminuria). Standard dipsticks are not sensitive enough to detect it.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Fasting glucose 118, 2-hour OGTT 165. Category?<\/summary><p>Prediabetes: impaired fasting glucose (100-125) and impaired glucose tolerance (140-199).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient with sickle cell anemia has an A1c of 5.0% but home glucose readings of 200 mg/dL. Why?<\/summary><p>Shortened red cell survival (and possibly hemoglobin variant interference) makes A1c falsely low. Use fructosamine.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Glucose 35 mg/dL, insulin high, C-peptide low. Cause?<\/summary><p>Injected (exogenous) insulin, because pancreatic insulin always comes with C-peptide.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why can a DKA patient have only \"small\" ketones on the dipstick?<\/summary><p>Most of the ketone in DKA is beta-hydroxybutyrate, which nitroprusside doesn't detect.<\/p><\/details>"},"C3":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Proteins do most of the work in the blood: they carry hormones and drugs, hold water in the vessels, fight infection and clot blood. When the body breaks proteins down, the nitrogen ends up as waste (urea, creatinine, ammonia, uric acid) that the liver and kidney have to deal with. So the tests in this lesson tell you about nutrition, the liver, the immune system and the kidney all at once.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Total protein and albumin<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Total protein<\/b> is measured by the <b>biuret method<\/b>: copper ions bind to peptide bonds in alkaline solution and turn violet. Every protein has peptide bonds, so this measures all of them.<\/li>\n<li><b>Albumin<\/b> is measured by <b>dye binding<\/b> with <b>bromcresol green (BCG)<\/b> or <b>bromcresol purple (BCP)<\/b>. Albumin grabs the dye and changes its color. BCP is more specific for albumin; BCG can also react a little with globulins.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Albumin<\/b> is the most abundant plasma protein. It is made only by the liver and it holds water in the bloodstream (oncotic pressure). Low albumin points to liver disease, malnutrition, or loss through the kidney (nephrotic syndrome) or gut. When albumin is low, fluid leaks into tissues: <b>edema<\/b>.<\/p>\n<p><b>Globulins<\/b> = total protein − albumin. The A/G ratio is normally above 1.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP): reading the bands<\/h3>\n<p>At pH 8.6, proteins separate by charge into five bands, from the anode: <b>albumin, alpha-1, alpha-2, beta, gamma<\/b>. Each band contains known proteins, so each pattern tells a story:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Alpha-1<\/b>: mainly alpha-1 antitrypsin. A <b>flat alpha-1 band<\/b> suggests <b>alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency<\/b> (early emphysema and liver disease).<\/li>\n<li><b>Alpha-2<\/b>: alpha-2 macroglobulin and haptoglobin. Rises in inflammation and, notably, in <b>nephrotic syndrome<\/b>. Alpha-2 macroglobulin is too big to leak through the kidney, so it stays behind while smaller proteins are lost.<\/li>\n<li><b>Beta<\/b>: transferrin, complement C3, beta-lipoprotein.<\/li>\n<li><b>Gamma<\/b>: the immunoglobulins (antibodies).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Classic patterns:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sharp narrow spike in gamma (or beta)<\/b> → <b>monoclonal gammopathy<\/b> such as multiple myeloma. One clone of plasma cells makes one identical antibody, so it all runs to one spot. Confirm and type it with <b>immunofixation<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Broad gamma increase<\/b> (polyclonal) → chronic inflammation or infection: many clones, many antibodies.<\/li>\n<li><b>Beta-gamma bridging<\/b> (no dip between beta and gamma) → <b>cirrhosis<\/b>, from a polyclonal rise in IgA.<\/li>\n<li><b>Low albumin, high alpha-2, low gamma<\/b> → <b>nephrotic syndrome<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Raised alpha-1 and alpha-2<\/b> → acute inflammation (acute-phase proteins).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Urea (BUN) and creatinine: the kidney pair<\/h3>\n<p><b>Urea<\/b> is made in the liver from the ammonia released when protein is broken down. <b>Creatinine<\/b> comes from the steady breakdown of creatine in muscle. Both are filtered by the kidney, so both rise when filtration falls.<\/p>\n<p><b>Creatinine<\/b> is the better kidney test because muscle produces it at a steady rate and diet barely affects it. <b>BUN<\/b> also changes with protein intake, gut bleeding (digested blood is protein), dehydration and liver function.<\/p>\n<p>The <b>BUN/creatinine ratio<\/b> (normally about 10-20) tells you <i>where<\/i> the problem is:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Above 20 → prerenal.<\/b> Not enough blood reaching the kidney (dehydration, heart failure, bleeding). The kidney itself is fine and reabsorbs more urea along with water, so BUN climbs faster than creatinine. A GI bleed also raises it.<\/li>\n<li><b>Normal or low → intrinsic kidney damage<\/b> such as acute tubular necrosis, because damaged tubules can't reabsorb urea.<\/li>\n<li><b>Postrenal<\/b> (blockage after the kidney, like an enlarged prostate) often starts with a high ratio.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>BUN 60 mg/dL, creatinine 1.1 mg/dL. Ratio ≈ 55, far above 20, so think <b>prerenal<\/b>: check for dehydration or a GI bleed.<\/div>\n<p><b>Measuring creatinine.<\/b> The <b>Jaffe method<\/b> reacts creatinine with alkaline picric acid to form a red-orange color. Other substances also react (\"non-creatinine chromogens\"), notably <b>glucose, ketones (acetoacetate), protein and some cephalosporins<\/b>, so results can read falsely high. <b>Enzymatic methods<\/b> avoid this. The kidney section later covers clearance and eGFR.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Ammonia<\/h3>\n<p>Ammonia is toxic to the brain. The liver normally turns it into urea. When the liver fails (cirrhosis, hepatitis) or in <b>Reye syndrome<\/b> (children given aspirin during a viral illness), ammonia builds up and causes confusion and coma (hepatic encephalopathy).<\/p>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: ammonia handling<\/b>Ammonia keeps rising in the tube as red cells and proteins break down. Collect in EDTA or heparin, <b>put the tube on ice<\/b>, and analyze within <b>15-30 minutes<\/b>. Avoid hemolysis, and the patient shouldn't smoke beforehand. A delayed, warm sample reads falsely high.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Uric acid<\/h3>\n<p>Uric acid is the end product of breaking down purines (from DNA and RNA). High levels form needle-shaped crystals in joints: <b>gout<\/b>. Levels also rise when many cells die at once (chemotherapy for leukemia: tumor lysis syndrome) and in kidney failure. It is measured with <b>uricase<\/b>, an enzyme that breaks down uric acid; the drop in absorbance at 293 nm, or a linked color reaction, gives the result.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is creatinine a better marker of kidney function than BUN?<\/summary><p>Creatinine is produced at a steady rate from muscle and isn't much affected by diet, hydration or liver function. BUN changes with all of those.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>SPEP shows a narrow spike in the gamma region. What is the next test?<\/summary><p>Immunofixation electrophoresis, to confirm it is monoclonal and identify the heavy and light chain (for example IgG kappa).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>An ammonia sample sat at room temperature for 2 hours. What happens to the result?<\/summary><p>It is falsely high, because ammonia is generated in the tube. Recollect on ice and run promptly.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A diabetic in DKA has a creatinine that seems too high for their kidney function. Why might that be?<\/summary><p>With the Jaffe method, acetoacetate (and very high glucose) reacts as a non-creatinine chromogen, falsely raising creatinine. An enzymatic method avoids it.<\/p><\/details>"},"C4":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Enzymes are proteins that live inside cells. When cells are damaged, enzymes leak into the blood, and because different organs contain different enzymes, the pattern of what leaks tells you <i>which<\/i> organ is hurt. Cardiac markers work the same way, with one more element: <i>timing<\/i>, because each marker rises and falls on its own schedule after a heart attack.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Cardiac markers: think about timing<\/h3>\n<p>After a heart attack (myocardial infarction, MI), heart cells die and release their contents. Small molecules escape first; bigger or more tightly bound ones take longer and linger.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Myoglobin<\/b>: a small oxygen-holding protein. It rises <b>first (1-3 h)<\/b>, peaks at 6-9 h and is gone within about <b>24 h<\/b>. But skeletal muscle has it too, so it is <b>not specific<\/b>. Its main use is that a normal myoglobin a few hours after pain makes MI unlikely.<\/li>\n<li><b>CK-MB<\/b>: the heart form of creatine kinase. Rises at 3-6 h, peaks 12-24 h, normal again in <b>2-3 days<\/b>. Because it clears quickly, it is useful for spotting a <b>second (re-)infarction<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Troponin I or T<\/b>: part of the heart's contraction machinery. The cardiac forms exist only in heart muscle, so troponin is the <b>most specific<\/b> and the <b>preferred<\/b> marker. Rises at 3-4 h (sooner with high-sensitivity assays), peaks 12-24 h, and stays high for <b>7-10 days or more<\/b>. That makes it the best marker for a patient who arrives days after chest pain.<\/li>\n<li><b>BNP / NT-proBNP<\/b>: not a damage marker. The stretched ventricle releases it, so it helps diagnose <b>heart failure<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: first in, first out<\/b>Myoglobin is first in and first out (1 day). CK-MB stays a couple of days. Troponin arrives a little later but stays over a week, so it is the \"<b>T<\/b>he long <b>T<\/b>imer\".<\/div>\n\n<h3>Liver enzymes: two kinds of injury<\/h3>\n<p>Liver tests split into two groups, and which group is high tells you the type of problem:<\/p>\n<p><b>Hepatocellular (liver cell) injury → AST and ALT.<\/b> These enzymes are inside liver cells, so they spill out when cells are inflamed or die (viral hepatitis, drugs, toxins).<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>ALT<\/b> is mostly in the liver, so it is <b>more liver-specific<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>AST<\/b> is also in heart, muscle and red cells.<\/li>\n<li>In viral hepatitis, ALT is usually ≥ AST. An <b>AST:ALT ratio above 2<\/b>, especially with high GGT, suggests <b>alcoholic liver disease<\/b> (alcohol damages mitochondria, where much of the AST lives, and lowers the vitamin B6 that ALT needs).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Cholestatic (bile flow) injury → ALP and GGT.<\/b> These enzymes line the bile ducts and rise when bile can't flow (gallstones, tumors, drug cholestasis).<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>ALP<\/b> is also made by <b>bone-building cells<\/b> (osteoblasts), so it rises in bone disease, growing children and pregnancy (placental ALP).<\/li>\n<li>To tell whether a high ALP comes from liver or bone, check <b>GGT or 5'-nucleotidase<\/b>: high with ALP → <b>liver<\/b>; normal → <b>bone<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>GGT<\/b> also rises with <b>alcohol<\/b> use and some drugs.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Pancreatic enzymes<\/h3>\n<p>In <b>acute pancreatitis<\/b>, digestive enzymes leak into the blood. <b>Amylase<\/b> rises within hours but falls back in 2-3 days, and the salivary glands also make it (mumps, salivary disease). <b>Lipase<\/b> is <b>more specific<\/b> for the pancreas and stays high longer (up to 1-2 weeks), so it is the preferred test.<\/p>\n\n<h3>CK and its three isoenzymes<\/h3>\n<p>Creatine kinase is made of two subunits, M (muscle) and B (brain). The three combinations point to different tissues:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>CK-MM<\/b>: skeletal muscle (most of the CK in blood). Very high after crush injury, rhabdomyolysis, intense exercise or IM injections.<\/li>\n<li><b>CK-MB<\/b>: heart.<\/li>\n<li><b>CK-BB<\/b>: brain, rarely seen in blood.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>LD and the \"flipped\" pattern<\/h3>\n<p>Lactate dehydrogenase is in nearly every cell, so total LD is not specific. Its five isoenzymes help. Normally <b>LD2 is higher than LD1<\/b>. Heart muscle and red cells are rich in LD1, so in <b>MI, hemolysis and megaloblastic anemia<\/b> LD1 rises above LD2: the \"<b>flipped<\/b>\" pattern. LD is also high in many cancers (cell turnover).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Other enzymes to recognise<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Acid phosphatase<\/b>: once used for prostate cancer, now replaced by PSA. Still used in forensic testing for semen.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cholinesterase (pseudocholinesterase)<\/b>: <b>low in organophosphate insecticide poisoning<\/b>; also tested before using the anesthetic succinylcholine, because low levels cause prolonged paralysis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>How enzyme activity is measured<\/h3>\n<p>Enzymes are measured by what they <i>do<\/i>, not by how much protein there is. Many reactions are linked to the coenzyme <b>NAD/NADH<\/b>. NADH absorbs light strongly at <b>340 nm<\/b>; NAD does not. So the analyzer watches absorbance at 340 nm rise or fall over time. The <b>rate<\/b> of change gives the enzyme activity (a kinetic assay).<\/p>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why temperature and timing matter for enzymes<\/b>Enzymes are sensitive to temperature, pH and substrate. Reactions must be read while the rate is steady (the linear phase), with plenty of substrate. If the enzyme activity is extremely high, the substrate gets used up too fast (\"substrate depletion\") and the result reads falsely low, so dilute and rerun.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient comes in 6 days after chest pain. Which marker is most likely still raised?<\/summary><p>Troponin. It stays up 7-10 days or more; CK-MB and myoglobin are back to normal.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>ALP is high but GGT is normal. Where is the ALP coming from?<\/summary><p>Most likely bone (or placenta in pregnancy). A liver source would raise GGT too.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is lipase preferred over amylase for pancreatitis?<\/summary><p>It is more specific to the pancreas and stays raised longer.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>AST 220, ALT 90, GGT high. Most likely cause?<\/summary><p>Alcoholic liver disease (AST:ALT above 2 with raised GGT).<\/p><\/details>"},"C5":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Electrolytes are the charged salts dissolved in body water. They control fluid balance, nerve signals and heart rhythm, and they are tightly linked to acid-base balance. Blood gas questions look intimidating, but there is a simple step-by-step method that solves almost all of them, and this lesson teaches it.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The main electrolytes and what each does<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sodium (135-145 mmol/L)<\/b>: the main salt outside cells. It mostly reflects <b>water balance<\/b>: low sodium usually means too much water, high sodium means too little.<\/li>\n<li><b>Potassium (3.5-5.1)<\/b>: the main salt <i>inside<\/i> cells. Small changes in blood potassium affect the heart. High K gives <b>peaked T waves<\/b> and dangerous arrhythmias; low K causes weakness and arrhythmias too.<\/li>\n<li><b>Chloride (98-107)<\/b>: follows sodium and moves opposite to bicarbonate.<\/li>\n<li><b>Bicarbonate (22-29)<\/b>: the main base buffer (it is the \"total CO2\" on a chemistry panel).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why potassium is so easily falsely high<\/b>Cells hold about 30 times more potassium than plasma. Anything that lets potassium leak out of cells in the tube makes the result read high: <b>hemolysis<\/b>, <b>delayed separation<\/b> (cells leak over time, especially if refrigerated), <b>fist clenching<\/b> during the draw, and <b>very high platelet or white counts<\/b> (they release K as the sample clots). Contamination with K2EDTA from a purple tube is another classic cause (it also makes calcium very low).<\/div>\n\n<h3>Anion gap: finding hidden acids<\/h3>\n<p>Blood is electrically neutral: positive charges equal negative charges. We measure the main cation (Na) and the main anions (Cl and HCO3), but not every anion. The difference is the <b>anion gap<\/b>:<\/p>\n<p><b>AG = Na − (Cl + HCO3)<\/b>, normally about 8-16 (some labs use 10-20 if K is added).<\/p>\n<p>If an extra acid enters the blood, it uses up bicarbonate and leaves its own unmeasured anion behind, so the gap rises. Causes of a <b>high anion gap metabolic acidosis<\/b> are remembered as <b>MUDPILES<\/b>: <b>M<\/b>ethanol, <b>U<\/b>remia, <b>D<\/b>KA, <b>P<\/b>araldehyde, <b>I<\/b>ron/<b>I<\/b>soniazid, <b>L<\/b>actic acidosis, <b>E<\/b>thylene glycol, <b>S<\/b>alicylates.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Na 140, Cl 100, HCO3 10. AG = 140 − 110 = <b>30<\/b>. High gap: look for lactate, ketones, kidney failure or a toxin.<\/div>\n<p>A <b>low<\/b> anion gap is unusual. Think lab error, low albumin (albumin is a big unmeasured anion), or a positively charged paraprotein (IgG myeloma).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Osmolality and the osmolal gap<\/h3>\n<p>Osmolality counts all dissolved particles. We can <b>calculate<\/b> it from the main ones:<\/p>\n<p><b>Calculated osmolality = 2 × Na + glucose ÷ 18 + BUN ÷ 2.8<\/b> (about 285-295 mOsm/kg).<\/p>\n<p>Sodium is doubled to count its partner anions. If the <b>measured<\/b> osmolality (by freezing point) is much higher than the calculated one, something unmeasured is adding particles. A <b>gap above about 10<\/b> suggests <b>toxic alcohols<\/b>: methanol, ethylene glycol, isopropanol, or ethanol itself.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Calcium and magnesium<\/h3>\n<p>About half of blood calcium is bound to albumin, and half is free (<b>ionized<\/b>). Only the ionized part is active. That's why low albumin lowers total calcium without causing symptoms, and why ionized calcium is the better test in very sick patients. Ionized calcium also rises in acidosis (H+ pushes Ca off albumin).<\/p>\n<p><b>PTH<\/b> raises calcium; the details are in the endocrine lesson. <b>Magnesium<\/b> is needed for PTH release and potassium handling, so low magnesium causes low calcium and low potassium that won't correct until magnesium is replaced, plus arrhythmias.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Acid-base basics<\/h3>\n<p>Normal arterial blood: <b>pH 7.35-7.45<\/b>, <b>pCO2 35-45 mmHg<\/b>, <b>HCO3 22-26 mmol/L<\/b>.<\/p>\n<p>pH is a balance between an acid controlled by the <b>lungs<\/b> (CO2, which forms carbonic acid) and a base controlled by the <b>kidneys<\/b> (HCO3). The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation shows the balance:<\/p>\n<p><b>pH = 6.1 + log [HCO3 ÷ (0.03 × pCO2)]<\/b>. The ratio of HCO3 to dissolved CO2 is normally <b>20:1<\/b>; as long as that ratio holds, pH stays normal.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>respiratory<\/b> problem changes <b>pCO2<\/b>. Slow breathing (lung disease, sedatives) retains CO2 → acidosis. Fast breathing (anxiety, aspirin early on, altitude) blows off CO2 → alkalosis.<\/li>\n<li>A <b>metabolic<\/b> problem changes <b>HCO3<\/b>. Gaining acid or losing base (DKA, diarrhoea, kidney failure) → acidosis. Losing acid or gaining base (vomiting, diuretics) → alkalosis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>The 3-step method for any blood gas<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Look at pH.<\/b> Below 7.35 = acidosis; above 7.45 = alkalosis.<\/li>\n<li><b>Find the culprit.<\/b> Which value explains the pH? If pH is low and pCO2 is high, it is respiratory acidosis. If pH is low and HCO3 is low, it is metabolic acidosis. (pCO2 acts like an acid; HCO3 like a base.)<\/li>\n<li><b>Check compensation.<\/b> Is the other value moving to fix it? The body tries to restore the 20:1 ratio, so <b>the compensating value moves in the same direction as the primary change<\/b>. If pH is back in the normal range, the disorder is \"fully compensated\".<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked examples<\/b>pH 7.25, pCO2 30, HCO3 12 → low pH, low HCO3 = <b>metabolic acidosis<\/b>; pCO2 is also low (same direction) = <b>respiratory compensation<\/b>.<br>pH 7.52, pCO2 28, HCO3 22 → high pH, low pCO2 = <b>respiratory alkalosis<\/b>; HCO3 is still normal = <b>acute, uncompensated<\/b>.<br>pH 7.50, pCO2 48, HCO3 36 after vomiting → high pH, high HCO3 = <b>metabolic alkalosis<\/b> with respiratory compensation.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: ROME<\/b><b>R<\/b>espiratory <b>O<\/b>pposite: pH and pCO2 move in opposite directions. <b>M<\/b>etabolic <b>E<\/b>qual: pH and HCO3 move in the same direction.<\/div>\n<p>Lungs compensate within minutes; kidneys take <b>days<\/b>. That is why acute respiratory problems usually show little metabolic compensation.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Blood gas specimen rules<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <b>heparin<\/b> (a dry heparin syringe), mix well, and keep it <b>air-tight<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Air bubbles<\/b> let gases equalise with room air (pO2 about 150, pCO2 near 0). So bubbles <b>raise pO2 and lower pCO2<\/b> (and raise pH).<\/li>\n<li>Analyze quickly. If delayed, ice the sample, because living cells keep consuming O2 and producing CO2 and acid.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>pH 7.30, pCO2 60, HCO3 29. Interpretation?<\/summary><p>Respiratory acidosis (low pH, high pCO2) with some metabolic compensation (HCO3 mildly raised, same direction as pCO2).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Measured osmolality 330, calculated 290. What does this suggest?<\/summary><p>An osmolal gap of 40, pointing to an unmeasured osmole such as ethylene glycol or methanol.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Potassium 6.8 on a grossly hemolyzed sample from a well patient with a normal ECG. Action?<\/summary><p>Don't report it as valid. Recollect a properly drawn sample, because hemolysis releases red cell potassium.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>An arterial sample arrives with a large air bubble. How are the results affected?<\/summary><p>pO2 falsely high, pCO2 falsely low, pH falsely high. Reject and recollect.<\/p><\/details>"},"C6":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">This lesson joins three topics that share the liver: <b>lipids<\/b> (fats that travel in protein \"boats\"), <b>bilirubin<\/b> (the yellow waste from old red cells that the liver processes), and <b>heme synthesis<\/b> (how the body builds the iron-carrying part of hemoglobin, and what goes wrong in porphyria and lead poisoning).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Why fats need lipoproteins<\/h3>\n<p>Fat and water don't mix, so cholesterol and triglycerides can't float freely in blood. They travel inside <b>lipoproteins<\/b>: balls with a fatty core and a coat of protein (<b>apolipoproteins<\/b>) that acts as an address label. Each lipoprotein has a different job:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Chylomicrons<\/b> carry <b>dietary<\/b> fat from the gut. They are the biggest and lightest, so they float. Label: <b>apo B-48<\/b> (plus C-II, which switches on the enzyme that unloads them).<\/li>\n<li><b>VLDL<\/b> carries triglyceride <b>made by the liver<\/b> out to the body. Label: <b>apo B-100<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>LDL<\/b> is what is left of VLDL after the triglyceride is unloaded. It carries mostly <b>cholesterol to the tissues<\/b>, including artery walls: the \"<b>bad<\/b>\" cholesterol. Label: <b>apo B-100<\/b>, which docks on the LDL receptor.<\/li>\n<li><b>HDL<\/b> collects extra cholesterol from tissues and brings it <b>back to the liver<\/b>: the \"<b>good<\/b>\", protective cholesterol. Label: <b>apo A-I<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Lp(a)<\/b> is LDL with an extra apo(a) protein that looks like plasminogen. It promotes clotting and raises heart risk, and it is mostly genetic.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: LDL and HDL<\/b><b>L<\/b>DL = <b>L<\/b>ousy (delivers cholesterol into arteries). <b>H<\/b>DL = <b>H<\/b>ealthy (takes it home to the liver).<\/div>\n\n<h3>Reading a lipid profile<\/h3>\n<p>Desirable values: total cholesterol <b>below 200<\/b>, LDL <b>below 100<\/b>, HDL <b>above 40<\/b> (men) or <b>50<\/b> (women), triglycerides <b>below 150 mg/dL<\/b>.<\/p>\n<p>LDL is often <b>calculated<\/b> with the <b>Friedewald equation<\/b>: <b>LDL = total cholesterol − HDL − (TG ÷ 5)<\/b>. The TG ÷ 5 part estimates the cholesterol carried by VLDL (VLDL has about 5 times more triglyceride than cholesterol).<\/p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>TC 240, HDL 40, TG 150. LDL = 240 − 40 − 30 = <b>170 mg/dL<\/b>.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: when Friedewald fails<\/b>If triglycerides are <b>above 400 mg/dL<\/b>, or chylomicrons are present (non-fasting sample), the TG ÷ 5 assumption breaks down. Measure LDL directly instead.<\/div>\n<p><b>Fasting:<\/b> triglycerides need a <b>9-12 hour<\/b> fast, because a meal floods the blood with chylomicrons.<\/p>\n<p><b>The refrigerator test.<\/b> Leave plasma in the fridge overnight. A <b>creamy layer on top<\/b> means <b>chylomicrons<\/b> (they are light and float). <b>Uniform cloudiness<\/b> throughout means <b>VLDL<\/b>. Both can happen together.<\/p>\n<p><b>Lipoprotein electrophoresis<\/b>, from the origin: chylomicrons (stay at the start) → LDL (beta) → VLDL (pre-beta) → HDL (alpha, farthest).<\/p>\n<p><b>Two inherited disorders:<\/b> <b>familial hypercholesterolemia<\/b> (faulty LDL receptor, so LDL can't be cleared and stays very high, giving early heart attacks) and <b>lipoprotein lipase or apo C-II deficiency<\/b> (chylomicrons can't be unloaded, so triglycerides are extremely high, plasma is milky, and pancreatitis is a risk).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Bilirubin: following the yellow pigment<\/h3>\n<p>Picture bilirubin's journey, and jaundice becomes easy:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Old red cells are broken down by macrophages. Heme becomes <b>unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin<\/b>. It doesn't dissolve in water, so it rides on albumin and <b>can't pass into urine<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>The liver takes it up and attaches glucuronic acid (using the enzyme UDP-glucuronyl transferase). Now it is <b>conjugated (direct) bilirubin<\/b>, which <b>is water-soluble<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Conjugated bilirubin goes out in bile to the gut, where bacteria turn it into <b>urobilinogen<\/b>. Most leaves in stool (as stercobilin, which makes stool brown); some is reabsorbed and a little appears in urine.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Now each type of jaundice follows from where the journey is blocked:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Prehepatic (hemolysis):<\/b> too much bilirubin made. <b>Indirect<\/b> bilirubin rises. Urine bilirubin is <b>negative<\/b> (indirect can't enter urine), but <b>urobilinogen is high<\/b> (more pigment reaches the gut). Retics and LD are high.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hepatic (hepatitis, cirrhosis):<\/b> sick liver cells struggle at every step, so <b>both<\/b> types rise. Urine bilirubin is positive; ALT and AST are high.<\/li>\n<li><b>Posthepatic (blockage, such as gallstones or a pancreatic tumor):<\/b> bile can't get out. <b>Direct<\/b> bilirubin backs up into the blood and spills into urine (<b>dark urine, positive bilirubin<\/b>). None reaches the gut, so urobilinogen is <b>low or absent<\/b> and stools are <b>pale<\/b>. ALP and GGT are high.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Inherited causes:<\/b> <b>Gilbert<\/b> (mild, common, slightly low conjugating enzyme, indirect rise during fasting or illness) and <b>Crigler-Najjar<\/b> (enzyme severely missing) raise <b>indirect<\/b> bilirubin. <b>Dubin-Johnson<\/b> and <b>Rotor<\/b> (problems moving conjugated bilirubin out of the cell) raise <b>direct<\/b> bilirubin.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: light<\/b>Bilirubin breaks down in light. Protect specimens (especially newborn samples) from light, or results will be falsely low.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Heme synthesis and the porphyrias<\/h3>\n<p>Heme is built in steps, a bit like an assembly line:<\/p>\n<p><b>Glycine + succinyl CoA → ALA<\/b> (by <b>ALA synthase<\/b>, the rate-limiting step, which needs vitamin <b>B6<\/b>) <b>→ PBG<\/b> (porphobilinogen, by ALA dehydratase) <b>→ porphyrins<\/b> (uro-, copro-, protoporphyrin) <b>→ heme<\/b> (iron is added by <b>ferrochelatase<\/b>).<\/p>\n<p>A blocked step causes the substances <i>before<\/i> it to pile up. That pile-up is what you measure:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP)<\/b>: PBG deaminase is low, so <b>ALA and PBG<\/b> build up. These early compounds are toxic to nerves, so attacks bring severe <b>abdominal pain, confusion and nerve symptoms, but no skin disease<\/b>. Urine may darken on standing in light. Screen PBG with the <b>Watson-Schwartz or Hoesch test<\/b> (Ehrlich reagent).<\/li>\n<li><b>Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT)<\/b>: uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase is low, so <b>uroporphyrins<\/b> build up. Porphyrins react with sunlight, so the problem is <b>blistering skin<\/b> on sun-exposed areas, not pain. The most common porphyria.<\/li>\n<li><b>Congenital erythropoietic porphyria<\/b>: severe light sensitivity and scarring, and red-brown teeth that glow under UV light.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: early vs late blocks<\/b>An <b>early<\/b> block piles up ALA and PBG, which hurt <b>nerves<\/b> (AIP). A <b>late<\/b> block piles up porphyrins, which hurt <b>skin<\/b> in sunlight (PCT).<\/div>\n<p><b>Lead poisoning<\/b> blocks two enzymes: <b>ALA dehydratase<\/b> (so ALA rises) and <b>ferrochelatase<\/b> (so iron can't be added and <b>free erythrocyte protoporphyrin / zinc protoporphyrin<\/b> rises). PBG stays normal, which helps separate lead from AIP. Add coarse basophilic stippling and a microcytic anemia, and the picture is complete.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Myoglobin<\/h3>\n<p>Myoglobin is the oxygen-holding heme protein of muscle. In crush injury or <b>rhabdomyolysis<\/b>, it pours into the blood and is small enough to pass into urine, turning it <b>red-brown<\/b>. It can block and damage the kidney tubules. The urine dipstick blood pad is positive (it detects heme), but no red cells are seen, and the plasma stays clear (unlike hemoglobinuria, where plasma is pink). CK will be very high.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Pale stools, dark urine positive for bilirubin, absent urobilinogen. Which type of jaundice?<\/summary><p>Posthepatic (obstructive). Conjugated bilirubin can't reach the gut, so it spills into urine and no urobilinogen forms.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is urine bilirubin negative in hemolytic jaundice?<\/summary><p>The excess is unconjugated bilirubin, which is bound to albumin and not water-soluble, so it can't be filtered into urine.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Severe abdominal pain, confusion, no rash, high urine PBG. Diagnosis?<\/summary><p>Acute intermittent porphyria.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Triglycerides are 520 mg/dL. How should LDL be reported?<\/summary><p>By direct LDL measurement, because the Friedewald calculation is invalid above 400 mg/dL.<\/p><\/details>"},"C7":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Hormones are chemical messages. Endocrine questions look complicated, but almost all of them use the same idea: the <b>feedback loop<\/b>. Once you can follow a loop, you can work out any pattern of \"high this, low that\" without memorizing it. This lesson explains the loops, then the hormone disorders, vitamins and trace elements.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The feedback loop: a thermostat<\/h3>\n<p>Most hormone systems have three levels. The <b>hypothalamus<\/b> (brain) senses a need and signals the <b>pituitary<\/b>, which signals the target <b>gland<\/b>, which makes the final hormone. When there's enough final hormone, it switches the top levels off (<b>negative feedback<\/b>), just like a thermostat switching off the heater when the room is warm.<\/p>\n<p>So to find where the problem is, compare the pituitary hormone with the gland hormone:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gland hormone <b>low<\/b> and pituitary hormone <b>high<\/b> → the <b>gland<\/b> has failed, and the pituitary is shouting at it (<b>primary<\/b> disease).<\/li>\n<li>Gland hormone <b>low<\/b> and pituitary hormone <b>low<\/b> → the <b>pituitary<\/b> isn't sending the signal (<b>secondary<\/b> disease).<\/li>\n<li>Gland hormone <b>high<\/b> and pituitary hormone <b>low<\/b> → the gland is overproducing on its own, and the pituitary has gone quiet.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Thyroid: the loop in action<\/h3>\n<p>TRH (hypothalamus) → <b>TSH<\/b> (pituitary) → <b>T4 and T3<\/b> (thyroid). TSH is the most sensitive first test.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>High TSH, low free T4 = primary hypothyroidism.<\/b> The thyroid is failing (most often <b>Hashimoto thyroiditis<\/b>, with anti-TPO antibodies), so TSH climbs.<\/li>\n<li><b>Low TSH, high free T4 = hyperthyroidism<\/b>, most often <b>Graves disease<\/b>. Antibodies (TSI) stimulate the TSH receptor, so the thyroid overproduces and the pituitary switches TSH off.<\/li>\n<li><b>Low TSH, low free T4 = secondary (pituitary) hypothyroidism.<\/b><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Measure <b>free<\/b> T4 rather than total T4. Most T4 is bound to thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG), and TBG rises in pregnancy and with estrogen, raising total T4 without changing thyroid function.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Suppress the excess, stimulate the deficiency<\/h3>\n<p>Single hormone levels go up and down through the day, so many conditions are confirmed with <b>dynamic tests<\/b>. The rule: <b>if you suspect too much hormone, try to suppress it; if you suspect too little, try to stimulate it.<\/b> A normal gland responds; a diseased one doesn't.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cushing syndrome<\/b> (too much cortisol: weight gain, purple stretch marks, high glucose): give <b>dexamethasone<\/b>, a cortisol-like drug that should switch off ACTH. In Cushing, cortisol <b>fails to suppress<\/b>. With high-dose dexamethasone, pituitary (Cushing <i>disease<\/i>) usually does suppress; ectopic ACTH from a tumour does not.<\/li>\n<li><b>Addison disease<\/b> (adrenal failure: low cortisol, low sodium, high potassium, dark skin): give synthetic ACTH (<b>cosyntropin<\/b>). Cortisol <b>fails to rise<\/b>. ACTH is high because the pituitary is trying to compensate.<\/li>\n<li><b>Acromegaly<\/b> (too much growth hormone): GH <b>fails to suppress<\/b> after a glucose drink; IGF-1 is high.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Other hormone disorders to know<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Conn syndrome<\/b> (primary hyperaldosteronism): an adrenal tumour makes too much aldosterone, which retains sodium and wastes potassium. Result: <b>high blood pressure, low potassium, high aldosterone, low renin<\/b> (renin is switched off by feedback). In secondary hyperaldosteronism, renin is high.<\/li>\n<li><b>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia<\/b>: usually 21-hydroxylase deficiency. Cortisol can't be made, so ACTH stays high and the building blocks pile up. <b>17-hydroxyprogesterone<\/b> is high (newborn screening test), and androgens rise.<\/li>\n<li><b>Pheochromocytoma<\/b>: an adrenal tumour making adrenaline-type hormones (catecholamines). Episodes of high blood pressure, sweating and palpitations. Test <b>plasma or urine metanephrines<\/b> (best), catecholamines and <b>VMA<\/b>. Neuroblastoma in children also raises VMA and HVA.<\/li>\n<li><b>Carcinoid tumour<\/b>: makes serotonin; test urine <b>5-HIAA<\/b> (its breakdown product). Avoid bananas, pineapple, walnuts and avocado beforehand; they contain serotonin.<\/li>\n<li><b>Prolactinoma<\/b>: high prolactin, causing milk production and loss of periods.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Steroid vs peptide hormones.<\/b> <b>Steroid<\/b> hormones (cortisol, aldosterone, estrogen, testosterone) are made from <b>cholesterol<\/b>, cross cell membranes and act inside the cell. <b>Peptide<\/b> hormones (insulin, GH, ACTH, PTH) can't cross membranes and act on surface receptors. <b>Catecholamines<\/b> are made from the amino acid tyrosine.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Calcium, PTH and phosphorus<\/h3>\n<p><b>PTH<\/b> raises blood calcium by pulling it from bone, holding it in the kidney, and activating vitamin D. It also makes the kidney excrete phosphorus. So calcium and phosphorus usually move in <b>opposite<\/b> directions:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Primary hyperparathyroidism<\/b> (usually an adenoma): <b>high PTH, high calcium, low phosphorus<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hypoparathyroidism<\/b> (often after neck surgery): <b>low PTH, low calcium, high phosphorus<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hypercalcemia of malignancy<\/b>: tumours make <b>PTH-related peptide<\/b>, so calcium is high but the real <b>PTH is suppressed (low)<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: high calcium and PTH<\/b>High calcium with high PTH points to the parathyroid. High calcium with <b>low<\/b> PTH points elsewhere: cancer (PTHrP), too much vitamin D, sarcoidosis.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Pregnancy and tumour markers<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>hCG<\/b>: pregnancy, and trophoblastic disease (molar pregnancy, choriocarcinoma) or testicular tumours. Very high levels can cause a falsely low result in sandwich assays (the <b>hook effect<\/b>); dilute and repeat.<\/li>\n<li><b>AFP<\/b>: made by the fetal liver. High maternal AFP suggests a <b>neural tube defect<\/b> (open spine lets AFP leak) or twins. <b>Low<\/b> AFP (with low estriol, high hCG and high inhibin A) suggests <b>Down syndrome<\/b>. In adults, AFP is a marker for <b>liver cancer<\/b> and germ cell tumours.<\/li>\n<li>Other tumour markers: <b>PSA<\/b> (prostate), <b>CA-125<\/b> (ovary), <b>CEA<\/b> (colon, mainly for monitoring), <b>CA 19-9<\/b> (pancreas). These are best for <b>monitoring<\/b> known cancer, not for screening healthy people.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Vitamins: fat-soluble vs water-soluble<\/h3>\n<p><b>A, D, E and K<\/b> are <b>fat-soluble<\/b>: they need fat to be absorbed and are stored in the body. Fat malabsorption (cystic fibrosis, pancreatic disease) causes all four to fall.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>A<\/b>: night blindness. <b>D<\/b>: rickets (children), osteomalacia (adults); measure <b>25-OH vitamin D<\/b>, the storage form. <b>E<\/b>: red cell hemolysis and nerve damage. <b>K<\/b>: bleeding with a <b>long PT<\/b>, because factors II, VII, IX and X need vitamin K.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The B vitamins and C are <b>water-soluble<\/b>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>B1 (thiamine)<\/b>: beriberi; <b>Wernicke encephalopathy<\/b> in alcoholics.<\/li>\n<li><b>B3 (niacin)<\/b>: <b>pellagra<\/b>: the \"3 Ds\", dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia.<\/li>\n<li><b>B6<\/b>: neuropathy and sideroblastic anemia (heme synthesis needs it).<\/li>\n<li><b>Folate<\/b>: megaloblastic anemia and <b>neural tube defects<\/b> in pregnancy. <b>B12<\/b>: megaloblastic anemia plus nerve damage.<\/li>\n<li><b>C<\/b>: <b>scurvy<\/b>: bleeding gums, poor wound healing (collagen needs vitamin C).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Trace elements<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Copper<\/b>: in <b>Wilson disease<\/b>, copper builds up in liver and brain and <b>ceruloplasmin<\/b> (the copper carrier) is <b>low<\/b>. Look for a Kayser-Fleischer ring in the eye.<\/li>\n<li><b>Zinc<\/b>: poor wound healing, rash, poor taste. <b>Iodine<\/b>: goitre and hypothyroidism. <b>Selenium<\/b> and <b>chromium<\/b>: antioxidant defence and glucose metabolism.<\/li>\n<li><b>ACE<\/b> (angiotensin-converting enzyme) is raised in <b>sarcoidosis<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>TSH low, free T4 low. Where is the problem?<\/summary><p>The pituitary (secondary hypothyroidism). A failing thyroid would make TSH high.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Hypertension, potassium 2.9, high aldosterone, low renin. Diagnosis?<\/summary><p>Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why does a malignancy cause high calcium with low PTH?<\/summary><p>The tumour makes PTH-related peptide, which raises calcium. The high calcium switches off the real parathyroid glands.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient with fat malabsorption has a long PT. Which vitamin is low and why does it matter?<\/summary><p>Vitamin K. Without it, the liver can't finish making factors II, VII, IX and X, so clotting slows (VII falls first, so PT prolongs).<\/p><\/details>"},"C8":{"min":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Toxicology answers two questions: <b>is this patient poisoned<\/b>, and <b>is this patient's medicine at a safe and effective level<\/b>? The second part, therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM), is mostly about <i>when<\/i> to draw the blood. Get the timing wrong and even a perfect test gives a useless answer.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Why we monitor some drugs and not others<\/h3>\n<p>Most drugs are safe across a wide range of doses. A few have a <b>narrow therapeutic range<\/b>: the effective level sits close to the toxic level, and patients handle the drug very differently (kidney or liver function, other drugs, age). Those drugs need blood levels. Examples: aminoglycosides, vancomycin, digoxin, lithium, phenytoin, theophylline and transplant drugs.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Peaks, troughs and steady state<\/h3>\n<p>After each dose, the drug level rises to a <b>peak<\/b> and then falls to a <b>trough<\/b> just before the next dose.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Trough<\/b> = drawn <b>just before the next dose<\/b>. It shows the lowest level and is used for most drugs (too high a trough means the drug is building up toward toxicity).<\/li>\n<li><b>Peak<\/b> = drawn after the drug has been absorbed and distributed (for IV aminoglycosides, about 30 minutes after the infusion ends). It shows whether the level is high enough to work.<\/li>\n<li><b>Steady state<\/b>: with regular dosing, levels settle into a repeating pattern after about <b>5 half-lives<\/b>. Drawing before then underestimates the long-term level.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why digoxin must be drawn 6-8 hours after the dose<\/b>Digoxin moves from the blood into heart and muscle tissue slowly. In the first hours, blood levels are high even though tissue levels (where it acts) are not yet. A level drawn too early reads falsely high and could lead to a wrong dose cut. Toxicity is also worse with <b>low potassium<\/b> (and low magnesium, high calcium), so check electrolytes alongside it.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Drug groups and their key points<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Aminoglycosides<\/b> (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin): damage the <b>kidney and inner ear<\/b>. Measure <b>peak<\/b> (effectiveness) and <b>trough<\/b> (toxicity).<\/li>\n<li><b>Vancomycin<\/b>: monitored by trough (or by area-under-the-curve dosing in newer practice).<\/li>\n<li><b>Cardiac drugs<\/b>: digoxin (above); <b>procainamide<\/b> is converted into an active metabolite, <b>NAPA<\/b>, so both are measured.<\/li>\n<li><b>Anticonvulsants<\/b> (phenytoin, valproic acid, carbamazepine, phenobarbital): <b>phenytoin is about 90% protein-bound<\/b>. Only the free (unbound) part works. If albumin is low (liver disease, malnutrition, kidney failure), more is free, so a \"normal\" total level can still be toxic. Measure <b>free phenytoin<\/b> in these patients.<\/li>\n<li><b>Lithium<\/b> (bipolar disorder): draw <b>12 hours after the last dose<\/b>. <b>Never use a lithium-heparin (green) tube<\/b>; it adds lithium and gives a falsely high result.<\/li>\n<li><b>Tricyclic antidepressants<\/b>: toxic to the heart in overdose.<\/li>\n<li><b>Immunosuppressants<\/b> (cyclosporine, tacrolimus): they concentrate inside red cells, so use <b>whole blood (EDTA)<\/b>, drawn as a trough.<\/li>\n<li><b>Theophylline, methotrexate<\/b>: narrow ranges; methotrexate levels guide leucovorin rescue.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Common poisonings<\/h3>\n<p><b>Acetaminophen (paracetamol).<\/b> In overdose, the normal safe pathway is overwhelmed and a toxic metabolite (NAPQI) builds up, destroying liver cells. The level is plotted on the <b>Rumack-Matthew nomogram<\/b> against hours since ingestion, starting at <b>4 hours<\/b> (earlier levels aren't meaningful because absorption is still going on). The antidote, <b>N-acetylcysteine<\/b>, restores glutathione, which neutralises NAPQI.<\/p>\n<p><b>Salicylate (aspirin).<\/b> It first stimulates the breathing centre, so the patient breathes fast: <b>respiratory alkalosis<\/b>. Then it disrupts cell energy production, causing lactic and keto acids to build up: <b>high anion gap metabolic acidosis<\/b>. Tinnitus (ringing in the ears) is a classic clue.<\/p>\n<p><b>Carbon monoxide.<\/b> CO binds hemoglobin about 200 times more tightly than oxygen. Standard pulse oximeters can't tell carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin, so they read <b>falsely normal<\/b>. Measure <b>carboxyhemoglobin by co-oximetry<\/b>. Patients may look \"cherry red\".<\/p>\n<p><b>Toxic alcohols.<\/b> All raise the <b>osmolal gap<\/b> early. As they are metabolised into acids, the <b>anion gap<\/b> rises:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Methanol<\/b> (windshield fluid, homemade spirits) → <b>formic acid<\/b> → damages the optic nerve: <b>blindness<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Ethylene glycol<\/b> (antifreeze) → <b>oxalic acid<\/b> → <b>calcium oxalate crystals<\/b> in urine and <b>kidney failure<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Isopropanol<\/b> (rubbing alcohol) → acetone: high osmolal gap but usually <b>no<\/b> acidosis.<\/li>\n<li><b>Ethanol<\/b> is measured enzymatically (alcohol dehydrogenase) or by gas chromatography.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Other toxins:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Lead<\/b>: anemia, basophilic stippling, nerve and brain damage (worst in children). Test <b>venous whole blood<\/b> in a <b>trace-metal-free tube<\/b> by atomic absorption or ICP-MS.<\/li>\n<li><b>Mercury, arsenic, cadmium<\/b>: heavy metals, measured by atomic absorption or ICP-MS.<\/li>\n<li><b>Organophosphate insecticides<\/b> block acetylcholinesterase: low <b>cholinesterase<\/b>, with drooling, sweating and small pupils.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cyanide<\/b> blocks cytochrome oxidase, so cells can't use oxygen. Bitter almond smell, lactic acidosis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Drugs of abuse: screen, then confirm<\/h3>\n<p>Urine drug screens are <b>immunoassays<\/b>: fast and cheap, but antibodies can <b>cross-react<\/b> with similar-looking medicines (for example some cold remedies with amphetamines). So a positive screen is only \"presumptive\". It must be <b>confirmed by GC-MS or LC-MS/MS<\/b>, which identifies the exact molecule. For legal (forensic) testing, a documented <b>chain of custody<\/b> is required at every step.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A lithium level is unexpectedly high and was drawn into a green-top tube. Likely cause?<\/summary><p>Contamination from lithium heparin. Recollect in a plain or sodium heparin tube.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient with very low albumin has a \"therapeutic\" total phenytoin but shows toxic symptoms. Why?<\/summary><p>With less albumin, more phenytoin is unbound and active. Measure the free phenytoin level.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>High osmolal gap, high anion gap and envelope-shaped crystals in urine. Which poison?<\/summary><p>Ethylene glycol (calcium oxalate crystals).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>When can an acetaminophen level first be plotted on the nomogram?<\/summary><p>At 4 hours after ingestion.<\/p><\/details>"},"C9":{"min":3,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Chemistry traps usually turn on a <b>method limitation<\/b> (what an instrument can't see or is fooled by) or a <b>specificity<\/b> issue (a test that rises for more than one reason). Each trap below comes with the reasoning, followed by more classic chemistry traps.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 1: pseudohyponatremia shows on every analyzer<\/h3>\n<p>It doesn't. <b>Indirect ISE<\/b> dilutes the sample before measuring and assumes plasma is about 93% water. In severe lipemia or very high protein (myeloma), there is less water per unit of plasma, so the diluted sample contains less sodium and reads <b>falsely low<\/b>. <b>Direct ISE<\/b> (undiluted, used on blood gas analyzers) measures sodium in the water phase and gives the true value. Measured osmolality will also be normal, which proves the low sodium is false.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 2: a \"small\" ketone result rules out severe ketosis<\/h3>\n<p>It doesn't. The dipstick uses <b>nitroprusside<\/b>, which reacts with <b>acetoacetate<\/b> (and weakly with acetone) but <b>not with beta-hydroxybutyrate<\/b>, the main ketone in severe DKA. As DKA is treated, beta-hydroxybutyrate turns into acetoacetate, so the dipstick can look <i>worse<\/i> while the patient is actually getting better.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 3: CK-MB is as good as troponin<\/h3>\n<p>Troponin is <b>more specific<\/b> (cardiac forms exist only in heart muscle) and stays raised for 7-10 days. CK-MB is also found in skeletal muscle in small amounts and returns to normal in 2-3 days. That short life is CK-MB's one advantage: it is better for detecting a <b>re-infarction<\/b> a few days after the first one.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 4: a high ALP means liver disease<\/h3>\n<p>ALP is made in the liver (bile ducts), <b>bone<\/b>, placenta and intestine. A high ALP in a growing teenager, a pregnant woman, or someone with Paget disease or bone metastases may have nothing to do with the liver. Check <b>GGT or 5'-nucleotidase<\/b>: high with ALP means liver; normal means bone or another source.<\/p>\n\n<h3>More chemistry traps<\/h3>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Hemolysis<\/b>Red cells contain far more potassium, LD, AST and phosphorus than plasma. A hemolyzed sample makes all four falsely high. Hemoglobin's color can also interfere with spectrophotometric tests.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Glucose in an old tube<\/b>An unspun tube left for hours reads falsely low (cells keep consuming glucose) unless it was drawn in a fluoride (gray) tube.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>EDTA contamination<\/b>Very high potassium with very low calcium (and low ALP and magnesium) in a well patient suggests EDTA carry-over from drawing in the wrong order. Recollect.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Biotin<\/b>High-dose biotin supplements interfere with streptavidin-biotin immunoassays: sandwich assays (TSH, troponin) read falsely <b>low<\/b>, competitive assays (free T4) read falsely <b>high<\/b>, mimicking hyperthyroidism.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Hook effect<\/b>An extremely high analyte (hCG, prolactin, tumour markers) can saturate both antibodies in a sandwich assay and give a falsely low result. Dilute and repeat when the result doesn't fit the clinical picture.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Total calcium with low albumin<\/b>Low albumin lowers total calcium while ionized calcium is normal. Correct for albumin or measure ionized calcium before calling it hypocalcemia.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Bilirubin and light<\/b>Samples left in light lose bilirubin and read falsely low.<\/div>\n\n<h3>How to approach a chemistry trap<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Does the result fit the patient? If not, suspect the sample (hemolysis, lipemia, wrong tube, delay) before suspecting disease.<\/li>\n<li>What does the method actually detect? Many traps hinge on a method missing something (beta-hydroxybutyrate) or being fooled by something (biotin, lipids).<\/li>\n<li>Is the marker specific? Ask which other organs or conditions raise it.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Sodium 125 on the main analyzer, 139 on the blood gas machine, triglycerides 2500. Which is right and why?<\/summary><p>139 (direct ISE). The main analyzer uses indirect ISE, which is falsely low with severe lipemia.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient's TSH is suppressed and free T4 high, but they feel well and take hair supplements. What do you suspect?<\/summary><p>Biotin interference. Ask the patient to stop biotin (often for 2-3 days) and retest, or use a method not based on biotin-streptavidin.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Potassium 7.9, calcium 4.0 mg/dL, patient well. Likely cause?<\/summary><p>K2EDTA contamination of the sample. Recollect, following the correct order of draw.<\/p><\/details>"},"H1":{"min":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Hematology is the study of blood cells: how they are made, what they look like, and what their numbers tell you. Almost every hematology question comes back to three skills: knowing how a normal cell grows up, reading the red cell indices, and knowing what \"normal\" looks like so you can spot what isn't. This lesson builds those three skills.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The big picture: one factory, three product lines<\/h3>\n<p>All blood cells are made in the bone marrow from one parent cell, the <b>hematopoietic stem cell<\/b> (CD34 positive). Think of the marrow as a factory with three product lines:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Red cells<\/b> carry oxygen. Their job depends on hemoglobin, so most red cell disease is about making too little hemoglobin, making the wrong kind, or losing cells too fast.<\/li>\n<li><b>White cells<\/b> fight infection. When they go wrong you get either a reaction to something (infection, allergy) or a cancer of the factory (leukemia).<\/li>\n<li><b>Platelets<\/b> plug holes in vessels. Too few means bleeding; too many can mean a marrow disease.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Each line is controlled by a signal: <b>erythropoietin (EPO)<\/b> from the kidney drives red cells, <b>thrombopoietin<\/b> from the liver drives platelets, and colony-stimulating factors drive white cells. When the exam says \"low EPO\", it is hinting that the kidney is not asking for red cells, or that the marrow is making them on its own (polycythemia vera).<\/p>\n\n<h3>How a cell grows up (maturation)<\/h3>\n<p>Every line follows the same three rules as a cell matures. Learn the rules and you can stage almost any cell you see:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>The cell gets smaller.<\/b> Blasts are big; mature cells are small.<\/li>\n<li><b>The nucleus tightens.<\/b> A young nucleus is fine and lacy with visible nucleoli. An old nucleus is dark and clumped.<\/li>\n<li><b>The cytoplasm changes colour.<\/b> Young cytoplasm is deep blue because it is full of RNA (the cell is busy building proteins). As the cell finishes its work, the blue fades and it turns pink or lilac.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><b>Red cell line:<\/b> pronormoblast → basophilic normoblast → polychromatic normoblast → orthochromic normoblast → reticulocyte → mature RBC. The names describe the colour. \"Basophilic\" means blue (lots of RNA). \"Polychromatic\" means mixed blue-grey, because hemoglobin (pink) is now being made alongside the RNA (blue). \"Orthochromic\" means the cytoplasm is nearly the right pink colour. The orthochromic normoblast pushes its nucleus out and becomes a reticulocyte, which still has a little RNA left. That leftover RNA is what new methylene blue stains in a retic count.<\/p>\n<p><b>Neutrophil line:<\/b> myeloblast → promyelocyte → myelocyte → metamyelocyte → band → segmented neutrophil. Two landmarks are worth remembering. The <b>promyelocyte<\/b> is where the big primary (azurophilic) granules appear. The <b>myelocyte<\/b> is the first stage with secondary (specific) granules, and the last stage that can still divide. After that, the nucleus just indents (metamyelocyte, kidney bean), stretches into a band, and finally pinches into segments.<\/p>\n<p><b>Platelet line:<\/b> megakaryoblast → promegakaryocyte → megakaryocyte → platelets. Megakaryocytes don't divide their cells; they copy their DNA over and over (endomitosis), become huge, and then shed thousands of platelets from their cytoplasm.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why \"megaloblastic\" cells look strange<\/b>In B12 or folate deficiency the cell cannot make DNA fast enough, but it can still make RNA and hemoglobin normally. So the cytoplasm matures on schedule while the nucleus lags behind. You get a big cell with a young-looking, open nucleus sitting in grown-up cytoplasm. This mismatch is called <b>nuclear-cytoplasmic asynchrony<\/b>, and it is the signature of megaloblastic anemia.<\/div>\n\n<h3>The red cell indices, explained<\/h3>\n<p>The analyzer measures a few things directly (RBC count, hemoglobin, and cell size) and calculates the rest. The indices describe the <i>average<\/i> red cell:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>MCV (mean cell volume)<\/b> = how big the average red cell is. Formula: Hct × 10 ÷ RBC. Normal 80-100 fL. Under 80 is microcytic (small); over 100 is macrocytic (big).<\/li>\n<li><b>MCH (mean cell hemoglobin)<\/b> = how much hemoglobin is in the average cell. Formula: Hgb × 10 ÷ RBC. Normal 27-33 pg.<\/li>\n<li><b>MCHC (mean cell hemoglobin concentration)<\/b> = how tightly packed the hemoglobin is. Formula: Hgb × 100 ÷ Hct. Normal 32-36 g/dL. Low MCHC means hypochromic (pale cells).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Hct 30%, RBC 4.0 × 10¹²/L, Hgb 9.0 g/dL.<br>MCV = 30 × 10 ÷ 4.0 = <b>75 fL<\/b> → microcytic.<br>MCH = 9.0 × 10 ÷ 4.0 = <b>22.5 pg<\/b> → low.<br>MCHC = 9.0 × 100 ÷ 30 = <b>30 g/dL<\/b> → hypochromic.<br>Small, pale cells: think iron deficiency first, then thalassemia.<\/div>\n<p><b>Why the MCHC is so useful as a check.<\/b> Red cells can't really be \"over-full\" of hemoglobin. An MCHC above about 36-37 g/dL is almost always one of three things: spherocytes (cells lost membrane and became dense balls), an artifact that falsely raised the hemoglobin (lipemia, very high WBC), or an artifact that falsely lowered the hematocrit (cold agglutinins clumping the cells). So a high MCHC should make you suspicious, not just record it.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: the rule of three<\/b>Hgb × 3 ≈ Hct (within about 3). Hgb 10 → Hct about 30. If the numbers don't fit, something is interfering with the sample. Look for lipemia, cold agglutinins or unusual cells before you release the result.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Reticulocytes: what the marrow is doing right now<\/h3>\n<p>The reticulocyte count is the marrow's report card. A red cell lives about 120 days, but a reticulocyte is only a day or so old, so the retic count tells you how many red cells the marrow is releasing <i>today<\/i>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>High retics<\/b> = the marrow is working hard to replace lost cells. Think bleeding or hemolysis.<\/li>\n<li><b>Low retics<\/b> in an anemic patient = the marrow can't keep up. Think iron, B12 or folate deficiency, aplastic anemia, or marrow being crowded out (leukemia, fibrosis).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Why we \"correct\" the retic count.<\/b> The retic count is reported as a percentage of red cells. If a patient is anemic, there are fewer red cells overall, so the same number of retics looks like a bigger percentage. The correction shrinks it back: corrected retic = retic % × (patient Hct ÷ 45).<\/p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Retic 6%, Hct 30%. Corrected = 6 × (30 ÷ 45) = <b>4%<\/b>. Still raised, so the marrow is genuinely responding.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Correcting the WBC for nucleated red cells<\/h3>\n<p>Many analyzers and the counting chamber can't tell a nucleated red cell (nRBC) from a white cell, because both have a nucleus. If the smear shows nRBCs, the WBC count is falsely high. The fix: corrected WBC = WBC × 100 ÷ (100 + nRBCs per 100 WBCs). With WBC 10.0 and 25 nRBCs per 100 WBC, corrected WBC = 10.0 × 100 ÷ 125 = 8.0.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Normal values: your baseline<\/h3>\n<p>You can't spot abnormal without knowing normal. These are approximate adult ranges (each lab sets its own):<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hemoglobin:<\/b> men 13.5-17.5, women 12.0-16.0 g/dL. Men run higher because testosterone stimulates EPO.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hematocrit:<\/b> men 41-53%, women 36-46%.<\/li>\n<li><b>WBC:<\/b> 4.5-11.0 × 10⁹/L.<\/li>\n<li><b>Platelets:<\/b> 150-450 × 10⁹/L.<\/li>\n<li><b>Differential:<\/b> neutrophils 50-70%, lymphocytes 20-40%, monocytes 2-9%, eosinophils 1-4%, basophils 0-1%. A handy order from most to least common is \"<b>N<\/b>ever <b>L<\/b>et <b>M<\/b>onkeys <b>E<\/b>at <b>B<\/b>ananas\".<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Newborns are the big exception: they have much higher hemoglobin and MCV, and children have relatively more lymphocytes. If a question gives you a baby's results, don't judge them by adult ranges.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The 8 facts, and why each one is true<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Low MCV = iron deficiency, thalassemia, chronic disease, sideroblastic anemia, lead.<\/b> All of these are problems making hemoglobin (no iron, faulty globin chains, or blocked heme). With less hemoglobin to fill them, the cells divide an extra time and end up small. <b>High MCV = B12 or folate deficiency, liver disease, alcohol<\/b>, because DNA is slow (megaloblastic) or the membrane carries extra lipid.<\/li>\n<li><b>The retic count tells you if the marrow is responding<\/b>, as explained above.<\/li>\n<li><b>Spherocytes plus a positive DAT = autoimmune hemolysis; negative DAT = hereditary spherocytosis.<\/b> In both, the spleen nibbles membrane off the cells and they round up. The DAT tells you <i>why<\/i>: antibody on the cell (immune) or an inherited membrane protein defect (spectrin, ankyrin).<\/li>\n<li><b>Schistocytes = DIC, TTP, HUS or a mechanical heart valve.<\/b> Fragments mean red cells are being sliced, either by fibrin strands in small vessels or by a metal valve.<\/li>\n<li><b>Auer rods = AML; t(15;17) = APL; t(9;22) = CML.<\/b> Auer rods are fused myeloid granules, so they prove the blasts are myeloid.<\/li>\n<li><b>PT tests the extrinsic pathway (factor VII) and warfarin; aPTT tests the intrinsic pathway and heparin.<\/b> Warfarin hits factor VII first because it has the shortest half-life.<\/li>\n<li><b>DIC: everything is used up.<\/b> Clotting is switched on all over the body, so platelets and fibrinogen are consumed (low), clotting times lengthen (PT and aPTT long), and the clots are broken down (D-dimer high).<\/li>\n<li><b>Light blue citrate tubes must be filled to the line (9:1).<\/b> Citrate works by binding calcium. Too little blood means too much citrate, which soaks up the calcium added during testing and falsely prolongs the clotting times.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Hgb 12 g/dL and Hct 45%. Is anything wrong?<\/summary><p>By the rule of three, Hgb 12 should give a Hct of about 36. A Hct of 45 is far off, so suspect an error or interference (and the MCHC would be a low 26.7). Recheck the sample before reporting.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which neutrophil stage is the last one able to divide?<\/summary><p>The myelocyte. It is also the first stage with secondary (specific) granules.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why does a megaloblastic cell have a \"young\" nucleus in \"old\" cytoplasm?<\/summary><p>B12 and folate are needed to make DNA. Without them the nucleus matures slowly, while RNA and hemoglobin production (the cytoplasm) carry on normally.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>An MCHC of 39 g/dL is reported. Name three possible causes.<\/summary><p>Spherocytes, a falsely high hemoglobin (lipemia, extreme leukocytosis) or a falsely low hematocrit (cold agglutinins). Warm the sample or replace the plasma with saline to sort out the artifacts.<\/p><\/details>"},"H2":{"min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Anemia means too little hemoglobin to carry the oxygen the body needs. It is not a diagnosis on its own; it is a sign that something is wrong with making red cells, keeping them alive, or keeping them in the body. The exam expects you to work out <i>which<\/i> anemia from a few clues: cell size, iron studies, the retic count and what the cells look like on the smear.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Step 1: sort the anemia by cell size<\/h3>\n<p>The MCV is the fastest way to narrow things down, because each size group has a different underlying problem.<\/p>\n<p><b>Microcytic (MCV under 80): a hemoglobin-making problem.<\/b> A developing red cell keeps dividing until it has enough hemoglobin inside. If hemoglobin is made slowly, the cell squeezes in an extra division and ends up small and pale. Hemoglobin needs three things: <b>iron<\/b>, <b>heme<\/b> (built from iron plus protoporphyrin) and <b>globin chains<\/b>. Break any one and you get small cells:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>No iron available: <b>iron deficiency<\/b> and <b>anemia of chronic disease<\/b> (iron is present but locked away).<\/li>\n<li>Heme can't be built: <b>sideroblastic anemia<\/b> and <b>lead poisoning<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Globin chains are missing: <b>thalassemia<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Macrocytic (MCV over 100): usually a DNA problem.<\/b> B12 and folate are needed to make DNA. Without them the nucleus divides slowly while the cytoplasm keeps growing, so the cells come out large (megaloblastic). Liver disease and alcohol give big cells for a different reason: extra cholesterol and lipid in the membrane makes the cell larger, but DNA is normal (so no hypersegmented neutrophils).<\/p>\n<p><b>Normocytic (MCV 80-100): normal-sized cells, just not enough of them.<\/b> Here the <b>retic count<\/b> decides the next step. High retics mean the marrow is working and cells are being lost (bleeding) or destroyed (hemolysis). Low retics mean the marrow isn't producing (aplastic anemia, kidney disease with low EPO, early chronic disease).<\/p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook for microcytic causes: \"TAILS\"<\/b><b>T<\/b>halassemia, <b>A<\/b>nemia of chronic disease, <b>I<\/b>ron deficiency, <b>L<\/b>ead poisoning, <b>S<\/b>ideroblastic anemia.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Step 2: read the iron studies<\/h3>\n<p>Four tests describe the body's iron. It helps to picture iron as money:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Ferritin<\/b> = savings in the bank (stored iron). It is the single best test for iron deficiency.<\/li>\n<li><b>Serum iron<\/b> = cash in your pocket (iron moving in the blood).<\/li>\n<li><b>TIBC (total iron-binding capacity)<\/b> = the number of empty trucks (transferrin) sent out to collect iron. The body makes more trucks when it is short of iron.<\/li>\n<li><b>Transferrin saturation<\/b> = how full those trucks are (serum iron ÷ TIBC).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Now each pattern makes sense:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Iron deficiency:<\/b> savings gone (<b>low ferritin<\/b>), little cash (<b>low iron<\/b>), and the liver sends out extra trucks (<b>high TIBC<\/b>). Saturation is very low.<\/li>\n<li><b>Anemia of chronic disease:<\/b> inflammation raises <b>hepcidin<\/b>, a hormone that locks iron inside macrophages. The savings are there (<b>ferritin normal or high<\/b>; ferritin also rises because it is an acute-phase protein), but cash is short (<b>low iron<\/b>), and the body makes <i>fewer<\/i> trucks (<b>low TIBC<\/b>).<\/li>\n<li><b>Thalassemia:<\/b> iron is fine (<b>normal or high<\/b>). The problem is the globin chain, not the iron.<\/li>\n<li><b>Sideroblastic anemia:<\/b> iron gets into the cell's mitochondria but can't be built into heme, so it piles up (<b>high iron, high ferritin<\/b>). In the marrow the trapped iron forms a ring around the nucleus: the <b>ringed sideroblast<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: TIBC<\/b>Iron deficiency and chronic disease both have low serum iron. The tie-breaker is TIBC: <b>high<\/b> in iron deficiency, <b>low<\/b> in chronic disease. Ferritin backs it up: low in iron deficiency, normal or high in chronic disease.<\/div>\n\n<h3>B12, folate and pernicious anemia<\/h3>\n<p>Both deficiencies cause the same blood picture: macrocytic anemia, <b>oval macrocytes<\/b>, <b>hypersegmented neutrophils<\/b> (5 or more lobes) and a very high LD (the marrow destroys many faulty cells before they leave, called ineffective erythropoiesis). Often all three cell lines are low.<\/p>\n<p>To tell them apart, use two metabolites: <b>homocysteine<\/b> rises in both, but <b>methylmalonic acid (MMA)<\/b> rises only in B12 deficiency. B12 deficiency can also damage nerves (numbness, poor balance), which folate deficiency does not.<\/p>\n<p><b>Pernicious anemia<\/b> is the classic cause of B12 deficiency. The body makes antibodies against <b>intrinsic factor<\/b> (or the parietal cells that make it). Intrinsic factor is the carrier B12 needs to be absorbed in the ileum, so no intrinsic factor means no B12 absorbed, however much you eat.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Aplastic anemia<\/h3>\n<p>Here the factory itself shuts down. The marrow is empty (replaced by fat), so all three lines fall: <b>pancytopenia<\/b> with a low retic count. Causes include drugs, chemicals (benzene), radiation, viruses and autoimmune attack on stem cells. The cells that are made look normal, which helps separate it from leukemia or megaloblastic anemia.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Reading red cell shapes<\/h3>\n<p>Each abnormal shape tells a story about what happened to the cell. Instead of memorising a list, link each shape to its cause:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Spherocyte<\/b> (small, round, no pale centre): the cell has lost membrane, so it rounds up. Caused by antibodies (autoimmune hemolysis), an inherited membrane defect (hereditary spherocytosis) or burns.<\/li>\n<li><b>Target cell<\/b>: too much membrane for the amount of hemoglobin, so the extra membrane bunches in the middle. Seen in thalassemia, HbC, liver disease and after splenectomy.<\/li>\n<li><b>Sickle cell<\/b>: HbS polymerises into rigid rods when it loses oxygen and bends the cell.<\/li>\n<li><b>Teardrop<\/b>: cells squeezed out of a scarred, crowded marrow (myelofibrosis).<\/li>\n<li><b>Schistocyte / helmet cell<\/b>: cells sliced by fibrin strands or a mechanical valve (DIC, TTP, HUS).<\/li>\n<li><b>Bite and blister cells<\/b>: the spleen plucked out Heinz bodies (G6PD deficiency).<\/li>\n<li><b>Acanthocyte<\/b> (few, uneven spikes): abnormal membrane lipids in liver disease or abetalipoproteinemia. <b>Echinocyte / burr cell<\/b> (many even spikes): uremia, or an artifact of drying.<\/li>\n<li><b>Stomatocyte<\/b> (slit-shaped pale area): alcoholism, hereditary stomatocytosis, Rh-null cells.<\/li>\n<li><b>Rouleaux<\/b> (stacked coins): too much plasma protein makes cells stick face to face (myeloma, inflammation). <b>Agglutination<\/b> (random clumps) means antibody, usually cold agglutinins.<\/li>\n<li><b>Polychromasia<\/b> (bluish-grey cells): young reticulocytes, a sign the marrow is responding.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: rouleaux vs agglutination<\/b>Rouleaux look like neat stacks of coins and disperse when the plasma is replaced with saline. Agglutination forms irregular grape-like clumps and does <b>not<\/b> disperse with saline.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Red cell inclusions: leftovers inside the cell<\/h3>\n<p>Inclusions are pieces of something left behind. Ask: what is it made of, and why is it still there?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Howell-Jolly body<\/b>: a small round piece of DNA. Normally the spleen removes it, so seeing it suggests <b>no spleen<\/b> (or megaloblastic anemia).<\/li>\n<li><b>Basophilic stippling<\/b>: clumped ribosomes (RNA), seen as fine blue dots. Coarse stippling points to <b>lead poisoning<\/b> (lead blocks the enzyme that breaks down RNA) or thalassemia.<\/li>\n<li><b>Pappenheimer bodies<\/b>: iron granules. They confirm with <b>Prussian blue<\/b> (iron stain). Seen in sideroblastic anemia and after splenectomy.<\/li>\n<li><b>Heinz bodies<\/b>: clumps of denatured hemoglobin. <b>Invisible on Wright stain<\/b>; you need a supravital stain (crystal violet). Seen in G6PD deficiency and unstable hemoglobins.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cabot rings<\/b>: loops of leftover mitotic spindle in severe anemia.<\/li>\n<li><b>HbH inclusions<\/b>: beta-chain tetramers in alpha thalassemia, giving a \"golf ball\" look with brilliant cresyl blue.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: which inclusions need a special stain?<\/b>\"<b>H<\/b>einz and <b>H<\/b>bH need a supravital stain; <b>P<\/b>appenheimer needs <b>P<\/b>russian blue.\" Everything else shows on a routine Wright stain.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Low iron, low TIBC, high ferritin. Which anemia?<\/summary><p>Anemia of chronic disease. Hepcidin traps iron in macrophages; ferritin is high as stored iron and as an acute-phase protein; the body makes less transferrin, so TIBC is low.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Macrocytic anemia with high homocysteine and normal MMA. B12 or folate?<\/summary><p>Folate deficiency. MMA rises only when B12 is lacking.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why do Howell-Jolly bodies suggest a missing spleen?<\/summary><p>The spleen normally \"pits\" these DNA fragments out of red cells. Without a spleen they stay in the circulating cells.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A smear shows coarse basophilic stippling and the patient paints old houses. What is the likely cause?<\/summary><p>Lead poisoning. Lead inhibits pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase (so RNA clumps) and blocks heme synthesis, giving a microcytic anemia.<\/p><\/details>"},"H3":{"min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Hemolysis means red cells are being destroyed early. A normal red cell lives about 120 days; in hemolytic anemia it may last only days or weeks. This lesson covers how to prove hemolysis is happening, how to work out the cause, and how the inherited hemoglobin disorders (sickle cell and thalassemia) fit in.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Two places cells can be destroyed<\/h3>\n<p>Where a red cell breaks down changes the lab picture, so learn this first:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Extravascular hemolysis<\/b> (the common type) happens inside macrophages, mainly in the spleen and liver. The macrophage eats the cell and recycles it neatly. Hemoglobin is broken into bilirubin, so bilirubin rises, but little free hemoglobin reaches the plasma. Spherocytes, warm autoimmune anemia and hereditary spherocytosis destroy cells this way, and the spleen often enlarges.<\/li>\n<li><b>Intravascular hemolysis<\/b> happens inside the blood vessels. Cells burst open, spilling hemoglobin straight into the plasma. You see pink plasma, hemoglobin in the urine and later <b>hemosiderin<\/b> in the urine (iron left in kidney cells). Causes include ABO transfusion reactions, PNH, mechanical valves and severe DIC.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Proving hemolysis: the indicator tests<\/h3>\n<p>Each test reflects one part of the clean-up process:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Haptoglobin goes down.<\/b> Haptoglobin is a \"sponge\" protein that grabs free hemoglobin and carries it to the liver. In hemolysis the sponge is used up faster than it is made, so the level falls. This is one of the most sensitive signs.<\/li>\n<li><b>LD goes up.<\/b> Red cells are full of lactate dehydrogenase (mostly LD1), which leaks out when they break.<\/li>\n<li><b>Indirect (unconjugated) bilirubin goes up.<\/b> Broken-down heme becomes unconjugated bilirubin faster than the liver can process it.<\/li>\n<li><b>Reticulocytes go up.<\/b> The healthy marrow speeds up to replace the lost cells.<\/li>\n<li><b>Free plasma hemoglobin<\/b> and <b>urine hemosiderin<\/b> rise mainly in intravascular hemolysis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Putting it together<\/b>Hgb 8.5, retics 9%, LD high, haptoglobin undetectable, indirect bilirubin 3.2 mg/dL. Every marker points the same way: the marrow is working hard (high retics) to replace cells that are being destroyed (low haptoglobin, high LD and bilirubin). That is hemolysis. The next step is to find out why.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Finding the cause: let the smear and DAT guide you<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Spherocytes + positive DAT → autoimmune hemolytic anemia.<\/b> The DAT (direct antiglobulin test) detects antibody or complement stuck on the patient's red cells. In <b>warm AIHA<\/b> the antibody is IgG and works at body temperature. In <b>cold agglutinin disease<\/b> it is IgM, usually anti-I, often after Mycoplasma pneumonia; it coats cells with complement, so the DAT is positive for C3d.<\/li>\n<li><b>Spherocytes + negative DAT + high MCHC → hereditary spherocytosis.<\/b> An inherited defect in membrane proteins (spectrin, ankyrin) makes the membrane fragile. The spleen trims it bit by bit until the cell becomes a sphere. Spheres have less surface for their volume, so they burst easily in dilute salt solutions: <b>increased osmotic fragility<\/b>. The modern confirmation is the <b>EMA binding test<\/b> by flow cytometry.<\/li>\n<li><b>Schistocytes + low platelets → microangiopathic hemolysis<\/b> (DIC, TTP, HUS). Red cells are cut by fibrin strands or platelet clumps in small vessels.<\/li>\n<li><b>Bite cells + Heinz bodies → G6PD deficiency.<\/b> G6PD protects hemoglobin from oxidation. Without it, oxidant drugs (primaquine, sulfa drugs, dapsone), fava beans or infection oxidise hemoglobin into Heinz bodies, and the spleen bites them out. Note: <b>test G6PD after the crisis<\/b>, because the oldest, most deficient cells have already been destroyed, which can give a falsely normal result.<\/li>\n<li><b>Loss of CD55 and CD59 → PNH<\/b> (paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria). These proteins protect cells from complement. Without them, complement punches holes in red cells (intravascular hemolysis). Flow cytometry for CD55/CD59 (or FLAER) is the test of choice.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Hemoglobin: what goes wrong<\/h3>\n<p>Normal adult hemoglobin (<b>HbA<\/b>) is made of two alpha chains and two beta chains (α2β2). There are also small amounts of <b>HbA2<\/b> (α2δ2, about 2-3%) and <b>HbF<\/b> (fetal, α2γ2, under 1-2% after infancy). Disorders come in two flavours:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Structural (wrong chain):<\/b> the chain is made in normal amounts but has the wrong amino acid. Example: sickle cell (HbS), HbC.<\/li>\n<li><b>Quantitative (too little chain):<\/b> the chain is normal but not enough is made. These are the <b>thalassemias<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Sickle cell disease and HbC<\/h3>\n<p>In <b>HbS<\/b>, a single swap at <b>position 6 of the beta chain<\/b> (valine replaces glutamic acid) makes the hemoglobin sticky when it loses oxygen. The molecules line up into long rigid rods that bend the cell into a sickle. Sickled cells block small vessels (painful crises) and are destroyed early (hemolysis).<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sickle trait (AS)<\/b>: about 40% HbS and 60% HbA. Usually no symptoms, because the normal HbA dilutes the sticky HbS.<\/li>\n<li><b>Sickle cell disease (SS)<\/b>: mostly HbS, some HbF, no HbA.<\/li>\n<li><b>HbC<\/b> has lysine at the same position 6. It causes target cells and crystals, and <b>HbSC disease<\/b> is milder than SS.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The <b>sickle solubility test<\/b> is a screen: a reducing agent removes oxygen, and HbS becomes insoluble, making the tube cloudy. Two limits are tested often. It can be <b>falsely negative in babies under 6 months<\/b> (they still have mostly HbF) or with very low hemoglobin, and it <b>can't tell trait from disease<\/b>. Always confirm with electrophoresis or HPLC.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Hemoglobin electrophoresis made simple<\/h3>\n<p>At alkaline pH 8.6, hemoglobins carry a negative charge and move toward the positive pole (anode). Each amino acid swap changes the charge a little, so different hemoglobins stop in different places. Glutamic acid is negative; replacing it with neutral valine (S) or positive lysine (C) makes the molecule less negative, so it moves less far.<\/p>\n<p>From farthest to closest to the start: <b>A → F → S → C<\/b>. Remember \"<b>A F<\/b>ast <b>S<\/b>illy <b>C<\/b>at\". HbC shares its slow spot with E, A2 and O; HbS shares its spot with D and G.<\/p>\n<p>To separate the look-alikes, use <b>acid citrate agar at pH 6.0-6.2<\/b>, which separates by a different property: C moves away from E/O/A2, and S moves away from D/G.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Thalassemias<\/h3>\n<p>In thalassemia one type of globin chain is under-produced. The leftover partner chains have nothing to pair with, so they clump and damage the cell. Result: small, pale cells (microcytic, hypochromic), often target cells and stippling.<\/p>\n<p><b>Beta thalassemia<\/b> (too few beta chains):<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Trait (minor)<\/b>: mild anemia, very small cells, and a raised <b>HbA2 above 3.5%<\/b>, because the body makes extra delta chains to make up. This raised A2 is the key lab clue.<\/li>\n<li><b>Major (Cooley anemia)<\/b>: little or no HbA, lots of HbF; severe anemia that needs regular transfusion.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Alpha thalassemia<\/b> (too few alpha chains). Everyone has <b>four<\/b> alpha genes, and severity tracks how many are deleted:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 deleted: silent carrier. 2 deleted: alpha thal trait, mild microcytosis.<\/li>\n<li>3 deleted: <b>HbH disease<\/b>. Spare beta chains form β4 tetramers (HbH).<\/li>\n<li>4 deleted: <b>Hb Bart's hydrops fetalis<\/b>. Spare gamma chains form γ4 (Hb Bart's), which can't release oxygen; usually fatal before or at birth.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: Mentzer index<\/b>MCV ÷ RBC count. <b>Below 13 → thalassemia<\/b>; above 13 → iron deficiency. Why: in thalassemia trait the marrow makes lots of tiny cells (high RBC count), while in iron deficiency it makes fewer cells.<\/div>\n\n<h3>HPFH and the Kleihauer-Betke test<\/h3>\n<p><b>HPFH<\/b> (hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin) is a harmless condition where HbF stays high into adult life. Red cell indices are normal, which is how you tell it apart from beta thalassemia.<\/p>\n<p>The <b>Kleihauer-Betke test<\/b> uses the fact that HbF resists acid. A blood smear is put in acid: adult HbA is washed out (leaving pale \"ghost\" cells), while fetal cells keep their hemoglobin and stain pink. Counting the pink cells in the mother's blood estimates how much fetal blood leaked into her circulation, which sets the dose of Rh immune globulin.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is haptoglobin low in hemolysis?<\/summary><p>It binds free hemoglobin, and the complex is removed by the liver. When hemoglobin is released faster than haptoglobin is made, the level drops.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A 3-month-old with suspected sickle cell disease has a negative solubility test. Is the disease ruled out?<\/summary><p>No. Infants under 6 months have mostly HbF, which can give a false negative. Use electrophoresis or HPLC.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>MCV 62, RBC 6.1, normal ferritin, HbA2 5%. Diagnosis?<\/summary><p>Beta thalassemia trait. Mentzer index 62 ÷ 6.1 ≈ 10 (under 13), iron is normal and HbA2 is above 3.5%.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why can G6PD activity look normal right after a hemolytic crisis?<\/summary><p>The oldest cells, which have the least enzyme, were destroyed first. The remaining young cells and reticulocytes have more G6PD. Retest a few months later.<\/p><\/details>"},"H4":{"min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">White cell questions ask one of two things: is this a <b>normal reaction<\/b> to something (infection, allergy, stress), or is it a <b>cancer of the blood-forming cells<\/b>? Once you know which white cell is raised and what it looks like, most answers follow. This lesson builds that reasoning and then walks through the leukemias, lymphomas and plasma cell diseases.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Reactive changes: the body responding normally<\/h3>\n<p>Each white cell has a job, so the one that is raised hints at the cause:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Neutrophils<\/b> fight bacteria. <b>Neutrophilia<\/b> means bacterial infection, steroids (they release neutrophils stuck to vessel walls) or physical stress.<\/li>\n<li><b>Eosinophils<\/b> fight parasites and drive allergy. Think \"<b>allergy, parasites, drugs<\/b>\".<\/li>\n<li><b>Basophils<\/b> rarely rise. A raised basophil count is a red flag for <b>CML<\/b> or another myeloproliferative disease.<\/li>\n<li><b>Monocytes<\/b> clean up. They rise in TB, chronic inflammation and during recovery from infection.<\/li>\n<li><b>Lymphocytes<\/b> fight viruses. Lymphocytosis suggests a viral infection or pertussis (whooping cough); in an older adult with lots of small mature lymphocytes, think CLL.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Left shift and toxic changes.<\/b> In a bad bacterial infection the marrow releases neutrophils before they are fully mature, so more <b>bands<\/b> and even metamyelocytes appear (\"shift to the left\" on the old counting sheets). Rushed neutrophils also show <b>toxic granulation<\/b> (dark granules), <b>Döhle bodies<\/b> (blue patches of leftover RNA) and <b>vacuoles<\/b>. Together these say \"bacterial infection\".<\/p>\n<p><b>Reactive lymphocytes<\/b> are big, with lots of blue cytoplasm that \"hugs\" the neighbouring red cells. Seen with a positive heterophile (Monospot) test, they mean <b>infectious mononucleosis<\/b> (EBV). The reactive cells are mostly T cells responding to infected B cells.<\/p>\n<p><b>Inherited neutrophil oddities<\/b> that show up as smear questions:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Pelger-Huët anomaly<\/b>: neutrophils with only two lobes (\"pince-nez\" glasses). They work normally, so it is harmless. Don't mistake them for bands. (Acquired \"pseudo-Pelger\" cells appear in myelodysplasia.)<\/li>\n<li><b>Chédiak-Higashi<\/b>: giant fused granules; infections and albinism.<\/li>\n<li><b>May-Hegglin<\/b>: giant platelets with Döhle-like inclusions in the neutrophils.<\/li>\n<li><b>Alder-Reilly<\/b>: dark granules in all white cells, seen in mucopolysaccharide storage diseases.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Leukemoid reaction vs CML: the LAP score<\/h3>\n<p>A severe infection can push the WBC very high with immature cells, which can look like chronic myeloid leukemia. The <b>LAP score<\/b> (leukocyte alkaline phosphatase) separates them. Healthy, activated neutrophils are full of LAP, so a <b>leukemoid reaction has a high LAP<\/b>. The neutrophils in CML come from a cancerous clone and have very little, so <b>CML has a low LAP<\/b>. Basophilia and the Philadelphia chromosome also point to CML.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Acute vs chronic leukemia<\/h3>\n<p>\"Acute\" and \"chronic\" describe how mature the cancer cells are, not how long the patient has been ill:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Acute leukemia<\/b>: the cells are stuck as <b>blasts<\/b>. Blasts can't do the job of mature cells and they crowd the marrow, so the patient gets anemia, bleeding and infections quickly. The WHO cut-off is <b>20% blasts<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Chronic leukemia<\/b>: the cells mature but are made in huge numbers. It develops slowly.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Then ask: <b>myeloid or lymphoid?<\/b> That gives four main types: AML, ALL, CML, CLL.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Telling blasts apart with stains and markers<\/h3>\n<p>Under the microscope, blasts look alike, so we use cytochemical stains and flow cytometry markers:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Myeloperoxidase (MPO) and Sudan black B<\/b> stain granules found in <b>myeloid<\/b> cells. Positive = AML.<\/li>\n<li><b>Nonspecific esterase (NSE)<\/b> stains <b>monocytic<\/b> cells (AML M4 and M5).<\/li>\n<li><b>TdT<\/b> is an enzyme found in the nucleus of immature lymphocytes. Positive = <b>ALL<\/b> lymphoblasts.<\/li>\n<li><b>PAS<\/b> gives \"block\" positivity in lymphoblasts and stains abnormal red cell precursors in M6.<\/li>\n<li><b>Auer rods<\/b> (pink needle-like rods of fused granules) prove the blast is <b>myeloid<\/b>. They are never seen in ALL.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: who stains with what<\/b>\"<b>M<\/b>PO and Sudan black = <b>M<\/b>yeloid; <b>N<\/b>SE = mo<b>N<\/b>ocytes; <b>T<\/b>dT = <b>T<\/b>iny lymphoblasts.\"<\/div>\n\n<h3>The AML subtypes worth knowing<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>APL (M3), t(15;17), PML-RARA.<\/b> The cells are promyelocytes packed with granules and bundles of Auer rods (\"faggot cells\"). Those granules release tissue factor, which sets off <b>DIC<\/b>, so APL is a bleeding emergency. It responds to <b>all-trans retinoic acid (ATRA)<\/b>, which forces the stuck promyelocytes to mature.<\/li>\n<li><b>M4 and M5<\/b>: monocytic blasts, NSE positive, often infiltrating gums and skin.<\/li>\n<li><b>M6<\/b>: erythroleukemia, abnormal PAS-positive red cell precursors.<\/li>\n<li><b>M7<\/b>: megakaryoblastic, marked by <b>CD41 and CD61<\/b> (platelet glycoproteins); often causes marrow fibrosis and is linked to Down syndrome in children.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>The lymphoid and chronic leukemias<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>ALL<\/b>: the most common childhood cancer. Lymphoblasts are <b>TdT positive<\/b> and MPO/Sudan black <b>negative<\/b>. Most are B-cell (CD10, CD19).<\/li>\n<li><b>CLL<\/b>: older adults, very high counts of small mature lymphocytes. These cells are fragile and burst when the smear is spread, leaving <b>smudge cells<\/b>. Markers: B cell markers <b>CD19, CD23<\/b> plus <b>CD5<\/b> (normally a T-cell marker).<\/li>\n<li><b>Hairy cell leukemia<\/b>: B cells with fine hair-like projections. <b>TRAP positive<\/b>; CD11c, CD25, CD103; often with a big spleen and a \"dry tap\" marrow.<\/li>\n<li><b>CML<\/b>: high WBC with every stage of neutrophil development, <b>basophilia<\/b>, low LAP. Cause: the <b>Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22)<\/b>, which joins BCR and ABL into a gene that is permanently switched on. Drugs such as imatinib block this BCR-ABL enzyme.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Myeloproliferative neoplasms and MDS<\/h3>\n<p><b>Myeloproliferative neoplasms<\/b> are marrow cancers where cells mature but are overproduced:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Polycythemia vera<\/b>: too many red cells. The <b>JAK2 V617F<\/b> mutation keeps the growth signal on, so red cells are made without EPO. That is why <b>EPO is low<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Essential thrombocythemia<\/b>: very high platelets.<\/li>\n<li><b>Primary myelofibrosis<\/b>: the marrow scars, so blood cells are made in the spleen and liver instead. Cells squeezed through the scarred marrow come out as <b>teardrops<\/b>, and a marrow aspirate is a \"<b>dry tap<\/b>\".<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS)<\/b> are the opposite problem: the marrow is busy but makes faulty cells. Expect <b>dysplasia<\/b> (odd-looking cells), macrocytosis, pseudo-Pelger cells and ring sideroblasts. Blasts stay <b>under 20%<\/b>; above that it is called AML.<\/p>\n<p><b>Erythrocytosis<\/b> (too many red cells) has three causes. It can be <b>relative<\/b> (low plasma volume, as in dehydration). It can be <b>primary<\/b>: polycythemia vera with low EPO. Or it can be <b>secondary<\/b>: high EPO driven by hypoxia (smoking, lung disease, altitude) or an EPO-secreting tumour.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Lymphomas<\/h3>\n<p>Lymphomas are cancers of lymphocytes that mainly live in lymph nodes. Each type has a hallmark:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hodgkin lymphoma<\/b>: the <b>Reed-Sternberg cell<\/b>, a big cell with two mirror-image nuclei (\"owl's eyes\"), CD15 and CD30 positive.<\/li>\n<li><b>Burkitt lymphoma<\/b>: fast-growing, \"<b>starry sky<\/b>\" look under the microscope (pale macrophages among dark tumour cells), <b>t(8;14)<\/b> moving MYC.<\/li>\n<li><b>Follicular lymphoma<\/b>: <b>t(14;18)<\/b> switching on BCL2, a gene that stops cells from dying.<\/li>\n<li><b>Mantle cell lymphoma<\/b>: <b>t(11;14)<\/b>, cyclin D1, CD5 positive (like CLL, but CD23 negative).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Plasma cell disorders<\/h3>\n<p><b>Multiple myeloma<\/b> is a cancer of one plasma cell clone. Because one clone makes one antibody, you get a single sharp <b>monoclonal spike<\/b> on protein electrophoresis, often free light chains in the urine (<b>Bence Jones protein<\/b>), and <b>rouleaux<\/b> on the smear (excess protein makes cells stack). Diagnosis needs more than 10% plasma cells in the marrow plus organ damage, remembered as <b>CRAB<\/b>: high <b>C<\/b>alcium, <b>R<\/b>enal failure, <b>A<\/b>nemia, <b>B<\/b>one lesions.<\/p>\n<p><b>Waldenström macroglobulinemia<\/b> makes an <b>IgM<\/b> paraprotein. IgM is a big pentamer, so the blood becomes thick (<b>hyperviscosity<\/b>): headaches, blurred vision, bleeding.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Platelet-related emergencies (preview of the next lesson)<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>TTP<\/b>: missing <b>ADAMTS13<\/b>, the enzyme that trims vWF. Giant vWF strands grab platelets and form clots in small vessels, giving low platelets, schistocytes, fever, kidney and brain signs. PT and aPTT are normal.<\/li>\n<li><b>HUS<\/b>: usually children after <b>E. coli O157:H7<\/b> diarrhoea; the Shiga toxin damages kidney vessels.<\/li>\n<li><b>HIT<\/b>: antibodies form against heparin bound to platelet factor 4, activating platelets. The platelet count drops about <b>5-10 days<\/b> after heparin starts, and the danger is clotting, not bleeding. Stop heparin and give a direct thrombin inhibitor such as argatroban.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>WBC 60 × 10⁹/L, toxic granulation, Döhle bodies, high LAP, no basophilia. Leukemoid reaction or CML?<\/summary><p>Leukemoid reaction. A high LAP and toxic changes point to a reactive process; CML has a low LAP and basophilia.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Blasts are MPO negative, Sudan black negative and TdT positive. Which leukemia?<\/summary><p>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why does APL so often cause DIC?<\/summary><p>The abnormal promyelocytes' granules release procoagulant (tissue factor-like) material, which triggers clotting throughout the body.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is EPO low in polycythemia vera but high in a heavy smoker with polycythemia?<\/summary><p>In PV the JAK2 mutation drives red cell production without EPO, so the kidney turns EPO down. In smokers, carbon monoxide causes tissue hypoxia, so the kidney raises EPO.<\/p><\/details>"},"H5":{"min":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">When a blood vessel is cut, the body stops the bleeding in two stages. <b>Primary hemostasis<\/b> is the fast first response: platelets stick to the injury and form a soft plug within minutes. <b>Secondary hemostasis<\/b> (next lesson) then reinforces the plug with fibrin. This lesson is about the platelet plug, the proteins it depends on, and the classic inherited and acquired disorders.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The platelet plug in three steps<\/h3>\n<p>Think of it as patching a hole in a hose:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Adhesion: platelets stick to the wound.<\/b> Damage exposes collagen under the vessel lining. <b>Von Willebrand factor (vWF)<\/b> acts like double-sided tape: one side grabs the collagen, the other grabs the platelet's <b>GPIb<\/b> receptor. Without vWF or GPIb, platelets can't hold on, especially in fast-flowing blood.<\/li>\n<li><b>Activation: platelets change shape and send signals.<\/b> Stuck platelets turn spiky, release ADP and serotonin from their granules, and make <b>thromboxane A2<\/b>. These signals call more platelets in.<\/li>\n<li><b>Aggregation: platelets stick to each other.<\/b> Activated platelets switch on the <b>GPIIb/IIIa<\/b> receptor. <b>Fibrinogen<\/b> forms bridges between GPIIb/IIIa receptors on neighbouring platelets, building a plug.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: the receptors<\/b>GP <b>I<\/b>b is used <b>I<\/b>nitially, to adhere (with vWF). GP <b>II<\/b>b/IIIa is used <b>second<\/b>, to aggregate (with fibrinogen).<\/div>\n\n<h3>Bleeding pattern tells you the stage<\/h3>\n<p>Platelet problems and clotting-factor problems bleed differently, which is a common question:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Platelet / primary hemostasis problems<\/b>: bleeding from skin and mucous membranes. Expect petechiae (pinpoint red dots), easy bruising, nosebleeds, gum bleeding, heavy periods, and bleeding <i>immediately<\/i> after a cut.<\/li>\n<li><b>Clotting factor problems<\/b> (like hemophilia): deep bleeding into joints and muscles, and bleeding that starts again hours after surgery.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Testing the platelet plug<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Platelet count<\/b>: normal about 150-450 × 10⁹/L. Spontaneous bleeding becomes likely below about 10-20.<\/li>\n<li><b>PFA-100 closure time<\/b>: blood is pushed through a tiny hole coated with collagen plus ADP or epinephrine, and the test times how long platelets take to plug it. It is prolonged by aspirin, vWD and platelet defects. It has replaced the old bleeding time.<\/li>\n<li><b>Platelet aggregation studies<\/b>: agonists are added to platelet-rich plasma and the machine measures how much light gets through as platelets clump. Each agonist tests a different pathway (see below).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>The four classic disorders, and why each test pattern happens<\/h3>\n<p><b>Bernard-Soulier syndrome: missing GPIb.<\/b> Platelets can't bind vWF, so <b>ristocetin<\/b> (an antibiotic that forces vWF to bind GPIb) produces no clumping. Adding normal plasma doesn't help, because the defect is on the platelet, not in the plasma. Platelets are <b>giant<\/b> and the count is low.<\/p>\n<p><b>Glanzmann thrombasthenia: missing GPIIb/IIIa.<\/b> Platelets can stick but not link to each other. So they fail every agonist that needs aggregation (<b>ADP, collagen, epinephrine, arachidonic acid<\/b>), but <b>ristocetin is normal<\/b>, because ristocetin only tests the GPIb-vWF step. Platelet size and count are normal.<\/p>\n<p><b>Von Willebrand disease: low or faulty vWF.<\/b> The most common inherited bleeding disorder. Platelets are normal, but the \"tape\" is missing, so the ristocetin response and <b>ristocetin cofactor activity<\/b> are low. Unlike Bernard-Soulier, adding normal plasma (which contains vWF) <b>corrects<\/b> the defect. vWF also carries and protects <b>factor VIII<\/b>, so factor VIII may be low and the <b>aPTT may be long<\/b>.<\/p>\n<p><b>ITP (immune thrombocytopenia): antibodies destroy platelets.<\/b> The marrow responds by making more megakaryocytes and releasing young, <b>large platelets<\/b>. The count is low, but everything else (hemoglobin, WBC, clotting times) is normal. In children it often follows a viral illness and settles by itself.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: Bernard-Soulier vs vWD<\/b>Both fail ristocetin. Add normal plasma: if it <b>corrects<\/b>, the missing piece was in the plasma (vWF, so vWD). If it <b>doesn't correct<\/b>, the platelet is missing GPIb (Bernard-Soulier). Giant platelets also point to Bernard-Soulier.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why aspirin affects platelets for their whole life<\/b>Aspirin permanently blocks cyclooxygenase, so the platelet can't make thromboxane A2. Platelets have no nucleus and can't make new enzyme, so the effect lasts the platelet's lifespan (7-10 days). Aggregation to <b>arachidonic acid<\/b> is absent, and responses to collagen and epinephrine are weak.<\/div>\n\n<h3>A falsely low platelet count<\/h3>\n<p>Sometimes the analyzer reports very low platelets in a patient who isn't bleeding. A common cause is <b>EDTA-dependent antibodies<\/b> that make platelets clump in the purple tube, or stick around neutrophils (<b>platelet satellitism<\/b>). The analyzer counts each clump as one big cell or misses it. Always look at the smear: if you see clumps, recollect in a <b>sodium citrate<\/b> tube, recount, and multiply by about 1.1 to correct for the citrate dilution.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Platelets are normal in size and number. They fail to aggregate with ADP, collagen and epinephrine but respond normally to ristocetin. Diagnosis?<\/summary><p>Glanzmann thrombasthenia (missing GPIIb/IIIa).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Ristocetin aggregation is absent and corrects when normal plasma is added. Diagnosis?<\/summary><p>Von Willebrand disease. The normal plasma supplied the missing vWF.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A child has platelets of 12 × 10⁹/L with large platelets two weeks after a cold. Everything else is normal. What is most likely?<\/summary><p>Immune thrombocytopenia (ITP).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is bleeding from gums and skin typical of platelet disorders?<\/summary><p>Small vessels in skin and mucous membranes rely on the quick platelet plug to seal them. Without it, they ooze straight away, causing petechiae and mucosal bleeding.<\/p><\/details>"},"H6":{"min":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The platelet plug is soft. Coagulation (secondary hemostasis) reinforces it with a mesh of <b>fibrin<\/b>, like pouring concrete over a sandbag wall. It works as a cascade: each clotting factor switches on the next, until thrombin turns fibrinogen into fibrin. The lab tests in this lesson each check one part of that cascade, so if you understand the cascade, the test patterns follow logically.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The cascade as three roads to one destination<\/h3>\n<p>The traditional lab model has three parts:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Intrinsic pathway<\/b> (everything already in the blood): factors <b>XII, XI, IX, VIII<\/b>. Tested by the <b>aPTT<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Extrinsic pathway<\/b> (triggered by tissue factor from damaged tissue): factor <b>VII<\/b>. Tested by the <b>PT<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Common pathway<\/b> (where both roads join): factors <b>X, V, II (prothrombin) and I (fibrinogen)<\/b>. Tested by <b>both<\/b> PT and aPTT.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>At the end, <b>thrombin<\/b> turns fibrinogen into fibrin strands, and <b>factor XIII<\/b> cross-links them so the clot is strong.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hooks<\/b><b>PT<\/b> → \"<b>P<\/b>lay <b>T<\/b>ennis <b>Out<\/b>side\" (extrinsic, factor VII). <b>aPTT<\/b> → \"<b>P<\/b>lay <b>T<\/b>able <b>T<\/b>ennis <b>In<\/b>side\" (intrinsic). Intrinsic factors: \"<b>12, 11, 9, 8<\/b>\": the price that was $12 is now $11.98. Common pathway: \"<b>1 × 2 × 5 = 10<\/b>\" (factors I, II, V, X).<\/div>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>How the tests actually work<\/b>Blood is collected into citrate, which binds calcium so the sample can't clot in the tube. In the lab, the plasma gets an activator plus calcium back, and the machine times how long a clot takes. For the <b>PT<\/b>, the activator is tissue factor (thromboplastin), which starts the extrinsic road. For the <b>aPTT<\/b>, it is a contact activator (silica, kaolin) plus phospholipid, which starts the intrinsic road. A missing factor anywhere along a road slows that test.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Monitoring blood thinners<\/h3>\n<p><b>Warfarin<\/b> blocks vitamin K, which the liver needs to finish making factors <b>II, VII, IX and X<\/b> (and the natural anticoagulants proteins C and S). Factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 6 hours), so it falls first, and the <b>PT<\/b> is the test that tracks warfarin. Because labs use different reagents, the PT is converted to the <b>INR<\/b>: (patient PT ÷ mean normal PT)<sup>ISI<\/sup>. The ISI rates how sensitive the reagent is.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>PT 18 s, mean normal PT 12 s, ISI 1.2. INR = (18 ÷ 12)<sup>1.2<\/sup> = 1.5<sup>1.2<\/sup> ≈ <b>1.6<\/b>.<\/div>\n<p><b>Unfractionated heparin<\/b> boosts antithrombin, which mainly blocks thrombin and factor Xa, and it is tracked with the <b>aPTT<\/b> (or anti-Xa). <b>Low molecular weight heparin<\/b> acts mostly on Xa and barely changes the aPTT, so it needs an <b>anti-Xa<\/b> assay.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Reading PT and aPTT patterns<\/h3>\n<p>Ask which road is broken:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>PT long, aPTT normal<\/b> → only the extrinsic road (factor VII): factor VII deficiency, early warfarin, early liver disease or vitamin K deficiency.<\/li>\n<li><b>aPTT long, PT normal<\/b> → the intrinsic road: hemophilia A (VIII), hemophilia B (IX), factor XI or XII deficiency, heparin, vWD (low VIII), or lupus anticoagulant.<\/li>\n<li><b>Both long<\/b> → the common road or many factors at once: deficiency of X, V, II or fibrinogen, DIC, severe liver disease, a big warfarin or heparin overdose.<\/li>\n<li><b>Both normal but the patient bleeds<\/b> → something the tests don't see: <b>factor XIII deficiency<\/b> (the clot forms but isn't cross-linked; screen with the clot solubility test in 5 M urea), a platelet function problem, or mild vWD.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: factor XII<\/b>Factor XII deficiency gives a very long aPTT but <b>no bleeding<\/b>. The body doesn't actually need factor XII to clot; it only matters in the test tube.<\/div>\n\n<h3>The mixing study: missing factor or inhibitor?<\/h3>\n<p>When a clotting time is long, mix the patient's plasma 1:1 with normal plasma and repeat:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>It corrects<\/b> → a <b>factor deficiency<\/b>. Normal plasma supplies at least 50% of every factor, which is enough to give a normal time.<\/li>\n<li><b>It doesn't correct<\/b> → an <b>inhibitor<\/b> is present and is also blocking the normal plasma. This could be <b>lupus anticoagulant<\/b> or an antibody against a factor (usually factor VIII). Factor VIII inhibitors are time-dependent, so they may correct at first and then prolong after incubating 1-2 hours at 37 °C.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Lupus anticoagulant<\/b> is an antibody against phospholipid. In the test tube it steals the phospholipid the test needs, so the aPTT is long. In the body it causes <b>clots<\/b> (and miscarriages), not bleeding. Adding extra phospholipid overcomes it; confirm with the <b>dRVVT<\/b> (dilute Russell viper venom time).<\/p>\n<p>Inhibitor strength is measured with the <b>Bethesda assay<\/b>, reported in Bethesda units.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Thrombin time and reptilase time<\/h3>\n<p>The <b>thrombin time<\/b> adds thrombin directly and times the conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin. It only checks the last step, so it is long when fibrinogen is low or abnormal, or when <b>heparin<\/b> is present (heparin blocks thrombin). <b>Reptilase<\/b> is a snake enzyme that also clots fibrinogen, but heparin can't block it. So a long thrombin time with a normal reptilase time means <b>heparin<\/b>; both long means a <b>fibrinogen<\/b> problem.<\/p>\n\n<h3>DIC and fibrin breakdown<\/h3>\n<p>Once a clot has done its job, <b>plasmin<\/b> breaks it down. The pieces are measured two ways:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>FDPs<\/b> (fibrin/fibrinogen degradation products) come from breaking down <i>either<\/i> fibrin or fibrinogen.<\/li>\n<li><b>D-dimer<\/b> comes only from <b>cross-linked fibrin<\/b>, so it proves a real clot was made and then broken down.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>In <b>DIC<\/b> (disseminated intravascular coagulation), something such as sepsis, trauma, an obstetric emergency or APL switches on clotting all over the body. Tiny clots use up platelets and factors, then get broken down. The pattern is <b>long PT and aPTT, low fibrinogen, low platelets, high D-dimer and FDPs, schistocytes<\/b>. The patient can bleed and clot at the same time.<\/p>\n<p>In the rare <b>primary fibrinolysis<\/b>, plasmin attacks fibrinogen directly without a clot forming. FDPs are high, but <b>D-dimer is normal<\/b> (no cross-linked fibrin) and platelets are normal.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Specimen rules that change results<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use a <b>3.2% sodium citrate<\/b> (light blue) tube, filled to the line for the 9:1 blood-to-citrate ratio. Underfilling means too much citrate, which binds the test calcium and <b>falsely prolongs<\/b> the times.<\/li>\n<li>If the <b>hematocrit is above 55%<\/b>, there is less plasma in the tube, so the citrate is too concentrated. Reduce the citrate volume using the lab's formula.<\/li>\n<li>Timing: PT is stable up to 24 hours at room temperature; aPTT within 4 hours, and within 1 hour (centrifuged) for heparin patients, because platelets release PF4, which neutralises heparin.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>More tests you should recognise<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Fibrinogen (Clauss method)<\/b>: low in DIC and liver disease.<\/li>\n<li><b>Factor assays<\/b>: patient plasma is mixed with plasma lacking one factor. How well it corrects gives that factor's activity as a percentage.<\/li>\n<li><b>Thromboelastography (TEG)<\/b>: whole-blood test of how fast a clot forms, how strong it is and how fast it breaks down. Used in surgery and trauma.<\/li>\n<li><b>Heparin neutralisation<\/b>: protamine, polybrene or heparinase removes heparin so other tests can be read.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Thrombophilia: too much clotting<\/h3>\n<p>The body has brakes on clotting: <b>antithrombin<\/b>, <b>protein C<\/b> and <b>protein S<\/b>. Clots form too easily when a brake fails or a factor resists the brake:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Factor V Leiden<\/b> (the most common inherited cause): factor V resists being switched off by activated protein C (\"<b>APC resistance<\/b>\").<\/li>\n<li><b>Prothrombin G20210A<\/b>: too much prothrombin.<\/li>\n<li><b>Protein C, protein S or antithrombin deficiency.<\/b> Antithrombin deficiency causes <b>heparin resistance<\/b>, because heparin works through antithrombin.<\/li>\n<li>Antiphospholipid antibodies and high homocysteine.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: testing on warfarin<\/b>Proteins C and S are vitamin K-dependent, so they fall on warfarin. Testing them during warfarin therapy can falsely suggest a deficiency.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>PT normal, aPTT long, corrects on mixing, boy with joint bleeds. Which factors are likely?<\/summary><p>Factor VIII (hemophilia A) or factor IX (hemophilia B). Both are intrinsic, X-linked, and cause joint bleeding.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Thrombin time long, reptilase normal. Why?<\/summary><p>Heparin. It blocks thrombin but not reptilase.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is D-dimer more specific than FDPs for a real clot?<\/summary><p>D-dimer comes only from cross-linked fibrin, which exists only after factor XIII has stabilised a real clot. FDPs also come from fibrinogen breakdown.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient on LMWH has a normal aPTT. Is the drug not working?<\/summary><p>Not necessarily. LMWH mainly inhibits Xa and barely affects the aPTT. Use an anti-Xa assay to monitor it.<\/p><\/details>"},"H7":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Analyzers are fast and accurate, but they can be fooled. A big part of a lab scientist's job is spotting when a number <i>can't<\/i> be right and knowing how to fix it. This lesson explains the common CBC errors, then the other hematology tests and markers you will meet.<\/p>\n\n<h3>How an analyzer counts cells<\/h3>\n<p>Most analyzers use the <b>Coulter (impedance) principle<\/b>. Cells are suspended in a salt solution that conducts electricity and pulled one at a time through a tiny opening with an electric current across it. A cell doesn't conduct well, so each one causes a brief rise in resistance: a pulse. The <b>number of pulses = the cell count<\/b> and the <b>size of each pulse = the cell's volume<\/b>. That is how the MCV is measured directly.<\/p>\n<p>Modern analyzers add <b>light scatter<\/b> (lasers) and sometimes fluorescent dyes to sort white cells into types. Results come with <b>histograms<\/b> (graphs of cell sizes) and <b>flags<\/b> that warn you to look at a smear.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>RDW<\/b> (red cell distribution width) measures how much red cells vary in size. It rises early in iron deficiency, because new small cells mix with old normal ones. It is usually normal in thalassemia trait, where all the cells are evenly small.<\/li>\n<li><b>MPV<\/b> (mean platelet volume) measures platelet size. Large platelets suggest the marrow is pushing out young ones (as in ITP).<\/li>\n<li><b>Hemoglobin<\/b> is measured by breaking open the red cells and reading the color (cyanmethemoglobin at 540 nm, or a cyanide-free SLS method). <b>Hematocrit<\/b> is calculated: RBC × MCV.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>The four classic CBC errors and how to fix them<\/h3>\n<p><b>1. Cold agglutinins.<\/b> Some patients have IgM antibodies that make red cells clump at room temperature. The analyzer sees each clump as one huge cell. So the <b>RBC count is falsely low<\/b> and the <b>MCV falsely high<\/b>. The hemoglobin is still right (it is measured after lysing all cells), so the calculated values go wrong: the Hct (RBC × MCV) is falsely low and the <b>MCHC is falsely very high<\/b> (often over 37). <b>Fix:<\/b> warm the sample to 37 °C for about 15-30 minutes and rerun.<\/p>\n<p><b>2. Lipemia, very high WBC, or icterus.<\/b> The hemoglobin method reads color through the liquid. Anything that makes the liquid cloudy (fat, huge numbers of white cells) scatters light, and the machine reads it as extra hemoglobin. So <b>Hgb, MCH and MCHC are falsely high<\/b>. <b>Fix:<\/b> replace the plasma with an equal volume of saline (or use a plasma blank) and rerun the hemoglobin.<\/p>\n<p><b>3. Platelet clumping.<\/b> EDTA can trigger antibodies that clump platelets. Clumps aren't counted as platelets, so the <b>platelet count is falsely low<\/b>. Big clumps can be counted as white cells, so the <b>WBC may be falsely high<\/b>. <b>Fix:<\/b> check the smear for clumps, then recollect in sodium citrate.<\/p>\n<p><b>4. Microclots.<\/b> A poorly mixed or slow draw lets small clots form, which trap cells (especially platelets) and can block the analyzer. Counts come out low. <b>Fix:<\/b> recollect. A clotted EDTA sample can't be rescued.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: the warning sign<\/b>An <b>MCHC above about 36-37<\/b> is a red flag. Ask: cold agglutinins? Lipemia or high WBC? Spherocytes? Then use the rule of three (Hgb × 3 ≈ Hct) to check your fix worked.<\/div>\n\n<h3>The ESR (erythrocyte sedimentation rate)<\/h3>\n<p>The ESR measures how far red cells fall in a tall tube in one hour. Normally red cells repel each other (they carry a negative surface charge) and fall slowly. In inflammation, proteins like fibrinogen and immunoglobulins cancel that charge, the cells stack into <b>rouleaux<\/b>, and heavy stacks sink fast. So <b>inflammation and rouleaux raise the ESR<\/b>.<\/p>\n<p>Things that <b>lower<\/b> the ESR are cells that can't stack (sickle cells, spherocytes, acanthocytes) and too many cells crowding the tube (polycythemia). Technical errors matter: a <b>tilted tube<\/b> or a warm room makes it falsely <b>high<\/b>; vibration disturbs the result; an old sample (over 2 hours at room temperature) gives a falsely <b>low<\/b> result.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Other special tests<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>G6PD screen<\/b> (fluorescent spot test): normal G6PD makes NADPH, which glows under UV light. No glow = deficiency. It can be <b>falsely normal right after a hemolytic crisis<\/b>, because the old, most deficient cells are already gone and young cells have more enzyme.<\/li>\n<li><b>Osmotic fragility<\/b>: red cells are put into salt solutions that get more and more dilute. Water rushes in and cells swell. Spherocytes have no spare membrane, so they burst sooner: <b>increased fragility in hereditary spherocytosis<\/b>. Target cells (extra membrane) show decreased fragility.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Flow cytometry markers: the cell's ID badge<\/h3>\n<p>Every cell type carries its own set of surface proteins, numbered as \"CD\" (cluster of differentiation) markers. Flow cytometry tags them with fluorescent antibodies and counts them cell by cell. Learn them in groups:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Stem cells:<\/b> CD34.<\/li>\n<li><b>T cells:<\/b> CD3 (all T cells), CD4 (helper), CD8 (cytotoxic).<\/li>\n<li><b>B cells:<\/b> CD19, CD20. <b>CLL<\/b> adds CD5 and CD23.<\/li>\n<li><b>Myeloid cells:<\/b> CD13, CD33, CD117.<\/li>\n<li><b>Monocytes:<\/b> CD14, CD64.<\/li>\n<li><b>Megakaryocytes/platelets:<\/b> CD41, CD61.<\/li>\n<li><b>PNH:<\/b> <i>loss<\/i> of CD55 and CD59.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Flow cytometry also reads two light signals: <b>forward scatter<\/b> tracks cell <b>size<\/b>, and <b>side scatter<\/b> tracks <b>granularity<\/b> (neutrophils, full of granules, scatter the most).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Molecular tests in hematology<\/h3>\n<p>Some blood cancers are defined by a single genetic change, and finding it confirms the diagnosis and guides treatment:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>BCR-ABL1<\/b> (from t(9;22)) = CML. Measured over time by quantitative PCR to check the response to treatment.<\/li>\n<li><b>JAK2 V617F<\/b> = polycythemia vera (in about 95%), and about half of essential thrombocythemia and myelofibrosis.<\/li>\n<li><b>PML-RARA<\/b> (from t(15;17)) = APL.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>RBC 2.0, MCV 130, MCHC 40, clumps on the smear. What do you do?<\/summary><p>Suspect cold agglutinins. Warm the sample to 37 °C and rerun. The RBC and MCV should return to sensible values and the MCHC should fall into range.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why does extreme leukocytosis falsely raise hemoglobin?<\/summary><p>The huge number of white cells makes the lysed sample cloudy. Cloudiness blocks light, which the analyzer reads as extra hemoglobin.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Name two things that falsely raise the ESR and one that falsely lowers it.<\/summary><p>Raise: tilted tube, warm room. Lower: a sample too old (over 2 hours), sickle cells or spherocytes.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which markers would you expect on CLL cells?<\/summary><p>B-cell markers CD19 and CD20 (often dim), plus CD5 and CD23.<\/p><\/details>"},"H8":{"min":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Exam traps are questions written so that a half-remembered fact leads to the wrong answer. They usually test whether you know the <i>reason<\/i> behind a fact, not just the fact. Below, each trap is explained, followed by more hematology traps that come up often.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 1: \"Target cells mean HbC\"<\/h3>\n<p>Target cells are typical of HbC, but they aren't specific to it. A target cell forms whenever there is <b>too much membrane for the amount of hemoglobin<\/b>. That happens when hemoglobin is low (thalassemia, iron deficiency), when membrane cholesterol is high (liver disease), and when the spleen isn't there to trim extra membrane (after splenectomy). Read the rest of the question: microcytosis with normal iron points to thalassemia; jaundice points to liver disease.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 2: \"I'll find Heinz bodies on the Wright stain\"<\/h3>\n<p>You won't. Heinz bodies are clumps of denatured hemoglobin. Wright stain colors hemoglobin pink, so a pink clump inside a pink cell is invisible. You need a <b>supravital stain<\/b> (crystal violet or new methylene blue) on <i>living<\/i> cells. On a Wright smear you only see the clues they leave behind: <b>bite cells<\/b> and <b>blister cells<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 3: spherocytes always mean the same thing<\/h3>\n<p>Spherocytes appear in both autoimmune hemolytic anemia and hereditary spherocytosis, and the smear can look identical. The <b>DAT<\/b> separates them: <b>positive<\/b> = antibody on the cells (autoimmune); <b>negative<\/b> = an inherited membrane defect. Burns and some toxins also make spherocytes, so always use the history.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 4: a long aPTT always means a factor deficiency<\/h3>\n<p>If the patient is on <b>heparin<\/b>, a long aPTT is the expected drug effect. Calling it a factor deficiency, or adding factor tests, is a mistake. Before you interpret a long aPTT, check the medications and how the sample was drawn (a sample drawn from a heparinized line is a classic error). A long thrombin time with a normal reptilase time confirms heparin.<\/p>\n\n<h3>More traps worth knowing<\/h3>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Reticulocytes without correction<\/b>A retic count of 6% in a patient with Hct 20% is less impressive than it looks. Correct it: 6 × 20 ÷ 45 ≈ 2.7%. Always correct before deciding the marrow is \"responding well\".<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Pelger-Huët cells counted as bands<\/b>Bilobed Pelger-Huët neutrophils look like bands or metamyelocytes. Counting them as bands creates a false left shift. If every neutrophil has two round lobes and the patient is well, think Pelger-Huët.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Nucleated red cells inflating the WBC<\/b>Analyzers may count nRBCs as white cells. If the smear shows many nRBCs, correct the WBC: WBC × 100 ÷ (100 + nRBCs).<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Auer rods rule out ALL<\/b>Auer rods appear only in myeloid blasts. A blast with an Auer rod is myeloid, whatever else the question says.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Sickle screen in infants<\/b>A negative sickle solubility test in a baby under 6 months doesn't rule out sickle cell disease, because HbF is still high. Use electrophoresis or HPLC.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Factor XII and bleeding<\/b>Factor XII deficiency gives a very long aPTT but no bleeding. Don't pick it as the cause of a bleeding patient's problem.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Lupus anticoagulant<\/b>Despite the name, it causes clotting, not bleeding. In the test tube it prolongs the aPTT and doesn't correct on mixing.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Underfilled citrate tube<\/b>An underfilled light-blue tube gives falsely long PT and aPTT. The fix is to recollect, not to report the result with a comment.<\/div>\n\n<h3>How to approach a trap question<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the whole case before the answers. Traps usually hide one detail that changes everything (a drug, an age, a temperature).<\/li>\n<li>Ask \"why would this happen?\" If your answer depends on a mechanism, check it fits every clue.<\/li>\n<li>Check for specimen problems first: a wrong tube, a delay, clots, lipemia or hemolysis.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A smear shows bite cells after the patient took a sulfa drug, but no Heinz bodies are seen. Is G6PD deficiency ruled out?<\/summary><p>No. Heinz bodies don't show on Wright stain. Use a supravital stain, and do a G6PD assay (ideally a few months after the episode).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Many target cells, MCV 68, normal ferritin, no jaundice. HbC or thalassemia?<\/summary><p>Thalassemia is more likely: microcytosis with normal iron. Confirm with hemoglobin electrophoresis or HPLC.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A heparinized patient's aPTT is 85 s. The doctor asks for a factor VIII assay. What should you check first?<\/summary><p>The heparin effect. The long aPTT is expected on heparin. Neutralise the heparin (or use a thrombin/reptilase time to confirm it) before interpreting factor results.<\/p><\/details>"},"I1":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The immune system is the body's defence force, with two branches. The <b>innate<\/b> branch is the fast, general-purpose first responder. The <b>adaptive<\/b> branch is slower but precise, and it remembers. Almost every immunology lab test detects some part of that defence: antibodies, cells, complement or cytokines. This lesson gives you the map.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The 8 big ideas, explained<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Each antibody class has a job.<\/b> IgM comes first and fixes complement best; IgG is the most abundant and crosses the placenta; IgA guards mucous membranes; IgE drives allergy.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cell markers are ID badges:<\/b> T cells CD3; helper T CD4 (HIV's target); cytotoxic T CD8; B cells CD19/CD20; NK cells CD16/CD56.<\/li>\n<li><b>Complement<\/b>: C1q starts the classical pathway; C5b-9 punches holes; C3b tags microbes for eating; C5a calls in white cells.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hypersensitivity, \"ACID\"<\/b>: Anaphylactic (I), Cytotoxic (II), Immune complex (III), Delayed (IV).<\/li>\n<li><b>ANA screens for lupus<\/b>; anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith confirm it.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hepatitis B<\/b>: HBsAg = infected now; anti-HBs = immune; anti-HBc IgM = acute infection or window period.<\/li>\n<li><b>Syphilis<\/b>: RPR/VDRL screen; FTA-ABS/TP-PA confirm. Very high antibody can cause a prozone false negative.<\/li>\n<li><b>Sensitivity<\/b> catches true disease (TP ÷ (TP + FN)); <b>specificity<\/b> clears true non-disease (TN ÷ (TN + FP)).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Innate vs adaptive immunity<\/h3>\n<p><b>Innate immunity<\/b> works within minutes and treats every invader much the same: skin and mucous barriers, <b>neutrophils<\/b> and <b>macrophages<\/b> (which eat microbes), <b>NK cells<\/b> (which kill infected or cancerous cells without prior exposure) and <b>complement<\/b>. Eating is much easier when the microbe is tagged with an <b>opsonin<\/b> (\"sauce\" that makes it tasty), mainly <b>IgG<\/b> and <b>C3b<\/b>. Neutrophils kill using a \"respiratory burst\" of oxygen radicals, which the <b>NBT<\/b> (or DHR) test checks.<\/p>\n<p><b>Adaptive immunity<\/b> takes days the first time but is precisely targeted, and it creates <b>memory<\/b>. <b>B cells<\/b> make antibodies (humoral immunity); <b>T cells<\/b> kill infected cells and coordinate the response (cell-mediated immunity).<\/p>\n<p><b>Antigen vocabulary<\/b>: an <b>epitope<\/b> is the small part of an antigen that an antibody actually binds. A <b>hapten<\/b> is a molecule too small to trigger a response alone, but immunogenic when attached to a larger carrier protein (penicillin binding to body proteins causes penicillin allergy this way).<\/p>\n\n<h3>The acute-phase response<\/h3>\n<p>When there is infection or injury, cytokines (especially <b>IL-6<\/b>) tell the liver to change what it makes. It raises <b>acute-phase proteins<\/b>: <b>CRP<\/b> (rises in 4-6 hours, peaks at 36-50 hours, falls fast once inflammation ends), <b>fibrinogen<\/b>, <b>haptoglobin<\/b>, <b>ferritin<\/b> and others. It makes less <b>albumin<\/b> (\"negative\" acute-phase protein). <b>High-sensitivity CRP<\/b> measures very low levels to estimate heart disease risk.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Antibodies: structure and the five classes<\/h3>\n<p>An antibody is shaped like a <b>Y<\/b>, made of <b>two heavy chains and two light chains<\/b> (kappa or lambda) held together by disulfide bonds.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The two arms are the <b>Fab<\/b> parts (fragment antigen-binding); their tips bind antigen.<\/li>\n<li>The stem is the <b>Fc<\/b> part (fragment crystallisable). It \"talks\" to the rest of the immune system: it binds complement and the Fc receptors on phagocytes.<\/li>\n<li>The enzyme <b>papain<\/b> cuts above the hinge, giving <b>2 Fab + 1 Fc<\/b>. <b>Pepsin<\/b> cuts below it, giving one joined <b>F(ab')2<\/b> (and the Fc is broken up).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The heavy chain type sets the class:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>IgG<\/b>: about 75% of serum antibody; the main antibody of the <b>secondary (memory) response<\/b>; the only class that <b>crosses the placenta<\/b> (protecting newborns, but also causing HDFN). IgG1 and IgG3 fix complement best.<\/li>\n<li><b>IgM<\/b>: the <b>first<\/b> antibody made in a new infection; a <b>pentamer<\/b> (five Y's joined by a J chain) with 10 binding sites, so it is the best at <b>agglutinating<\/b> and <b>fixing complement<\/b>. It doesn't cross the placenta, so <b>IgM in a newborn<\/b> means the baby itself is infected (a TORCH infection).<\/li>\n<li><b>IgA<\/b>: in secretions (saliva, tears, breast milk, gut) as a <b>dimer<\/b> with a J chain and a <b>secretory piece<\/b> that protects it from digestion.<\/li>\n<li><b>IgE<\/b>: the <b>lowest<\/b> concentration. It sits on <b>mast cells and basophils<\/b>; when allergen cross-links it, they release histamine (allergy). Also fights parasites.<\/li>\n<li><b>IgD<\/b>: mainly a <b>receptor on the surface of B cells<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b>\"<b>M<\/b>\"akes it <b>first<\/b> (IgM); \"<b>G<\/b>\"ets through the placenta (IgG); \"<b>A<\/b>\" is at the <b>A<\/b>ir-and-gut doors (IgA); \"<b>E<\/b>\" is for <b>E<\/b>xplosive allergy (IgE).<\/div>\n<p><b>Primary vs secondary response<\/b>: the first exposure gives a slow, small response, mostly <b>IgM<\/b>. A later exposure triggers memory cells, giving a fast, large, long-lasting response, mostly <b>IgG<\/b>. That is why vaccines boost, and why IgM suggests a <i>recent<\/i> infection while IgG alone suggests a past one or immunity.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Immune cells and their markers<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>T cells<\/b> all carry <b>CD3<\/b>.\n <ul>\n <li><b>Helper T cells: CD4<\/b>. They recognise antigen shown on <b>HLA class II<\/b> (on antigen-presenting cells) and direct the response. HIV infects and destroys them.<\/li>\n <li><b>Cytotoxic T cells: CD8<\/b>. They recognise antigen on <b>HLA class I<\/b> (on all nucleated cells) and kill infected or cancerous cells.<\/li>\n <li><b>Regulatory T cells<\/b>: CD4, CD25, FOXP3. They calm the response and prevent autoimmunity.<\/li>\n <\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><b>B cells<\/b>: <b>CD19, CD20, CD21<\/b>. They mature into <b>plasma cells<\/b>, which pour out antibody.<\/li>\n<li><b>NK cells<\/b>: <b>CD16, CD56<\/b>. They kill cells missing normal HLA class I (a sign of viral infection or cancer).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: CD4 and CD8 with class I and II<\/b>\"Rule of 8\": <b>CD4 × class II = 8<\/b>, and <b>CD8 × class I = 8<\/b>.<\/div>\n<p><b>MHC (HLA)<\/b>: <b>class I<\/b> (HLA-A, B, C) is on <b>all nucleated cells<\/b> (and platelets). <b>Class II<\/b> (HLA-DR, DQ, DP) is only on <b>antigen-presenting cells<\/b>: macrophages, dendritic cells and B cells.<\/p>\n<p><b>Flow cytometry<\/b> counts these cells: <b>forward scatter<\/b> reflects size, <b>side scatter<\/b> reflects granularity, and fluorescent antibodies identify CD markers. Uses include the <b>CD4 count<\/b> in HIV and <b>CD34<\/b> counts of stem cells for transplant.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Cytokines: the immune system's messages<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>IL-1 and TNF-alpha<\/b>: fever and inflammation.<\/li>\n<li><b>IL-2<\/b>: makes T cells grow.<\/li>\n<li><b>IL-4 and IL-5<\/b>: drive B cells to make IgE, and grow eosinophils (allergy).<\/li>\n<li><b>IL-6<\/b>: the acute-phase response (CRP).<\/li>\n<li><b>IL-8<\/b>: calls neutrophils to the site.<\/li>\n<li><b>Interferon-gamma<\/b>: activates macrophages. TB-infected T cells release it when they see TB antigens, which is the basis of the <b>IGRA (QuantiFERON)<\/b> blood test.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Cytokines are measured by ELISA, multiplex bead assays or flow cytometry.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A newborn's cord blood IgM is high. What does this suggest?<\/summary><p>An infection acquired in the womb (TORCH). IgM can't cross the placenta, so it was made by the baby.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which enzyme digestion produces two Fab fragments and one Fc?<\/summary><p>Papain. (Pepsin produces F(ab')2.)<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>CD8 T cells recognise antigen on which HLA class?<\/summary><p>HLA class I, found on all nucleated cells.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which cytokine drives CRP production?<\/summary><p>IL-6.<\/p><\/details>"},"I2":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">This lesson covers three ways the immune system's machinery shows up in disease. <b>Complement<\/b> is a protein cascade that punches holes in invaders. <b>Hypersensitivity<\/b> is the immune system overreacting and harming the body. <b>Immunodeficiency<\/b> is the immune system missing a part. Each disease follows logically from which part is affected.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Complement: a cascade like falling dominoes<\/h3>\n<p>Complement is a set of over 30 plasma proteins that switch each other on in sequence. Three different \"start buttons\" all lead to the central step, the splitting of <b>C3<\/b>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Classical pathway<\/b>: started by <b>antibody bound to antigen<\/b> (IgM, or IgG1/IgG3). <b>C1q<\/b> grabs the antibody's Fc region, then <b>C4 and C2<\/b> are cleaved to form the C3 convertase <b>C4b2a<\/b>. This is how antibodies kill.<\/li>\n<li><b>Alternative pathway<\/b>: started directly by <b>microbial surfaces<\/b> (such as bacterial endotoxin), without antibody. Uses <b>factor B, factor D and properdin<\/b>; convertase <b>C3bBb<\/b>. It provides fast protection before antibodies exist.<\/li>\n<li><b>Lectin pathway<\/b>: <b>mannose-binding lectin (MBL)<\/b> recognises sugars on microbes and then uses C4 and C2 like the classical pathway.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>All three end the same way: C5 is split, and <b>C5b, C6, C7, C8 and C9<\/b> assemble into the <b>membrane attack complex (MAC)<\/b>, a pore that lets water rush into the cell and burst it.<\/p>\n<p>The <b>leftover pieces<\/b> have jobs too:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>C3b<\/b>: the main <b>opsonin<\/b>, a tag that helps phagocytes grab the microbe.<\/li>\n<li><b>C3a, C4a, C5a<\/b>: <b>anaphylatoxins<\/b> that make mast cells release histamine (swelling, leaky vessels).<\/li>\n<li><b>C5a<\/b>: a strong <b>chemotaxin<\/b> that calls neutrophils to the site.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Reading complement levels<\/h3>\n<p>When a pathway is switched on, its components are <b>used up<\/b>, so their levels fall. Which components are low tells you which pathway is active:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Low C3 and low C4<\/b> → the <b>classical<\/b> pathway is being used up by immune complexes: <b>SLE<\/b>, serum sickness, some glomerulonephritis.<\/li>\n<li><b>Low C3, normal C4<\/b> → the <b>alternative<\/b> pathway (C4 isn't part of it): for example <b>post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Low C4 with C1 inhibitor deficiency<\/b> → <b>hereditary angioedema<\/b>. C1 inhibitor normally keeps C1 in check. Without it, C1 constantly cleaves C4 (so C4 is low even between attacks), and bradykinin builds up, causing sudden swelling of the face, throat or gut. C3 is normal.<\/li>\n<li><b>Missing C5-C9<\/b> (the MAC) → repeated <b>Neisseria<\/b> infections, because these bacteria are mainly killed by the MAC.<\/li>\n<li><b>Missing C3<\/b> → severe repeated infections with encapsulated bacteria (no opsonisation).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>CH50<\/b> tests the <b>whole classical pathway<\/b> in one go: it measures how well the patient's serum lyses antibody-coated sheep red cells. A very low CH50 means a component is missing.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: complement is fragile<\/b>Complement breaks down at room temperature and is <b>destroyed by heating serum to 56 °C for 30 minutes<\/b>, which is how labs \"inactivate\" serum on purpose. Samples for complement testing must be fresh or frozen promptly.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Hypersensitivity: four ways to overreact (ACID)<\/h3>\n<p><b>Type I, Anaphylactic (immediate)<\/b>: minutes after exposure. <b>IgE<\/b> sits on mast cells; allergen cross-links it, and the mast cell releases histamine and other mediators. Examples: hay fever, asthma, food allergy, bee-sting anaphylaxis. Tests: total IgE and <b>allergen-specific IgE<\/b> (ImmunoCAP), skin-prick tests.<\/p>\n<p><b>Type II, Cytotoxic<\/b>: <b>IgG or IgM against antigens on a cell or tissue<\/b>, often with complement. The body attacks its own cells or transfused cells. Examples: <b>transfusion reactions<\/b>, <b>HDFN<\/b>, <b>autoimmune hemolytic anemia<\/b>, <b>Goodpasture<\/b> (anti-GBM), and antibodies that block or stimulate receptors such as <b>myasthenia gravis<\/b> and Graves disease.<\/p>\n<p><b>Type III, Immune complex<\/b>: antibody and free (soluble) antigen form <b>complexes<\/b> that circulate and get stuck in small vessels (kidney, joints, skin), where they activate complement and cause inflammation. Examples: <b>SLE<\/b>, <b>serum sickness<\/b>, the <b>Arthus reaction<\/b>, post-strep <b>glomerulonephritis<\/b>. Complement C3 and C4 are often low.<\/p>\n<p><b>Type IV, Delayed<\/b>: <b>T cells, not antibody<\/b>, so it takes <b>48-72 hours<\/b> to develop. Examples: the <b>TB skin test<\/b>, <b>contact dermatitis<\/b> (poison ivy, nickel), and graft rejection.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: type II vs type III<\/b>Type <b>II<\/b>: antibody hits a <b>fixed target<\/b> (a cell). Type <b>III<\/b>: antibody and antigen meet <b>in the blood<\/b> and the complexes land somewhere else.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Immunodeficiency: what's missing tells you the infections<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>DiGeorge syndrome<\/b>: the <b>thymus<\/b> (and parathyroids) don't develop, so there are few <b>T cells<\/b> and low calcium (tetany). Heart defects are common. Infections: viruses and fungi.<\/li>\n<li><b>Bruton (X-linked) agammaglobulinemia<\/b>: boys with <b>no mature B cells<\/b> and very <b>low immunoglobulins<\/b>. Infections start at about <b>6 months<\/b>, when the mother's IgG runs out: repeated bacterial infections.<\/li>\n<li><b>SCID<\/b> (severe combined immunodeficiency): <b>both T and B cells<\/b> missing. Severe infections of every kind in infancy (\"bubble boy\"). Patients must receive <b>irradiated<\/b> blood.<\/li>\n<li><b>Selective IgA deficiency<\/b>: the <b>most common<\/b> immunodeficiency; often no symptoms or mild sinus and gut infections. Danger: some make <b>anti-IgA<\/b> and can have <b>anaphylaxis<\/b> from normal blood products. It can also give <b>false-negative IgA-based tests<\/b> (like the IgA anti-tTG test for celiac disease).<\/li>\n<li><b>Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD)<\/b>: neutrophils can eat bacteria but <b>can't kill them<\/b>, because the respiratory burst enzyme (NADPH oxidase) is missing. The <b>NBT test is negative<\/b> (no color change) and the DHR test shows no burst. Infections with catalase-positive organisms (Staph, Aspergillus, Serratia).<\/li>\n<li><b>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome<\/b>: the triad <b>eczema, low (small) platelets, and infections<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>HIV<\/b>: acquired; destroys <b>CD4 T cells<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>HIV testing today<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Screen<\/b> with a <b>4th-generation antigen/antibody combination immunoassay<\/b>. It detects HIV antibodies <i>and<\/i> the <b>p24 antigen<\/b>, which appears earlier, shortening the window period.<\/li>\n<li>If reactive, run an <b>HIV-1/HIV-2 antibody differentiation immunoassay<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>If that is negative or unclear, test for <b>HIV-1 RNA (NAT)<\/b>. A positive NAT with a negative differentiation assay means <b>acute (early) HIV infection<\/b>: antigen and RNA appear before antibodies.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The <b>Western blot<\/b> is no longer used as the first confirmation step. <b>Viral load<\/b> (RT-PCR) and <b>CD4 count<\/b> monitor treatment. A CD4 count below <b>200/µL<\/b> defines AIDS.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Low C3 and normal C4 after a strep throat infection. Which pathway is active?<\/summary><p>The alternative pathway (C4 is used only by the classical and lectin pathways).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient has repeated Neisseria meningitidis infections. Which complement deficiency should be checked?<\/summary><p>Terminal components C5-C9 (the membrane attack complex).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A positive TB skin test read at 48-72 hours is which type of hypersensitivity?<\/summary><p>Type IV (delayed, T-cell mediated).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A boy's infections begin at 6 months, and he has no B cells. Diagnosis and why the timing?<\/summary><p>Bruton X-linked agammaglobulinemia. Maternal IgG protected him until it was used up at about 6 months.<\/p><\/details>"},"I3":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Autoimmune disease happens when the immune system attacks the body's own tissues. Each disease tends to make antibodies against a particular target, so those <b>autoantibodies<\/b> work as fingerprints. This lesson explains how to read them, how the ANA screen works, and how to sort out the plasma cell disorders that produce a monoclonal protein.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Screening vs specific antibodies<\/h3>\n<p>Autoimmune testing usually goes in two steps:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>A <b>sensitive screen<\/b> that rarely misses disease but isn't specific. The classic example is the <b>ANA<\/b>: positive in almost all SLE, but also in other autoimmune diseases, with some drugs, and in about 5% of healthy people (more in older adults).<\/li>\n<li>A <b>specific test<\/b> that confirms the diagnosis, such as <b>anti-dsDNA or anti-Smith<\/b> for SLE.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>So a <b>negative ANA makes SLE very unlikely<\/b>, but a positive ANA alone doesn't prove it.<\/p>\n\n<h3>How the ANA test works and what the patterns mean<\/h3>\n<p>The ANA is done by <b>indirect immunofluorescence<\/b> on <b>HEp-2 cells<\/b> (human tumour cells with large nuclei). The patient's serum is put on the cells; any antibody against nuclear parts sticks, and a fluorescent anti-human antibody makes it glow. The result has a <b>titer<\/b> (positive at 1:40 or 1:80 or above, depending on the lab) and a <b>pattern<\/b> that hints at the target:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Homogeneous<\/b> (the whole nucleus glows evenly): antibodies to <b>dsDNA<\/b> or <b>histones<\/b> (SLE, drug-induced lupus).<\/li>\n<li><b>Rim / peripheral<\/b> (the edge of the nucleus glows): <b>anti-dsDNA<\/b>, very specific for <b>SLE<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Speckled<\/b> (fine or coarse dots): antibodies to <b>Sm, RNP, SSA, SSB<\/b> (SLE, mixed connective tissue disease, Sjögren).<\/li>\n<li><b>Nucleolar<\/b> (the nucleoli glow): <b>scleroderma<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Centromere<\/b> (a fixed number of discrete dots): <b>CREST<\/b> (limited scleroderma).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A positive ANA is then \"reflexed\" to specific antibody tests (ELISA or multiplex). Anti-dsDNA can be confirmed with the <b>Crithidia luciliae<\/b> test, which uses an organism with a pure DNA structure (the kinetoplast).<\/p>\n\n<h3>The autoantibody fingerprints<\/h3>\n<p>Group them by organ or disease to remember them:<\/p>\n<p><b>Connective tissue diseases<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>SLE<\/b>: ANA screen; <b>anti-dsDNA<\/b> and <b>anti-Smith<\/b> specific; low <b>C3 and C4<\/b> during flares (immune complexes use up complement). Anti-dsDNA levels track kidney involvement.<\/li>\n<li><b>Drug-induced lupus<\/b> (hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid): <b>anti-histone<\/b>; usually no kidney disease, and it improves when the drug stops.<\/li>\n<li><b>Sjögren syndrome<\/b> (dry eyes and mouth): <b>anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La)<\/b>. Anti-SSA in a pregnant woman can cause <b>heart block in the newborn<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Diffuse scleroderma<\/b>: <b>anti-Scl-70<\/b> (topoisomerase I). <b>CREST<\/b> (Calcinosis, Raynaud's, Esophageal problems, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasia): <b>anti-centromere<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Mixed connective tissue disease<\/b>: high <b>anti-U1 RNP<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Polymyositis<\/b>: <b>anti-Jo-1<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Rheumatoid arthritis<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rheumatoid factor (RF)<\/b> is usually an <b>IgM antibody against the Fc part of IgG<\/b>. It is <b>not specific<\/b>: also positive in other autoimmune diseases, chronic infections (hepatitis C, endocarditis) and healthy older adults.<\/li>\n<li><b>Anti-CCP<\/b> (cyclic citrullinated peptide) is <b>much more specific<\/b> for RA and can appear years before symptoms.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Gut and liver<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Celiac disease<\/b>: <b>anti-tissue transglutaminase (tTG) IgA<\/b> and <b>anti-endomysial IgA<\/b>. Always check <b>total IgA<\/b>: IgA deficiency is more common in celiac disease and gives false negatives (use IgG-based tests instead).<\/li>\n<li><b>Primary biliary cholangitis<\/b>: <b>anti-mitochondrial antibody (AMA)<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Autoimmune hepatitis<\/b>: <b>anti-smooth muscle<\/b> and ANA (type 1); anti-LKM (type 2).<\/li>\n<li><b>Pernicious anemia<\/b>: <b>anti-intrinsic factor<\/b> (more specific) and <b>anti-parietal cell<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Nerve, muscle, kidney and vessels<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Myasthenia gravis<\/b>: <b>anti-acetylcholine receptor<\/b> antibodies block the signal from nerve to muscle, causing weakness that worsens with use.<\/li>\n<li><b>Goodpasture syndrome<\/b>: <b>anti-glomerular basement membrane (anti-GBM)<\/b>, attacking both kidney and lung.<\/li>\n<li><b>Granulomatosis with polyangiitis<\/b> (formerly Wegener): <b>c-ANCA<\/b> (cytoplasmic pattern), target <b>PR3<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Microscopic polyangiitis<\/b>: <b>p-ANCA<\/b> (perinuclear pattern), target <b>MPO<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Endocrine<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Graves disease<\/b>: <b>TSH receptor antibodies<\/b> (TSI) that <i>stimulate<\/i> the thyroid.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hashimoto thyroiditis<\/b>: <b>anti-TPO<\/b> (thyroid peroxidase) and <b>anti-thyroglobulin<\/b>, which damage the thyroid.<\/li>\n<li><b>Type 1 diabetes<\/b>: <b>anti-GAD<\/b>, anti-islet cell, anti-insulin.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hooks<\/b><b>c<\/b>-ANCA = <b>c<\/b>ytoplasmic, PR3, GPA. <b>p<\/b>-ANCA = <b>p<\/b>erinuclear, MPO, <b>p<\/b>olyangiitis (microscopic).<br><b>Histone<\/b> = \"<b>His<\/b>\" drug (hydralazine, isoniazid, procainamide).<br><b>M<\/b>itochondrial = pri<b>M<\/b>ary biliary cholangitis.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Monoclonal gammopathies<\/h3>\n<p>Normally, thousands of plasma cell clones make thousands of different antibodies, which spread across the gamma region as a broad band. If <b>one clone<\/b> grows out of control, it makes huge amounts of <b>one identical antibody<\/b>, the <b>M-protein<\/b> (monoclonal protein), which shows up as a <b>sharp spike<\/b>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP)<\/b> <b>finds<\/b> the spike.<\/li>\n<li><b>Immunofixation (IFE)<\/b> <b>identifies<\/b> it: which heavy chain (G, A, M) and which light chain (kappa or lambda). A monoclonal protein has <b>only one light chain type<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Serum free light chains<\/b> (kappa/lambda ratio) and urine tests detect free light chains (<b>Bence Jones protein<\/b>). The urine dipstick misses it, because the dipstick reacts mainly with albumin.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The conditions:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>MGUS<\/b> (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance): small M-protein (under 3 g/dL), <b>under 10% plasma cells<\/b> in the marrow, and <b>no organ damage<\/b>. Not cancer, but about 1% per year progress, so it is monitored.<\/li>\n<li><b>Multiple myeloma<\/b>: <b>more than 10% plasma cells<\/b> plus organ damage, \"<b>CRAB<\/b>\": high <b>C<\/b>alcium, <b>R<\/b>enal failure, <b>A<\/b>nemia, <b>B<\/b>one lesions. Rouleaux on the smear; high ESR.<\/li>\n<li><b>Waldenström macroglobulinemia<\/b>: <b>IgM<\/b> M-protein from lymphoplasmacytic cells. Big IgM pentamers thicken the blood: <b>hyperviscosity<\/b> (headache, blurred vision, bleeding).<\/li>\n<li><b>Cryoglobulinemia<\/b>: proteins that <b>precipitate in the cold<\/b> and redissolve when warmed, blocking small vessels in the fingers and skin. Often linked to hepatitis C. <b>Collect and keep the sample at 37 °C<\/b> until the serum is separated, or the cryoglobulin will precipitate with the clot and be missed.<\/li>\n<li><b>Amyloidosis (AL)<\/b>: abnormal <b>light chains<\/b> fold into fibres that deposit in the heart, kidney and nerves. Congo red stain shows apple-green birefringence under polarised light.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>ANA positive, rim pattern, low C3 and C4, proteinuria. Which disease and which confirmatory antibody?<\/summary><p>SLE; anti-dsDNA (and/or anti-Smith).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient on hydralazine develops joint pain and a positive ANA. Which antibody?<\/summary><p>Anti-histone (drug-induced lupus).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A celiac screen (tTG IgA) is negative but symptoms strongly suggest celiac disease. What should be checked?<\/summary><p>Total IgA. IgA deficiency causes false negatives; use IgG-based tests if IgA is low.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>IgG kappa M-protein 1.5 g/dL, 4% marrow plasma cells, no CRAB features. Diagnosis?<\/summary><p>MGUS.<\/p><\/details>"},"I4":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Serology diagnoses infections by measuring the body's antibody response, or by detecting pieces of the organism (antigens). It is essential when an organism is hard to grow (viruses, syphilis, Lyme). The key to every serology question is <b>timing<\/b>: which marker appears first, which shows a current infection, and which shows a past infection or immunity.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The general rule: IgM = recent, IgG = past or immune<\/h3>\n<p>After a first infection, <b>IgM<\/b> appears first and fades over weeks to months. <b>IgG<\/b> follows and usually stays for years. So:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>IgM positive<\/b> → recent or current infection.<\/li>\n<li><b>IgG positive, IgM negative<\/b> → past infection or vaccination (immunity).<\/li>\n<li>A <b>fourfold (two-dilution) rise in IgG titer<\/b> between an acute sample and a convalescent sample 2-4 weeks later also proves a recent infection.<\/li>\n<li><b>IgG avidity<\/b>: early IgG binds weakly (low avidity) and strengthens over months. High-avidity CMV IgG means the infection is <b>more than about 3 months old<\/b>, which helps date infections in pregnancy.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Hepatitis B: the most-tested serology<\/h3>\n<p>Work out each marker's meaning and any pattern becomes readable:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>HBsAg<\/b> (surface antigen): the virus coat. <b>Present = infected now<\/b> (acute or chronic). It's the <b>first marker<\/b> to appear. Still present after <b>6 months<\/b> = <b>chronic<\/b> infection.<\/li>\n<li><b>Anti-HBs<\/b> (antibody to surface antigen): the <b>protective<\/b> antibody. Present = <b>immune<\/b>, from recovery or vaccination.<\/li>\n<li><b>Anti-HBc IgM<\/b> (antibody to the core): appears early and marks <b>acute infection<\/b>. It is the only marker present in the <b>window period<\/b>, when HBsAg has cleared but anti-HBs hasn't appeared yet.<\/li>\n<li><b>Anti-HBc total<\/b> (IgG + IgM): shows <b>infection at some time<\/b>. Vaccines contain only surface antigen, so <b>a vaccinated person never has anti-HBc<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>HBeAg<\/b>: shows the virus is multiplying actively, so <b>high infectivity<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Reading the common patterns<\/b><b>HBsAg +, anti-HBc IgM +<\/b> → acute hepatitis B.<br><b>HBsAg −, anti-HBs +, anti-HBc +<\/b> → recovered from past infection (immune).<br><b>HBsAg −, anti-HBs +, anti-HBc −<\/b> → vaccinated.<br><b>HBsAg −, anti-HBs −, anti-HBc IgM +<\/b> → window period of acute infection.<br><b>HBsAg + for more than 6 months, anti-HBc IgG +<\/b> → chronic infection.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Other hepatitis viruses<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hepatitis A<\/b> (faecal-oral): <b>anti-HAV IgM<\/b> = acute; <b>IgG<\/b> = past infection or vaccine. No chronic form.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hepatitis C<\/b> (blood): screen for <b>anti-HCV antibody<\/b>, then confirm current infection with <b>HCV RNA<\/b>. Antibody alone can't tell current from cleared infection.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hepatitis D<\/b>: a defective virus that needs the HBV surface coat, so it infects only people who also have <b>hepatitis B<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Epstein-Barr virus (mononucleosis)<\/h3>\n<p><b>Heterophile antibodies<\/b> are odd IgM antibodies made during mono that react with animal red cells (horse, sheep, beef). The <b>Monospot<\/b> detects them. Their classic identity: they are <b>absorbed by beef red cells but not by guinea pig kidney<\/b> (Forssman antibodies are the reverse). The heterophile test is often <b>negative in children under about 4-5 years<\/b> and early in illness.<\/p>\n<p>EBV-specific antibodies give more detail: <b>VCA IgM<\/b> = acute; <b>VCA IgG<\/b> appears early and lasts for life; <b>EBNA<\/b> antibody appears only after a few months, so <b>EBNA positive means past infection<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Syphilis: two kinds of test<\/h3>\n<p>Syphilis testing uses two families of tests that measure different things:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Nontreponemal tests (RPR, VDRL)<\/b> detect \"<b>reagin<\/b>\", antibody against <b>cardiolipin<\/b> released from damaged cells, not against the organism itself. They are cheap screens, and their <b>titer falls with successful treatment<\/b>, so they monitor therapy. But other conditions that damage cells also produce reagin: <b>biologic false positives<\/b> in SLE, pregnancy, mononucleosis, hepatitis, IV drug use and old age.<\/li>\n<li><b>Treponemal tests (FTA-ABS, TP-PA, EIA/CIA)<\/b> detect antibody against <b>Treponema pallidum itself<\/b>. They confirm the diagnosis but <b>stay positive for life<\/b>, even after cure, so they can't monitor treatment.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Many labs now use the \"<b>reverse algorithm<\/b>\": a treponemal EIA first, then RPR on positives. Only the <b>VDRL<\/b> is accepted for testing <b>CSF<\/b> (neurosyphilis).<\/p>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: the prozone<\/b>In secondary syphilis, antibody levels can be so high that they swamp the test: every antigen site is covered by separate antibodies, so no lattice (clumping) forms, and the RPR reads <b>falsely negative<\/b>. <b>Diluting<\/b> the serum restores the right balance and the test turns positive.<\/div>\n<p><b>Stages of syphilis<\/b>: <b>primary<\/b> (a painless <b>chancre<\/b>; nontreponemal tests may still be negative), <b>secondary<\/b> (rash including <b>palms and soles<\/b>, condylomata lata; tests strongly positive), <b>latent<\/b> (no symptoms, tests positive), and <b>tertiary<\/b> (gummas, aortic and brain damage).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Lyme disease<\/h3>\n<p>Caused by <i>Borrelia burgdorferi<\/i> from a tick bite. <b>Early localised<\/b>: the bull's-eye <b>erythema migrans<\/b> rash (diagnosed clinically; antibodies may not be there yet). <b>Early disseminated<\/b>: heart block, facial palsy, meningitis. <b>Late<\/b>: arthritis and nerve problems. Testing is <b>two-tier<\/b>: an EIA screen, then a <b>Western blot<\/b> or a second EIA to confirm.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Other serology<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rubella<\/b>: IgG = immune; IgM = acute (dangerous in early pregnancy). <b>TORCH<\/b> = Toxoplasma, Other (syphilis, parvovirus, varicella), Rubella, CMV, Herpes: infections that harm the fetus.<\/li>\n<li><b>Measles, mumps, rubella<\/b>: IgM acute, IgG immune.<\/li>\n<li><b>Parvovirus B19<\/b>: IgM = recent infection.<\/li>\n<li><b>West Nile virus<\/b>: IgM in serum or CSF; blood donors are screened by NAT.<\/li>\n<li><b>Post-strep complications<\/b>: <b>ASO<\/b> (anti-streptolysin O) and <b>anti-DNase B<\/b> show a recent group A strep infection, supporting rheumatic fever or glomerulonephritis. Anti-DNase B is better after skin infections.<\/li>\n<li><b>Fungal antigens<\/b>: cryptococcal antigen (CSF, serum), Histoplasma antigen (urine), Aspergillus galactomannan (serum).<\/li>\n<li><b>HIV<\/b>: markers appear in the order <b>RNA → p24 antigen → antibody<\/b>; 4th-generation tests detect both antigen and antibody.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Tuberculosis testing<\/h3>\n<p><b>Tuberculin skin test (PPD, Mantoux)<\/b>: a small amount of TB protein is injected under the skin. If the person's T cells have met TB before, they gather at the site over 2-3 days (a <b>type IV delayed<\/b> reaction). Read at <b>48-72 hours<\/b>, measuring the <b>hardened area (induration), not the redness<\/b>. Cut-offs depend on risk:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>≥5 mm<\/b>: highest risk (HIV, close contacts of TB, immunosuppressed).<\/li>\n<li><b>≥10 mm<\/b>: moderate risk (healthcare workers, recent immigrants, injection drug users).<\/li>\n<li><b>≥15 mm<\/b>: low risk.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>IGRAs (QuantiFERON, T-SPOT)<\/b> are blood tests: the patient's T cells are exposed to TB-specific antigens, and the test measures interferon-gamma release. Advantages: <b>not affected by BCG vaccination<\/b> (the antigens aren't in BCG), one visit, no reading error.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: what these tests can't do<\/b>Neither the skin test nor an IGRA separates latent TB from active TB. Active disease is diagnosed by AFB smear, culture and PCR on clinical specimens.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, anti-HBc negative. Interpretation?<\/summary><p>Immune from vaccination (no core antibody means never infected).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A 3-year-old has symptoms of mono but a negative Monospot. Next test?<\/summary><p>EBV-specific serology (VCA IgM, VCA IgG, EBNA). Heterophile antibodies are often absent in young children.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why does the RPR titer help monitor syphilis treatment but the FTA-ABS doesn't?<\/summary><p>The RPR titer falls after successful treatment; treponemal antibodies like FTA-ABS stay positive for life.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A healthcare worker vaccinated with BCG needs TB screening. Which test avoids a false positive from BCG?<\/summary><p>An interferon-gamma release assay (QuantiFERON or T-SPOT).<\/p><\/details>"},"I5":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Immunology tests all rely on one event: an antibody binding its antigen. What differs is <b>how you see<\/b> that binding: as a visible precipitate, as clumping, as color from an enzyme, as fluorescence, or as scattered light. Understand each method's principle and you can predict when it gives a false result. This lesson also covers transplant immunology.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Precipitation: the lattice and the zones<\/h3>\n<p>When soluble antigen and antibody meet, each antibody can grab two antigens and each antigen can be grabbed by several antibodies, so they build a large <b>lattice<\/b> that becomes visible as a precipitate. The lattice forms best when the amounts are balanced, the <b>zone of equivalence<\/b>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Prozone<\/b> = <b>too much antibody<\/b>. Every antigen gets its own antibodies, no cross-links form, and the result is <b>falsely negative<\/b>. Fix: <b>dilute the serum<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Postzone<\/b> = <b>too much antigen<\/b>. Every antibody arm gets its own antigen; again no lattice, again a false negative. Fix: repeat later when more antibody is present, or dilute the antigen.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b><b>Pro<\/b>zone = <b>pro<\/b>-antibody (antibody excess). <b>Post<\/b>zone = antigen excess. The \"hook effect\" in sandwich immunoassays is the same idea as postzone: so much antigen that it saturates both antibodies.<\/div>\n<p>Classic precipitation methods:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Ouchterlony (double diffusion)<\/b>: antigen and antibody diffuse toward each other from wells in a gel, and the pattern of lines shows whether two antigens are identical, partly identical or unrelated.<\/li>\n<li><b>Radial immunodiffusion (RID)<\/b>: antibody is in the gel; antigen diffuses out from a well and forms a ring. The ring size reflects the antigen concentration. It was used for IgG, IgA and IgM.<\/li>\n<li><b>Immunofixation<\/b>: proteins are separated by electrophoresis, then specific antisera \"fix\" (precipitate) the band. It identifies monoclonal proteins.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Agglutination: clumping particles<\/h3>\n<p>If the antigen is on a <b>particle<\/b> (a red cell, a latex bead, a bacterium), antibody makes the particles <b>clump<\/b>, which is easy to see. It is very sensitive and used in blood typing, latex tests (cryptococcal antigen, RF) and the Monospot. Prozone and postzone apply here too.<\/p>\n<p><b>Titers<\/b> measure antibody strength: make <b>serial two-fold dilutions<\/b> (1:2, 1:4, 1:8...) and find the highest dilution that still reacts. The titer is the <b>reciprocal<\/b>: reacting at 1:64 = titer 64. A <b>fourfold rise<\/b> between acute and convalescent samples is significant.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Labeled immunoassays: ELISA and friends<\/h3>\n<p>For tiny amounts, a <b>label<\/b> is attached to an antibody or antigen to make a signal: an enzyme (ELISA, makes color), a fluorescent dye, or a chemiluminescent molecule (makes light). Three formats matter:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sandwich (two-site)<\/b>: for <b>antigens<\/b> big enough to bind two antibodies. A capture antibody holds the antigen; a labeled detection antibody binds on top. <b>More antigen = more signal.<\/b> Used for hCG, TSH, troponin. Very high antigen can cause a <b>hook effect<\/b> (falsely low).<\/li>\n<li><b>Indirect<\/b>: for detecting <b>antibody<\/b> in a patient. Antigen is fixed to the well, patient antibody binds, then a labeled <b>anti-human IgG<\/b> detects it. More antibody = more signal. Used for HIV and hepatitis antibody screens.<\/li>\n<li><b>Competitive<\/b>: for <b>small molecules<\/b> (drugs, hormones like T4). Patient analyte and a labeled version of the analyte <b>compete<\/b> for a limited number of antibody sites. The more patient analyte, the less label binds: <b>more analyte = less signal<\/b> (inverse relationship).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: competitive assays run backwards<\/b>In a competitive assay a <b>low signal means a high concentration<\/b>. Biotin interference also runs in opposite directions in the two formats: falsely low in sandwich assays, falsely high in competitive ones.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Other techniques<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Western blot<\/b>: proteins (such as viral proteins) are separated by size on a gel, transferred (blotted) onto a membrane, and then patient serum is added. Bands show which specific proteins the patient has antibodies to. It is used to confirm Lyme disease.<\/li>\n<li><b>Immunofluorescence<\/b>: <b>direct<\/b> (a labeled antibody binds antigen in tissue) or <b>indirect<\/b> (patient antibody binds first, then labeled anti-human IgG). Indirect IF on HEp-2 cells is the ANA test.<\/li>\n<li><b>Nephelometry<\/b>: measures light <b>scattered<\/b> by immune complexes; used for IgG, IgA, IgM, complement, CRP and others. Fast and automated.<\/li>\n<li><b>Monoclonal antibodies<\/b> are produced by <b>hybridoma technology<\/b>: a single antibody-making B cell is fused with an immortal myeloma cell, giving an endless supply of one identical antibody. Most modern immunoassay reagents are monoclonal.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Predictive value depends on prevalence<\/h3>\n<p>Sensitivity and specificity belong to the test. Predictive values also depend on <b>how common the disease is<\/b> in the people tested. When a disease is rare, even a very specific test gives many false positives compared with true positives, so the <b>positive predictive value (PPV) is low<\/b>. Test a high-risk group (high prevalence) and PPV rises. That's why screening tests in low-risk populations are always followed by confirmatory tests.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Transplant immunology<\/h3>\n<p>The immune system recognises a transplanted organ as foreign mainly through <b>HLA<\/b> (the human MHC). The more HLA differences, the stronger the rejection.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>HLA matching<\/b>: <b>HLA-A, -B and -DR<\/b> matter most, with <b>DR<\/b> the most important for kidney grafts.<\/li>\n<li><b>Crossmatch<\/b>: recipient serum is tested against donor lymphocytes (by complement-dependent cytotoxicity or flow cytometry) to detect <b>preformed anti-HLA antibodies<\/b>. A positive crossmatch predicts <b>hyperacute rejection<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Types of rejection, by timing and mechanism:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hyperacute<\/b>: minutes to hours; <b>preformed antibodies<\/b> (ABO or HLA) attack the graft's blood vessels at once.<\/li>\n<li><b>Acute<\/b>: days to weeks; mainly <b>T-cell mediated<\/b> (and sometimes antibody). Usually treatable with stronger immunosuppression.<\/li>\n<li><b>Chronic<\/b>: months to years; slow scarring and narrowing of vessels; hard to treat.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Graft-versus-host disease (GVHD)<\/b> is rejection in reverse: <b>donor lymphocytes attack the recipient<\/b>. It happens when immune cells are transferred to someone who can't reject them: a stem cell transplant, or an <b>unirradiated<\/b> blood product given to an immunocompromised patient. Skin, gut and liver are hit.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A latex cryptococcal antigen test is negative but India ink is full of yeast. What happened, and what do you do?<\/summary><p>Antigen excess prevented lattice formation (a prozone/hook-type effect). Dilute the CSF and repeat.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>In a competitive immunoassay, the signal is very low. Is the analyte concentration high or low?<\/summary><p>High. More patient analyte displaces the labeled analyte, lowering the signal.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Serial two-fold dilutions react up to 1:64. What is the titer?<\/summary><p>64.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A kidney is rejected within minutes of the vessels being connected. Mechanism?<\/summary><p>Hyperacute rejection from preformed antibodies (ABO or anti-HLA) attacking the graft's vessels.<\/p><\/details>"},"I6":{"min":3,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Immunology traps test whether you know <i>which<\/i> part of the immune system does what, and whether a test is a screen or a confirmation. Each listed trap is explained below, followed by more classics.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 1: \"IgG comes first\"<\/h3>\n<p>IgG is the most abundant antibody, which tempts people to pick it. But <b>IgM is made first<\/b> in a new (primary) response, and as a big pentamer with ten binding sites it is the <b>best at activating complement<\/b> and at agglutinating. IgG dominates the later, memory response. So IgM = recent infection; IgG alone = past infection or immunity.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 2: \"all hypersensitivity is antibody-driven\"<\/h3>\n<p>Types I, II and III involve antibodies (IgE; IgG/IgM against cells; immune complexes). <b>Type IV involves T cells, not antibody<\/b>. That's why it takes 48-72 hours (T cells have to travel to the site and recruit macrophages) and why it can't be passed on with serum. Examples: TB skin test, poison ivy, nickel allergy, graft rejection.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 3: using the wrong syphilis test to monitor treatment<\/h3>\n<p><b>Treponemal tests<\/b> (FTA-ABS, TP-PA, EIA) detect antibody against the organism and <b>stay positive for life<\/b>, even after a cure. <b>Nontreponemal tests<\/b> (RPR, VDRL) detect reagin, and their <b>titer falls with successful treatment<\/b>. Use the RPR titer to follow therapy; a fourfold fall means a response.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 4: a positive ANA means lupus<\/h3>\n<p>The ANA is a sensitive <b>screen<\/b>: nearly all SLE patients are positive, but so are other autoimmune diseases, people on some drugs, and about 5% of healthy people. A positive ANA must be followed by specific tests. <b>Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith<\/b> are <b>specific<\/b> for SLE. A <b>negative<\/b> ANA makes SLE very unlikely.<\/p>\n\n<h3>More immunology traps<\/h3>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Prozone<\/b>Very high antibody can give a false-negative agglutination or precipitation test (for example RPR in secondary syphilis). Dilute and repeat.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Heterophile in young children<\/b>A negative Monospot in a child under about 4-5 doesn't rule out mono. Use EBV-specific antibodies.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>IgA deficiency and celiac testing<\/b>An IgA-based tTG test is falsely negative if the patient is IgA deficient. Check total IgA.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Hepatitis B vaccination<\/b>Vaccinated people have anti-HBs only, never anti-HBc. Anti-HBc means a real infection at some time.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>BCG and the skin test<\/b>BCG vaccination can cause a positive tuberculin skin test. IGRAs are not affected.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Complement handling<\/b>Heating serum to 56 °C for 30 minutes destroys complement. Samples for complement tests must be fresh or frozen.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Rheumatoid factor<\/b>RF is not specific for rheumatoid arthritis. Anti-CCP is.<\/div>\n\n<h3>How to approach an immunology trap<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Ask which arm of the immune system is involved: antibody (which class?), T cell or complement.<\/li>\n<li>Ask whether the test is a <b>screen<\/b> (sensitive) or a <b>confirmation<\/b> (specific).<\/li>\n<li>Think about timing: IgM vs IgG, window periods, and which markers stay for life.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which antibody class best activates complement?<\/summary><p>IgM.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>After syphilis treatment, which test should fall in titer?<\/summary><p>The nontreponemal test (RPR or VDRL).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A reaction appears 48 hours after skin contact with nickel. Which hypersensitivity type, and what mediates it?<\/summary><p>Type IV, mediated by T cells (no antibody).<\/p><\/details>"},"L1":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Every patient result depends on one assumption: the test was working correctly when it ran. <b>Quality control (QC)<\/b> is how the lab proves it. You run samples with known values (controls) alongside the patients, and if a control misbehaves, you don't trust the patient results. This lesson explains the statistics behind QC in plain terms, then how to read QC charts and the Westgard rules.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The 8 big ideas, explained<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Accuracy vs precision.<\/b> Accuracy = hitting the true value. Precision = getting the same answer again and again.<\/li>\n<li><b>Shift vs trend.<\/b> A shift is a sudden jump to a new level; a trend is a gradual drift. Both are systematic errors.<\/li>\n<li><b>Westgard rules<\/b>: 1-2s warns; 1-3s and R-4s mean random error; 2-2s, 4-1s and 10x mean systematic error. Failed QC means patient results are held.<\/li>\n<li><b>Who does what<\/b>: CLIA is the federal law, CAP and The Joint Commission accredit, OSHA protects workers, CLSI writes standards.<\/li>\n<li><b>Standard precautions<\/b>: treat all blood and body fluids as infectious; never recap needles.<\/li>\n<li><b>Blood spills<\/b>: fresh 1:10 bleach.<\/li>\n<li><b>Order of draw<\/b>: blood cultures, light blue, red/gold, green, lavender, gray.<\/li>\n<li><b>Most errors are preanalytic<\/b>: wrong patient, wrong tube, poor collection.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Accuracy and precision: the dartboard<\/h3>\n<p>Picture throwing darts at a target:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Accurate and precise<\/b>: all darts tightly grouped in the bullseye. The ideal.<\/li>\n<li><b>Precise but not accurate<\/b>: tightly grouped, but off to one side. Every result is wrong by the same amount: <b>systematic error (bias)<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Accurate on average but not precise<\/b>: spread all around the bullseye: <b>random error<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Precision<\/b> is measured by the <b>standard deviation (SD)<\/b> and the <b>coefficient of variation (CV)<\/b>. <b>Accuracy<\/b> is judged by comparing with the true value (reference materials, proficiency testing, method comparison).<\/p>\n\n<h3>The basic statistics, made simple<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mean<\/b> = the average (add all values, divide by how many).<\/li>\n<li><b>Standard deviation (SD)<\/b> = how spread out the values are around the mean. Small SD = tight, precise results.<\/li>\n<li><b>Coefficient of variation (CV%)<\/b> = (SD ÷ mean) × 100. It expresses spread as a percentage, so you can compare precision between tests with different units or levels. Lower CV = better precision.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Control mean 100 mg/dL, SD 5. CV = 5 ÷ 100 × 100 = <b>5%<\/b>. Today's control is 112: (112 − 100) ÷ 5 = <b>+2.4 SD<\/b>, which breaks the 1-2s warning limit.<\/div>\n<p><b>The normal (Gaussian) distribution<\/b>: if you run the same control many times, the results form a bell curve. Within <b>±1 SD<\/b> fall about <b>68%<\/b> of results; within <b>±2 SD<\/b> about <b>95%<\/b>; within <b>±3 SD<\/b> about <b>99.7%<\/b>. That is where QC limits come from: a control outside 2 SD should happen only about 1 time in 20 by chance, and outside 3 SD only about 3 times in 1,000.<\/p>\n<p><b>Reference intervals<\/b> are built the same way: the <b>middle 95%<\/b> of healthy people (about mean ± 2 SD). That means <b>about 5% of healthy people will fall outside the \"normal\" range<\/b> just by chance; an abnormal result isn't always disease. Labs establish a reference interval with at least <b>120<\/b> healthy people, or verify a manufacturer's interval with at least <b>20<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Levey-Jennings charts and the Westgard rules<\/h3>\n<p>Control results are plotted each day on a <b>Levey-Jennings chart<\/b>: the mean is drawn as the centre line, with lines at ±1, ±2 and ±3 SD. Run at least <b>two levels<\/b> of control (for example normal and abnormal) each day. The Westgard rules are a checklist for spotting trouble. The name tells you the rule: \"<b>2-2s<\/b>\" means <b>2<\/b> controls beyond <b>2 s<\/b>(D).<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>1-2s<\/b>: one control outside ±2 SD. A <b>warning<\/b> only. It prompts you to check the other rules, but doesn't reject the run by itself.<\/li>\n<li><b>1-3s<\/b>: one control outside ±3 SD. <b>Reject. Random error<\/b> (or a big systematic error).<\/li>\n<li><b>R-4s<\/b>: within one run, one control above +2 SD and another below −2 SD (a range of more than 4 SD). <b>Reject. Random error<\/b>: the results are scattering.<\/li>\n<li><b>2-2s<\/b>: two controls in a row (or both levels in one run) beyond 2 SD <b>on the same side<\/b>. <b>Reject. Systematic error.<\/b><\/li>\n<li><b>4-1s<\/b>: four in a row beyond 1 SD on the same side. <b>Reject. Systematic error.<\/b><\/li>\n<li><b>10x<\/b>: ten in a row on the same side of the mean. <b>Reject. Systematic error<\/b> (a shift).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: random or systematic?<\/b><b>Random<\/b> rules catch <b>scatter<\/b>: one wild point (1-3s) or points far apart in opposite directions (R-4s). <b>Systematic<\/b> rules catch <b>everything leaning one way<\/b>: 2-2s, 4-1s, 10x.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Shifts, trends and their causes<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Shift<\/b>: an abrupt jump to a new level that stays (six or more points on one side). Causes: a <b>new reagent lot<\/b>, a <b>recalibration<\/b>, a new control lot, maintenance or a part replaced.<\/li>\n<li><b>Trend<\/b>: values <b>gradually drifting<\/b> in one direction (six or more points steadily rising or falling). Causes: <b>deteriorating reagent<\/b>, an <b>aging lamp<\/b>, a dirty probe, slow temperature drift.<\/li>\n<li><b>Increased scatter<\/b>: random error. Causes: <b>pipetting problems<\/b>, <b>bubbles<\/b>, poor mixing, an unstable power supply.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>What to do when QC fails<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Stop.<\/b> Don't report patient results from that run.<\/li>\n<li><b>Look for the cause<\/b>: reagents (expired? new lot? stored properly?), calibration, the instrument (maintenance, lamp, temperature), the control material itself.<\/li>\n<li><b>Fix it<\/b>: recalibrate, replace the reagent, do maintenance. Then <b>rerun the controls<\/b>. Simply rerunning the same control until it \"passes\" is not acceptable.<\/li>\n<li><b>Review patient results<\/b> run since the last acceptable QC, and rerun them if needed.<\/li>\n<li><b>Document<\/b> everything (corrective action).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: changing the mean<\/b>Never change the control mean or widen the limits to make QC \"pass\". Means are set for a new control lot by running it alongside the old lot, collecting at least <b>20 data points<\/b>.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Other quality tools<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Method verification<\/b> (before a new test is used for patients): check <b>accuracy<\/b>, <b>precision<\/b>, the <b>reportable range<\/b> (linearity) and the <b>reference interval<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Delta checks<\/b>: compare a patient's result with their previous one. A big unexplained change (Hgb 14 yesterday, 6 today) flags a possible <b>mix-up<\/b> or contaminated sample.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>More statistics you should recognise<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mean, median, mode<\/b>: average, middle value, and most frequent value. In a symmetric bell curve they are about equal; in a skewed distribution they separate.<\/li>\n<li><b>95% confidence interval<\/b> = mean ± 1.96 SD (about 2 SD).<\/li>\n<li><b>Method comparison<\/b> (old vs new method on the same samples): the <b>correlation coefficient (r)<\/b> shows how closely they track (1.0 = perfect). In the regression line, the <b>slope<\/b> shows <b>proportional error<\/b> (the gap grows with concentration) and the <b>y-intercept<\/b> shows <b>constant error<\/b> (a fixed offset).<\/li>\n<li><b>t-test<\/b> compares two <b>means<\/b> (accuracy). <b>F-test<\/b> compares two <b>SDs<\/b> (precision).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Two consecutive controls are both +2.2 SD. Which rule, and what type of error?<\/summary><p>2-2s; systematic error (such as calibration drift).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Control level 1 is +2.5 SD and level 2 is −2.3 SD in the same run. Which rule?<\/summary><p>R-4s (range over 4 SD): random error. Reject the run.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>QC values have been slowly rising for 8 days. Pattern and likely cause?<\/summary><p>A trend (systematic). Likely a deteriorating reagent, an aging light source or temperature drift.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Mean 250, SD 10. What is the CV?<\/summary><p>10 ÷ 250 × 100 = 4%.<\/p><\/details>"},"L2":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Laboratories are regulated because wrong results harm patients, and labs are dangerous places to work. This lesson explains who makes the rules and what they require (regulation), how to protect yourself and others from biological, chemical and fire hazards (safety), and what to do the moment something goes wrong (emergencies).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Who does what: the organisations<\/h3>\n<p>Think of it as <b>law vs inspection vs guidance<\/b>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>CLIA '88<\/b> (Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments, run by <b>CMS<\/b>): the <b>federal law<\/b> covering every lab that tests human samples for health care. It sets test <b>complexity levels<\/b> (waived, moderate, high), personnel qualifications, QC and <b>proficiency testing<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>CAP<\/b> (College of American Pathologists): <b>accredits and inspects<\/b> labs (every 2 years). Labs accredited by CAP have \"deemed status\": they are treated as meeting CLIA.<\/li>\n<li><b>The Joint Commission<\/b>: accredits <b>hospitals<\/b>, including their labs. <b>COLA<\/b> accredits mainly physician office labs.<\/li>\n<li><b>FDA<\/b>: clears or approves <b>tests, instruments, reagents<\/b> and regulates <b>blood products<\/b> and blood banks.<\/li>\n<li><b>OSHA<\/b>: protects <b>workers<\/b>: the bloodborne pathogens standard, chemical hygiene and hazard communication.<\/li>\n<li><b>CLSI<\/b>: writes <b>consensus standards and guidelines<\/b> (order of draw, susceptibility breakpoints, method evaluation). Guidance, not law, but widely required by inspectors.<\/li>\n<li><b>AABB<\/b>: standards for <b>blood banks and transfusion<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>CDC<\/b>: disease surveillance, infection-control guidance, and it advises on CLIA.<\/li>\n<li><b>ISO 15189<\/b>: the international quality standard for medical labs.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b><b>CLIA<\/b> = the law. <b>CAP / TJC<\/b> = the inspectors. <b>OSHA<\/b> = your safety. <b>FDA<\/b> = the products. <b>CLSI<\/b> = the how-to manual.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Proficiency testing (PT)<\/h3>\n<p>PT checks a lab's accuracy from the outside. An approved provider sends <b>unknown samples<\/b>, the lab tests them and reports results, and the provider grades them against other labs.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typically <b>3 events a year<\/b>, <b>5 samples<\/b> per event.<\/li>\n<li>Passing score: <b>80%<\/b> for most tests, but <b>100% for ABO and Rh typing<\/b> and compatibility testing (no room for error).<\/li>\n<li>PT samples must be <b>tested like patient samples<\/b> (same staff, same methods). <b>Never<\/b> send PT samples to another lab or discuss results with other labs before the deadline. That is \"PT referral\", a serious CLIA violation that can cost a lab its certificate.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Waived tests<\/b> (simple tests with little risk of error: urine dipstick, glucose meter, rapid strep, urine hCG) need only a <b>certificate of waiver<\/b> and must follow the manufacturer's instructions exactly.<\/p>\n<p><b>Record retention<\/b> (typical US requirements, check local rules): patient test records and QC <b>2 years<\/b>; blood bank records <b>5 years or longer<\/b>; pathology reports <b>10 years<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Biological safety<\/h3>\n<p><b>Standard precautions<\/b> treat <b>all blood and body fluids from every patient<\/b> as potentially infectious, because you can't tell who is infected by looking.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>PPE<\/b>: gloves, fluid-resistant lab coat, and face/eye protection when splashes are possible.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hand hygiene<\/b>: alcohol rub is fine for clean hands, but use <b>soap and water<\/b> when hands are <b>visibly dirty<\/b> and after contact with <b>C. difficile<\/b> (alcohol doesn't kill its spores).<\/li>\n<li><b>Sharps<\/b>: <b>never recap<\/b> needles; drop them straight into a puncture-resistant sharps container, and replace it when it is <b>two-thirds full<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>OSHA bloodborne pathogens standard<\/b>: employers must <b>offer hepatitis B vaccine free<\/b> (within 10 days of starting), provide PPE and training, and keep an <b>exposure control plan<\/b> reviewed every year.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Blood spills<\/b>: put on gloves, cover with absorbent material, flood with <b>freshly made 1:10 bleach<\/b> (about 0.5% hypochlorite; diluted bleach loses strength within a day), leave for at least <b>10 minutes<\/b>, wipe up and discard as biohazard waste.<\/p>\n<p><b>Biosafety levels<\/b> match containment to danger:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>BSL-1<\/b>: organisms not known to cause disease in healthy adults.<\/li>\n<li><b>BSL-2<\/b>: the standard clinical lab: S. aureus, Salmonella, hepatitis B, HIV in clinical specimens. Biosafety cabinet for splash or aerosol work.<\/li>\n<li><b>BSL-3<\/b>: organisms spread by <b>air<\/b> that cause serious disease: <b>M. tuberculosis cultures<\/b>, <b>Brucella<\/b>, <b>Coccidioides<\/b>, Francisella. Controlled access and negative-pressure rooms.<\/li>\n<li><b>BSL-4<\/b>: exotic, deadly agents with no treatment (Ebola): full-body positive-pressure suits.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Chemical safety<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Every hazardous chemical must have a <b>Safety Data Sheet (SDS)<\/b>, which lists hazards, PPE, first aid and spill response. Staff must be able to reach it.<\/li>\n<li><b>NFPA 704 diamond<\/b> (on doors and containers): <b>blue = health<\/b>, <b>red = flammability<\/b>, <b>yellow = reactivity<\/b>, <b>white = special<\/b> hazards (W with a line = reacts with water; OX = oxidiser). Each is rated <b>0 (none) to 4 (severe)<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Always add acid to water<\/b>, never water to acid. Adding water to concentrated acid releases heat so fast it can boil and spatter. (\"Do as you oughta, add acid to water.\")<\/li>\n<li>Store <b>acids and bases apart<\/b>, flammables in approved safety cabinets, and limit quantities on the bench.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Fire safety<\/h3>\n<p>Two acronyms:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>RACE<\/b> (what to do): <b>R<\/b>escue anyone in danger, pull the <b>A<\/b>larm, <b>C<\/b>ontain the fire (close doors), <b>E<\/b>xtinguish if it is small and safe (or Evacuate).<\/li>\n<li><b>PASS<\/b> (how to use an extinguisher): <b>P<\/b>ull the pin, <b>A<\/b>im at the base of the fire, <b>S<\/b>queeze the handle, <b>S<\/b>weep side to side.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Fire classes<\/b>: <b>A<\/b> = ordinary combustibles (paper, wood: \"Ash\"), <b>B<\/b> = flammable liquids (\"Barrel\"), <b>C<\/b> = electrical (\"Current\"; never use water), <b>D<\/b> = combustible metals.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Waste and shipping<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Biohazard waste<\/b> is decontaminated by <b>autoclaving<\/b> (steam at <b>121 °C, 15 psi, for 30 minutes or more<\/b>) or incineration. Sharps go in rigid containers. Chemical waste is sorted by hazard class.<\/li>\n<li><b>Shipping infectious specimens<\/b> uses <b>triple packaging<\/b>: a leak-proof primary container, a leak-proof secondary container with absorbent material, and a rigid outer package. <b>Category A<\/b> (can cause life-threatening disease, such as Ebola cultures) is <b>UN2814<\/b>; <b>Category B<\/b> (most diagnostic specimens) is <b>UN3373<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Emergency procedures: what to do first<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Needlestick or cut<\/b>: <b>wash with soap and water immediately<\/b> (don't squeeze, and don't use bleach on skin), <b>report at once<\/b>, and get evaluated. The source patient is tested if possible. HIV post-exposure prophylaxis works best when started <b>within hours<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Splash to eyes, nose or mouth<\/b>: flush with water or the <b>eyewash for 15 minutes<\/b>, then report.<\/li>\n<li><b>Chemical on skin<\/b>: remove contaminated clothing and rinse for at least <b>15 minutes<\/b> (safety shower for large areas).<\/li>\n<li><b>Spills<\/b>: use the correct spill kit; mercury needs special cleanup.<\/li>\n<li>Know where the <b>alarms, exits, extinguishers, eyewash and shower<\/b> are before you need them.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which agency issues CLIA regulations, and which accredits labs on its behalf?<\/summary><p>CMS administers CLIA. Accrediting bodies such as CAP and The Joint Commission inspect labs and have deemed status.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>What score is needed to pass proficiency testing for ABO grouping?<\/summary><p>100%. (Most other analytes need 80%.)<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>The red section of the NFPA diamond shows a 4. What does it mean?<\/summary><p>Extremely flammable.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A technologist gets a needlestick. What is the first step?<\/summary><p>Wash the site with soap and water immediately, then report it and get evaluated.<\/p><\/details>"},"L3":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Most laboratory errors happen <b>before the sample ever reaches the analyzer<\/b>: the wrong patient, the wrong tube, the wrong order, a long tourniquet, a delay. This lesson explains the collection rules and the reasons behind them, then the everyday lab math (dilutions, solutions, conversions) that shows up as quick calculation questions.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Patient identification comes first<\/h3>\n<p>Use <b>two independent identifiers<\/b> (such as full name and date of birth, or name and medical record number), checked against the request, and <b>label tubes at the bedside<\/b> in front of the patient. Never use a room or bed number; patients move. A mislabeled sample is worse than no sample, because it puts one patient's result in another's chart.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Order of draw: why it matters<\/h3>\n<p>When several tubes are filled from one needle, a tiny amount of additive from one tube can be carried into the next. The CLSI order is designed so that each carry-over does the least harm:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Blood culture bottles<\/b> (sterile; must come first to avoid contamination).<\/li>\n<li><b>Light blue (sodium citrate)<\/b>: coagulation. Comes before tubes whose clot activators or other additives would start clotting or interfere.<\/li>\n<li><b>Red or gold (serum, with or without gel/clot activator)<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Green (heparin)<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Lavender (EDTA)<\/b>: CBC, blood bank. EDTA carried into a chemistry tube adds <b>potassium<\/b> (K2EDTA) and binds <b>calcium<\/b>, so it must come late.<\/li>\n<li><b>Gray (sodium fluoride/potassium oxalate)<\/b>: glucose, lactate. Fluoride and oxalate damage cells and bind calcium, so last.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b>\"<b>B<\/b>oys <b>L<\/b>ove <b>R<\/b>ay's <b>G<\/b>arage <b>L<\/b>ate <b>G<\/b>reasy\": Blood culture, Light blue, Red, Green, Lavender, Gray.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: EDTA contamination<\/b>Very high potassium with very low calcium (and low ALP and magnesium) in a well patient = EDTA carry-over or drawing the chemistry tube after the lavender. Recollect in the right order.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Venipuncture technique and its effects<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep the <b>tourniquet under 1 minute<\/b>. Longer, and water leaks out of the vein, concentrating proteins, cells and protein-bound substances (calcium, cholesterol).<\/li>\n<li><b>Fist pumping<\/b> releases potassium from working forearm muscles, falsely raising K.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hemolysis<\/b> (small needle, forcing blood through a syringe, shaking tubes) releases what is inside red cells: <b>potassium, LD, AST, phosphate and magnesium<\/b> rise.<\/li>\n<li><b>Avoid<\/b> an arm with an <b>IV running<\/b> (the sample would be diluted with IV fluid, often showing high glucose or electrolytes), a <b>hematoma<\/b>, the side of a <b>mastectomy<\/b> (lymph drainage problems), or a dialysis <b>fistula<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Invert additive tubes gently the right number of times, so the additive mixes without causing hemolysis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Capillary (skin puncture) collection<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Infants<\/b>: the <b>outer side of the heel<\/b>, no deeper than <b>2 mm<\/b> (to avoid hitting the heel bone).<\/li>\n<li><b>Older children and adults<\/b>: the side of the fingertip pad of the <b>3rd or 4th finger<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Wipe away the first drop<\/b>, which contains tissue fluid.<\/li>\n<li>For capillary collections, the <b>EDTA tube is filled first<\/b>, the opposite of venipuncture, because platelets start clumping at the wound, which would ruin the CBC.<\/li>\n<li>Don't squeeze hard (\"milking\"), which adds tissue fluid and causes hemolysis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Special handling: match the condition to the reason<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>On ice<\/b>: <b>ammonia<\/b> (keeps rising in the tube), <b>lactate<\/b> (cells keep making it), <b>blood gases<\/b> if delayed (cells keep using oxygen), <b>ACTH<\/b> and some other hormones (unstable).<\/li>\n<li><b>Protect from light<\/b>: <b>bilirubin<\/b> (breaks down in light), some vitamins.<\/li>\n<li><b>Keep warm (37 °C)<\/b>: <b>cold agglutinins<\/b> and <b>cryoglobulins<\/b>. They would attach to cells or precipitate in the cold and be lost with the clot.<\/li>\n<li><b>Don't refrigerate before separating<\/b>: <b>potassium<\/b>. In the cold, the cells' sodium-potassium pumps stop and potassium leaks out.<\/li>\n<li><b>Separate serum or plasma within 2 hours<\/b> for most chemistry, or cells keep altering the sample (glucose falls, potassium and phosphate rise).<\/li>\n<li><b>Fasting 8-12 hours<\/b> for glucose and lipids (triglycerides especially).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Specimen rejection and chain of custody<\/h3>\n<p>Reject specimens that are <b>unlabeled or mislabeled<\/b>, in the <b>wrong tube<\/b>, <b>clotted<\/b> (an EDTA tube for a CBC), <b>too little volume<\/b> (wrong blood-to-additive ratio, especially citrate), <b>too old<\/b> or stored wrongly, or obviously <b>contaminated<\/b> (drawn above an IV). Document and request a new sample.<\/p>\n<p>For legal samples (forensic drug tests, blood alcohol), a <b>chain of custody<\/b> form records <b>every person who handles the specimen<\/b>, with dates and times, so it can be proved in court that it wasn't tampered with.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Lab math made easy<\/h3>\n<p><b>Dilutions.<\/b> A dilution is <b>sample volume ÷ total volume<\/b>. Adding 0.5 mL serum to 4.5 mL saline gives 0.5 ÷ 5.0 = <b>1:10<\/b> (not 1:9, because the total includes the sample). To get the real concentration, multiply the diluted result by the <b>dilution factor<\/b> (10).<\/p>\n<p><b>Serial dilutions multiply.<\/b> A 1:2 followed by a 1:5 = 1:10. In a two-fold series starting at 1:2, the tubes are 1:2, 1:4, 1:8, 1:16, 1:32...<\/p>\n<p><b>C1 × V1 = C2 × V2<\/b> (making a weaker solution from a stronger one). To make 100 mL of 10% bleach from 100% stock: 100% × V1 = 10% × 100 mL, so V1 = 10 mL stock + 90 mL water.<\/p>\n<p><b>Solutions:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Percent (w/v)<\/b> = grams per 100 mL. A 10% NaCl solution has 10 g per 100 mL, so 250 mL needs 25 g.<\/li>\n<li><b>Molarity (M)<\/b> = moles per liter (moles = grams ÷ molecular weight).<\/li>\n<li><b>Normality (N)<\/b> = equivalents per liter. For an acid, equivalents = moles × the number of H+ it can release (1 M H2SO4 = 2 N).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Conversions:<\/b> °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9. Glucose mg/dL ÷ 18 = mmol/L. Creatinine mg/dL × 88.4 = µmol/L.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked examples<\/b>98.6 °F → (98.6 − 32) × 5/9 = <b>37 °C<\/b>.<br>Glucose 126 mg/dL ÷ 18 = <b>7.0 mmol/L<\/b>.<br>Creatinine 1.2 mg/dL × 88.4 = <b>106 µmol/L<\/b>.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Equipment basics<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Temperatures<\/b> (recorded daily): refrigerator <b>2-8 °C<\/b> (blood bank fridge 1-6 °C), freezer <b>−20 °C or colder<\/b>, incubator <b>35-37 °C<\/b>, room temperature <b>20-25 °C<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Centrifuge<\/b>: the force depends on both speed and radius: <b>RCF = 1.118 × 10⁻⁵ × radius (cm) × rpm²<\/b>. Check speed (with a tachometer) and timers regularly.<\/li>\n<li><b>Laboratory water<\/b>: <b>Type I<\/b> (purest) for sensitive work (reagents, trace metals, molecular); <b>Type II<\/b> for general lab tests; <b>Type III<\/b> for rinsing glassware.<\/li>\n<li><b>pH meters<\/b>: calibrate with at least <b>two buffers<\/b> that bracket the expected pH.<\/li>\n<li><b>Pipettes<\/b>: check accuracy and precision regularly; read the <b>meniscus at eye level<\/b> (bottom of the curve for clear liquids).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which tube follows the light blue tube in the order of draw?<\/summary><p>The red or gold (serum) tube.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A skin puncture needs a CBC and a chemistry tube. Which is filled first?<\/summary><p>The EDTA (CBC) tube, to prevent platelet clumping from affecting the count.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>How do you prepare 500 mL of 0.85% saline?<\/summary><p>0.85 g per 100 mL × 5 = 4.25 g NaCl, made up to 500 mL with water.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why must a cryoglobulin sample be kept at 37 °C until separated?<\/summary><p>Cryoglobulins precipitate in the cold and would be lost with the clot, giving a falsely negative result.<\/p><\/details>"},"L4":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Lab instruments look very different, but each is built on a simple physical principle: light absorbed, emitted or scattered; an electrical signal; separation by charge, size or mass. Learn the principle and you can answer \"which method measures X?\" and \"what interferes with it?\". The second half covers molecular methods, which copy and detect DNA and RNA.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Light-based methods<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Spectrophotometry / photometry<\/b>: measures how much light a colored solution <b>absorbs<\/b> at a chosen wavelength. By <b>Beer's law<\/b>, absorbance is proportional to concentration. Most chemistry tests use it.<\/li>\n<li><b>Fluorometry<\/b>: a molecule absorbs light at one wavelength and <b>emits<\/b> light at a longer one. Because the detector looks at emitted light against a dark background (at a right angle), it is much <b>more sensitive<\/b> than absorbance. Weak points: quenching and background fluorescence.<\/li>\n<li><b>Nephelometry<\/b> measures light <b>scattered<\/b> at an angle by particles; <b>turbidimetry<\/b> measures the light <b>blocked<\/b> (straight through). Both are used for proteins and immunoglobulins (antigen-antibody complexes form particles).<\/li>\n<li><b>Osmometry<\/b>: dissolved particles lower the freezing point, so measuring the <b>freezing point depression<\/b> gives the total particle concentration (osmolality).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Separation methods<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Electrophoresis<\/b>: charged molecules move in an <b>electric field<\/b>, separating by charge (and size). Used for serum proteins, hemoglobins, isoenzymes and DNA.<\/li>\n<li><b>Chromatography<\/b>: a mixture is carried by a moving phase (gas or liquid) over a stationary phase. Each compound sticks to the stationary phase differently, so they come out at different times (retention time). <b>Gas chromatography (GC)<\/b> for volatile compounds (alcohols, drugs), <b>HPLC<\/b> for larger or heat-sensitive ones (vitamins, HbA1c, hemoglobin variants), <b>thin-layer (TLC)<\/b> as a simple screen.<\/li>\n<li><b>Mass spectrometry (MS)<\/b>: molecules are given a charge (ionised) and sorted by their <b>mass-to-charge ratio (m/z)<\/b>, giving a unique fingerprint. Combined with GC or LC (GC-MS, LC-MS/MS), it is the <b>gold standard<\/b> for drug confirmation and newborn screening (tandem MS), and MALDI-TOF uses it for microbe identification.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Electrochemistry: measuring with electrodes<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Potentiometry<\/b> (measures <b>voltage<\/b> at zero current): <b>ion-selective electrodes<\/b> for Na, K, Cl, and the <b>pH electrode<\/b>. The membrane lets only the target ion interact, creating a voltage that depends on its activity. The <b>Severinghaus electrode<\/b> for <b>pCO2<\/b> is a modified pH electrode: CO2 diffuses through a membrane and changes the pH of a bicarbonate solution.<\/li>\n<li><b>Amperometry<\/b> (measures <b>current<\/b>): the <b>Clark electrode<\/b> for <b>pO2<\/b>. Oxygen is reduced at a cathode, and the current is proportional to the oxygen.<\/li>\n<li><b>Coulometry<\/b>: counts the total charge used in a reaction; the classic <b>chloride<\/b> titrator (silver ions generated until all chloride is used up). Used for sweat chloride.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: the blood gas electrodes<\/b><b>pH<\/b> and <b>pCO2<\/b> are <b>potentiometric<\/b> (voltage); <b>pO2<\/b> is <b>amperometric<\/b> (current). \"Clark measures oxygen with Current.\"<\/div>\n\n<h3>Cell-counting and microbiology instruments<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Flow cytometry<\/b>: cells flow single file past a laser. <b>Forward scatter<\/b> reflects size, <b>side scatter<\/b> reflects granularity, and fluorescent antibodies identify CD markers. Uses: leukemia/lymphoma typing, CD4 counts, PNH, stem cell counts.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hematology analyzers<\/b>: <b>impedance (Coulter)<\/b> counts and sizes cells by the pulse each cell makes in an electric current; <b>optical scatter<\/b> and dyes produce the differential.<\/li>\n<li><b>Automated microbiology<\/b>: continuous-monitoring blood culture systems (detect CO2 from growth), ID and susceptibility cards, and MALDI-TOF.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>PCR: copying DNA<\/h3>\n<p>The <b>polymerase chain reaction<\/b> makes millions of copies of one short DNA target so it can be detected. Each cycle has three temperature steps:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Denaturation (about 95 °C)<\/b>: heat splits the double-stranded DNA into single strands.<\/li>\n<li><b>Annealing (about 50-65 °C)<\/b>: cooling lets short <b>primers<\/b> stick to each end of the target sequence. The primers define what gets copied.<\/li>\n<li><b>Extension (72 °C)<\/b>: a heat-stable DNA polymerase (<b>Taq<\/b>) builds new strands from the primers, using free nucleotides.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Each cycle <b>doubles<\/b> the copies, so after 30 cycles one target becomes about a billion (2³⁰) copies.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: the PCR order<\/b>\"<b>D<\/b>on't <b>A<\/b>sk <b>E<\/b>veryone\": <b>D<\/b>enature, <b>A<\/b>nneal, <b>E<\/b>xtend (hot, cool, warm).<\/div>\n<p>Variations:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>RT-PCR<\/b>: <b>reverse transcriptase<\/b> first converts <b>RNA into DNA<\/b> (cDNA), so RNA viruses like <b>HIV and hepatitis C<\/b> can be amplified.<\/li>\n<li><b>Real-time (quantitative) PCR<\/b>: a fluorescent signal is measured <b>every cycle<\/b>. The more target at the start, the fewer cycles it takes to cross the threshold, which gives a <b>quantity<\/b> (viral load).<\/li>\n<li><b>NAAT<\/b> is the general term for nucleic acid amplification tests, such as the urine/swab tests for <b>Chlamydia and gonorrhoea<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: PCR contamination<\/b>PCR is so sensitive that one stray copy of a previous product (<b>amplicon carryover<\/b>) can make a negative sample positive. Prevention: <b>one-way workflow<\/b> through separate rooms for reagent prep, sample prep and amplification/detection; filter pipette tips; UNG enzyme; and a <b>no-template (negative) control<\/b> in every run. If the negative control amplifies, the run is invalid.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Other molecular methods<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>FISH<\/b> (fluorescence in situ hybridisation): fluorescent DNA probes bind specific chromosome regions in cells, showing translocations or extra copies (BCR-ABL, HER2, PML-RARA).<\/li>\n<li><b>Sequencing<\/b>: reads the exact order of DNA bases (Sanger, or next-generation sequencing for many genes at once). <b>16S rRNA sequencing<\/b> identifies bacteria.<\/li>\n<li><b>Blotting<\/b>: <b>Southern<\/b> = <b>DNA<\/b>, <b>Northern<\/b> = <b>RNA<\/b>, <b>Western<\/b> = <b>protein<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: blots<\/b>\"<b>SNOW DROP<\/b>\": <b>S<\/b>outhern = <b>D<\/b>NA, <b>N<\/b>orthern = <b>R<\/b>NA, <b>O<\/b> (nothing), <b>W<\/b>estern = <b>P<\/b>rotein.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which electrode measures pO2, and what does it measure electrically?<\/summary><p>The Clark electrode; it measures current (amperometry).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>What are the three steps of a PCR cycle, in order?<\/summary><p>Denaturation (~95 °C), annealing (~50-65 °C), extension (72 °C).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A PCR no-template control shows amplification. What does it mean?<\/summary><p>Contamination (usually amplicon carryover). The run is invalid.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which blot detects RNA?<\/summary><p>Northern blot.<\/p><\/details>"},"L5":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A lab isn't just instruments and tests; it is people, money, records and responsibility. This lesson covers how labs keep quality across the whole testing process, manage staff, equipment, reagents and budgets, handle information and ethics, and teach others. Questions here are mostly common-sense \"best practice\" scenarios, so understanding the <i>purpose<\/i> of each rule is the key.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Quality across the whole testing process<\/h3>\n<p>A result can go wrong at any of three phases, and quality assurance (QA) watches all of them:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Preanalytic<\/b> (before testing): ordering, patient ID, collection, labelling, transport. <b>Most errors happen here.<\/b><\/li>\n<li><b>Analytic<\/b> (the testing itself): calibration, QC, instrument function, technique.<\/li>\n<li><b>Postanalytic<\/b> (after testing): result entry and transcription, reporting, interpretation, critical value calls.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>QA is bigger than QC. It also includes competency assessment, equipment maintenance, proficiency testing, corrective action, document control and monitoring of quality indicators (turnaround times, rejection rates).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Critical values<\/h3>\n<p>A critical (panic) value is a result so abnormal that the patient may be in danger unless someone acts now (for example potassium 6.8, glucose 40, a positive blood culture). It must be:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Called promptly<\/b> to the responsible clinician or caregiver (not left in the system for someone to find).<\/li>\n<li><b>Read back<\/b> by the person receiving it, to confirm it was heard correctly.<\/li>\n<li><b>Documented<\/b>: who was told, the date and time, the result, and the read-back.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Quality improvement tools<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>PDCA (Plan-Do-Check-Act)<\/b>: plan a change, try it on a small scale, check the results, then act (adopt it, adjust it, or drop it). Repeat.<\/li>\n<li><b>Root-cause analysis (RCA)<\/b>: after an error, keep asking \"why?\" until you reach the underlying system problem, rather than stopping at \"someone made a mistake\".<\/li>\n<li><b>Fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram<\/b>: sorts possible causes into categories (people, methods, equipment, materials, environment) on the \"bones\" of a fish.<\/li>\n<li><b>Pareto chart<\/b>: a bar chart of problems from most to least frequent, showing the \"vital few\" causes behind most of the trouble (the 80/20 rule).<\/li>\n<li><b>Incident (occurrence) reports<\/b> exist to <b>find and fix system problems<\/b>, not to blame or punish individuals.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>People: competency, management and evaluation<\/h3>\n<p><b>Competency assessment<\/b> (CLIA) proves each person can perform each test correctly: for new staff <b>at 6 months and 12 months<\/b> (twice in the first year), then <b>annually<\/b>. It uses <b>six elements<\/b>: direct observation of testing; monitoring how results are recorded and reported; reviewing worksheets, QC and maintenance records; direct observation of instrument maintenance; testing known samples (blind or PT samples); and problem-solving exercises.<\/p>\n<p><b>Leadership styles<\/b>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Autocratic<\/b>: the leader decides alone. Useful in emergencies, but staff may feel excluded.<\/li>\n<li><b>Democratic (participative)<\/b>: the group helps decide. Builds buy-in; slower.<\/li>\n<li><b>Laissez-faire<\/b>: hands-off. Works only with highly skilled, self-motivated teams.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Human resources<\/b>: clear <b>job descriptions<\/b>, orientation and training, <b>annual performance reviews<\/b>, and <b>progressive discipline<\/b> (verbal warning, written warning, suspension, termination) that is documented at each step.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Documents, equipment and reagents<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Procedure manuals<\/b>: each test's procedure states its purpose, principle, specimen requirements, reagents, step-by-step procedure, calculations, QC, reference ranges, limitations and references. Reviewed at least <b>annually<\/b> by the director, and old versions are archived.<\/li>\n<li><b>Instruments<\/b>: daily, weekly and monthly <b>maintenance logs<\/b>; verify performance after repairs; <b>calibration verification at least every 6 months<\/b> (and after major maintenance, reagent lot changes or QC problems).<\/li>\n<li><b>Reagents<\/b>: label with identity, concentration, <b>date prepared or opened<\/b>, <b>expiry<\/b>, storage requirements and the preparer's <b>initials<\/b>. Test each <b>new lot<\/b> with QC before using it on patients. Use <b>first in, first out (FIFO)<\/b>, and never use expired reagents, even if QC passes.<\/li>\n<li><b>Point-of-care testing (POCT)<\/b>: tests at the bedside (glucose meters, blood gases). The lab oversees operator training, QC and competency. Know each device's limits: for example, an abnormal hematocrit can bias some glucose meters.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Information systems, privacy and ethics<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>LIS<\/b> (laboratory information system) handles orders, specimen tracking, instrument interfaces, results, <b>autoverification<\/b> rules (automatically releasing results that pass defined checks) and an <b>audit trail<\/b> of every change.<\/li>\n<li><b>Corrected reports<\/b>: clearly marked \"corrected\", the <b>original result kept<\/b> on record, and the clinician notified.<\/li>\n<li><b>HIPAA<\/b> protects patient health information. Share only the <b>minimum necessary<\/b> information, only with people <b>authorised<\/b> and involved in the patient's care. Never discuss patients in public places or give results to unverified callers.<\/li>\n<li><b>Professional ethics<\/b>: keep confidentiality, report honestly, <b>never falsify or \"dry-lab\" results<\/b>, admit and report errors, and work within your scope of practice.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Money: budgets, costs and break-even<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Direct costs<\/b> belong to a specific test: reagents, supplies, labour time. <b>Indirect (overhead) costs<\/b> are shared: rent, utilities, administration.<\/li>\n<li><b>Fixed costs<\/b> stay the same however many tests you run (instrument lease, salaries). <b>Variable costs<\/b> rise with each test (reagents).<\/li>\n<li><b>Cost per test<\/b> = sum of the costs per test. Reagents $1.50 + labour $0.75 + overhead $0.25 = <b>$2.50<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Break-even point<\/b> (number of tests needed to cover costs) = <b>fixed costs ÷ (price per test − variable cost per test)<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Productivity<\/b> is tracked as tests (or billable tests) per <b>FTE<\/b> (one full-time equivalent = one person working full time).<\/li>\n<li><b>Inventory<\/b>: FIFO, minimum (par) levels for reordering, and tracking of expiry dates.<\/li>\n<li><b>Adding a new test<\/b>: compare cost, turnaround time, accuracy and clinical usefulness against sending it to a reference lab.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>An instrument has fixed costs of $50,000 a year. Each test sells for $15 and costs $5 in reagents. Break-even = 50,000 ÷ (15 − 5) = <b>5,000 tests<\/b> a year.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Education principles<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Adults learn best<\/b> (andragogy) when the material is relevant to their work, practical and partly self-directed.<\/li>\n<li><b>Learning objectives<\/b> should be <b>measurable<\/b>, using action verbs: \"the student will <b>calculate<\/b> a corrected WBC\", not \"will understand\".<\/li>\n<li><b>Three learning domains<\/b>: <b>cognitive<\/b> (knowledge and thinking: explaining Westgard rules), <b>psychomotor<\/b> (physical skills: venipuncture, pipetting), <b>affective<\/b> (attitudes and values: professionalism, respecting confidentiality).<\/li>\n<li><b>Teaching a skill<\/b>: explain, <b>demonstrate<\/b>, let the learner <b>practise under supervision<\/b>, then give <b>prompt, specific feedback<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Evaluate<\/b> with written questions (cognitive), direct observation and checklists (psychomotor), and observed behaviour (affective).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: learning domains<\/b><b>C<\/b>ognitive = <b>C<\/b>ranium (head knowledge). <b>P<\/b>sychomotor = <b>P<\/b>hysical (hands). <b>A<\/b>ffective = <b>A<\/b>ttitude (heart).<\/div>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A potassium of 6.9 must be reported. What three things are required?<\/summary><p>Call it promptly to the responsible caregiver, get a read-back, and document who was told, when, and the result.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>When must a new employee's competency be assessed?<\/summary><p>At 6 months and 12 months in the first year, then annually.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A student practising venipuncture is learning in which domain?<\/summary><p>Psychomotor.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A reagent expired yesterday but QC is in range. Can it be used?<\/summary><p>No. Expired reagents must not be used for patient testing, even if QC passes.<\/p><\/details>"},"L6":{"min":3,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Lab operations traps test whether you know the <b>right action<\/b>, not just the right fact. The wrong answers are usually tempting shortcuts: repeating QC until it passes, changing the mean, using a convenient but wrong dilution. Each listed trap is explained below, followed by more classics.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 1: treating 1-2s as a rejection<\/h3>\n<p>A single control outside ±2 SD happens by chance about once in 20 runs, so <b>1-2s is only a warning<\/b>. It tells you to look harder at the other rules. Reject the run for <b>1-3s or R-4s<\/b> (random error) or <b>2-2s, 4-1s or 10x<\/b> (systematic error).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 2: \"rerun QC until it passes\"<\/h3>\n<p>When QC fails, the correct sequence is: <b>stop reporting<\/b>, <b>find and fix the cause<\/b> (reagent, calibration, instrument), <b>rerun controls<\/b>, then <b>review patient results<\/b> since the last good QC. Simply rerunning the same control until one lands in range hides real problems and may release wrong patient results.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 3: the wrong bleach<\/h3>\n<p>Spills of blood are disinfected with a <b>1:10 dilution of household bleach<\/b> (about 0.5% sodium hypochlorite), made <b>fresh daily<\/b>, because diluted bleach loses strength. Leave it on for at least 10 minutes. Concentrated bleach is not \"better\"; it is a chemical hazard. 70% alcohol is not adequate for blood spills.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 4: getting help with proficiency testing<\/h3>\n<p>PT samples must be handled exactly like patient samples, by the people who normally test patients. <b>Never<\/b> send them to another lab, compare results with other labs before the deadline, or retest them more than a patient sample would be. That is a serious CLIA violation (PT referral), even if the intent was just to \"check\".<\/p>\n\n<h3>More lab operations traps<\/h3>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Changing the QC mean<\/b>Never shift the mean or widen limits to make QC pass. New control lots get their mean from at least 20 runs alongside the old lot.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Expired reagents<\/b>An expired reagent can't be used for patients even if QC is in range.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Dilution arithmetic<\/b>0.5 mL serum + 4.5 mL diluent is a 1:10 dilution, not 1:9. The total volume is the denominator.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Order of draw<\/b>Light blue comes before red/gold, then green, lavender, gray. EDTA (lavender) drawn before chemistry raises potassium and lowers calcium.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Patient identification<\/b>Room and bed number are never an identifier. Use two independent identifiers.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Needlestick first aid<\/b>Wash with soap and water. Don't squeeze, and don't apply bleach to the wound. Report immediately.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Fire extinguisher classes<\/b>Never use water on a Class C (electrical) fire.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Most errors<\/b>The preanalytic phase (identification, collection, transport) causes the most lab errors, not the analytic phase.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Critical values<\/b>Calling and documenting is required; leaving a critical result in the computer for the clinician to find is not acceptable.<\/div>\n\n<h3>How to approach a lab operations trap<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Ask what <b>protects the patient<\/b> first. Holding results, recollecting, and calling the clinician usually beat speed or convenience.<\/li>\n<li>Prefer the answer that <b>finds and fixes the cause<\/b> over one that just makes the numbers look right.<\/li>\n<li>Remember that <b>documentation<\/b> is part of every correct action.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>One control is at +2.3 SD and all other rules pass. Accept or reject?<\/summary><p>Accept the run. It is a 1-2s warning only, so look closely at the other rules and the next runs.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>QC fails after a new reagent lot is loaded. What is the correct action?<\/summary><p>Don't report patient results; troubleshoot (recalibrate, check the reagent), repeat QC, then review affected patient results and document.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A coworker suggests sending a PT sample to a sister lab to \"double-check\" before reporting. Is this allowed?<\/summary><p>No. Referring PT samples is a serious CLIA violation.<\/p><\/details>"},"M1":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Microbiology is detective work. You start with a specimen and a few clues (what the organism looks like under the microscope, what it grows on, and how it reacts to a few quick tests) and narrow it down step by step. This lesson gives you the toolkit: stains, culture media and the basic identification tests. Every later micro lesson builds on it.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The 8 big ideas, explained<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Gram stain order: crystal violet, iodine, decolorizer, safranin.<\/b> Too much decolorizer washes the purple out of Gram-positives, so they falsely look pink.<\/li>\n<li><b>Catalase separates Staph (positive) from Strep (negative); coagulase identifies S. aureus.<\/b><\/li>\n<li><b>Strep identification<\/b> uses a few disks and tests: bacitracin (group A), CAMP (group B), optochin (S. pneumoniae), bile esculin (Enterococcus and group D).<\/li>\n<li><b>Oxidase splits Gram-negative rods.<\/b> Enterobacterales are oxidase negative; Pseudomonas, Neisseria, Vibrio and Campylobacter are oxidase positive.<\/li>\n<li><b>MacConkey agar<\/b>: pink colonies ferment lactose (E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterobacter); colorless colonies don't (Salmonella, Shigella, Proteus, Pseudomonas).<\/li>\n<li><b>Haemophilus needs X and V factors<\/b>, supplied by chocolate agar.<\/li>\n<li><b>Malaria: thick smear to find, thin smear to identify.<\/b><\/li>\n<li><b>Never refrigerate CSF.<\/b> Neisseria meningitidis and H. influenzae die in the cold.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>How the Gram stain works<\/h3>\n<p>Bacteria have one of two wall designs, and the Gram stain exploits the difference:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Gram-positive<\/b> bacteria have a <b>thick peptidoglycan wall<\/b>. Crystal violet and iodine form a large complex that gets trapped in the thick wall. Alcohol dehydrates the wall and seals the dye in, so they stay <b>purple<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Gram-negative<\/b> bacteria have a <b>thin peptidoglycan layer plus an outer lipid membrane<\/b>. Alcohol dissolves the lipid membrane and the dye washes out. The red counterstain (<b>safranin<\/b>) then makes them <b>pink<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Steps: <b>crystal violet<\/b> (primary stain) → <b>iodine<\/b> (mordant, locks the dye) → <b>alcohol/acetone<\/b> (decolorizer, the critical step) → <b>safranin<\/b> (counterstain).<\/p>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: wrong Gram reactions<\/b><b>Over-decolorizing<\/b>, <b>old cultures<\/b> (worn-out walls) and antibiotic-treated cells make Gram-positives look pink. <b>Under-decolorizing<\/b> or a smear that is too thick makes Gram-negatives look purple. Always check the control organisms on the slide.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b>\"<b>P<\/b>urple <b>P<\/b>ositive\" (thick wall holds the purple). Gram-negatives \"lose\" the purple, then turn pink.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Other stains and when to use them<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Acid-fast stains<\/b>: mycobacteria have waxy <b>mycolic acid<\/b> in their wall that resists ordinary stains. Carbolfuchsin is driven in with heat (<b>Ziehl-Neelsen, \"hot\"<\/b>) or a stronger detergent (<b>Kinyoun, \"cold\"<\/b>). Once in, acid-alcohol can't remove it, so they stay red (\"acid-fast\").<\/li>\n<li><b>Modified acid-fast<\/b> uses a <b>weaker acid<\/b> (1% sulfuric acid) for organisms that are only partly acid-fast: <b>Nocardia<\/b>, <b>Cryptosporidium<\/b>, <b>Cyclospora<\/b>, <b>Cystoisospora<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Auramine-rhodamine<\/b>: a fluorescent acid-fast stain. More sensitive and quicker to scan, so it is used to <b>screen<\/b>; positives are confirmed with a traditional stain.<\/li>\n<li><b>India ink<\/b>: makes the dark background light up around the clear <b>capsule<\/b> of Cryptococcus in CSF. (The antigen test is more sensitive.)<\/li>\n<li><b>KOH and calcofluor white<\/b>: KOH dissolves human cells so fungal elements stand out; calcofluor binds fungal cell walls and fluoresces.<\/li>\n<li><b>Lactophenol cotton blue<\/b>: stains fungal structures for identification.<\/li>\n<li><b>Giemsa<\/b>: blood parasites (malaria, Babesia), Chlamydia inclusions, Histoplasma inside cells.<\/li>\n<li><b>Acridine orange<\/b>: fluorescent stain that finds bacteria in blood cultures or specimens when the Gram stain is hard to read.<\/li>\n<li><b>Trichrome<\/b>: intestinal protozoa in stool.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Culture media: three types<\/h3>\n<p>Media fall into three groups, and many plates are two at once:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Enriched<\/b> media feed fussy (fastidious) organisms. <b>Blood agar<\/b> grows most bacteria. <b>Chocolate agar<\/b> is blood agar heated until the red cells burst, releasing <b>X factor (hemin)<\/b> and <b>V factor (NAD)<\/b> for Haemophilus and Neisseria.<\/li>\n<li><b>Selective<\/b> media contain inhibitors that stop unwanted organisms. For example, <b>MacConkey<\/b> has bile salts and crystal violet that stop Gram-positives.<\/li>\n<li><b>Differential<\/b> media make different organisms look different. <b>MacConkey<\/b> has lactose and a pH indicator: fermenters make acid and turn pink.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Hemolysis on blood agar<\/b> is your first clue for streptococci: <b>alpha<\/b> = partial, greenish (hemoglobin oxidised); <b>beta<\/b> = clear, complete lysis; <b>gamma<\/b> = none.<\/p>\n<p><b>Plates to remember for specific organisms<\/b> (tie each to its reason):<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>EMB<\/b>: E. coli gives a <b>green metallic sheen<\/b> (strong lactose fermentation).<\/li>\n<li><b>Hektoen, XLD, SS<\/b>: stool pathogens. <b>H2S<\/b> production gives <b>black colonies<\/b> (Salmonella).<\/li>\n<li><b>CIN<\/b>: Yersinia enterocolitica, \"<b>bull's-eye<\/b>\" colonies.<\/li>\n<li><b>Thayer-Martin / Martin-Lewis<\/b>: chocolate agar with antibiotics to select <b>N. gonorrhoeae<\/b> from contaminated genital sites.<\/li>\n<li><b>Campy-BAP<\/b> at <b>42 °C<\/b>, microaerophilic: Campylobacter (other gut flora don't like 42 °C).<\/li>\n<li><b>TCBS<\/b>: Vibrio; <b>V. cholerae yellow<\/b> (ferments sucrose), V. parahaemolyticus green.<\/li>\n<li><b>Mannitol salt<\/b>: high salt selects staph; S. aureus ferments mannitol and turns it <b>yellow<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Bile esculin<\/b>: Enterococcus and group D strep hydrolyse esculin in bile and turn the agar <b>black<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Regan-Lowe, Bordet-Gengou<\/b>: Bordetella pertussis. <b>Loeffler, Tinsdale<\/b> (tellurite): Corynebacterium diphtheriae.<\/li>\n<li><b>Löwenstein-Jensen, Middlebrook<\/b>: mycobacteria. <b>Sabouraud dextrose<\/b>: fungi. <b>BCYE<\/b>: Legionella (needs <b>cysteine and iron<\/b>).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>The basic identification tests, and why they work<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Catalase<\/b>: breaks hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen, which makes bubbles. Staph are positive; strep and enterococci negative. Don't pick up blood agar: red cells contain catalase and cause false positives.<\/li>\n<li><b>Coagulase<\/b>: S. aureus makes an enzyme that clots plasma. The slide test detects bound coagulase (clumping factor); the tube test detects free coagulase (read at 4 hours and again at 24 hours).<\/li>\n<li><b>Oxidase<\/b>: detects cytochrome c oxidase, giving a purple color in seconds. Use a platinum, wooden or plastic loop: iron (nichrome) loops give false positives.<\/li>\n<li><b>PYR<\/b>: positive (red) for <b>group A strep<\/b> and <b>Enterococcus<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Urease<\/b>: splits urea into ammonia (pink). Rapid in <b>Proteus<\/b>, <b>H. pylori<\/b>, <b>Cryptococcus<\/b>, Brucella.<\/li>\n<li><b>Indole<\/b>: tryptophanase breaks tryptophan into indole, giving a red ring with Kovac reagent. <b>E. coli<\/b> and <b>Proteus vulgaris<\/b> are positive.<\/li>\n<li><b>Bile solubility<\/b>: <b>S. pneumoniae<\/b> carries an autolysin that bile switches on, so the colony dissolves.<\/li>\n<li><b>DNase<\/b>: S. aureus, Serratia, Moraxella.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hippurate hydrolysis<\/b>: group B strep and Campylobacter jejuni.<\/li>\n<li><b>Nitrate reduction<\/b>: almost all Enterobacterales positive (this is also why the urine nitrite test works).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Gram-positive cocci appear pink in a smear from an old culture. What happened?<\/summary><p>Old cells have damaged walls that lose the crystal violet, so they stain Gram-variable or pink. Over-decolorization causes the same thing. Use a fresh culture.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why does chocolate agar grow Haemophilus but blood agar usually doesn't?<\/summary><p>Heating lyses the red cells, releasing X factor (hemin) and V factor (NAD). Intact blood agar also contains enzymes that destroy V factor.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A catalase test on colonies picked from blood agar bubbles weakly. Why be careful?<\/summary><p>Red cells carried over from the agar contain catalase and can give a false positive.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which stain is used to screen a sputum for mycobacteria quickly and sensitively?<\/summary><p>Auramine-rhodamine (fluorescent acid-fast). Positives are confirmed with Ziehl-Neelsen or Kinyoun.<\/p><\/details>"},"M10":{"min":3,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Microbiology traps mostly hit look-alikes: organisms that share a hemolysis pattern, a colony color or a stain reaction but differ in one key test. Below, each listed trap is explained, followed by more classic micro traps and a quick way to avoid them.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 1: \"alpha-hemolytic strep = viridans\"<\/h3>\n<p><b>S. pneumoniae<\/b> is alpha-hemolytic too. Two tests separate it from viridans strep: <b>optochin<\/b> (pneumococcus is sensitive) and <b>bile solubility<\/b> (pneumococcus dissolves, thanks to its autolysin). If the optochin zone is borderline (for example after incubating in CO2), bile solubility settles it. A Gram stain with lancet-shaped diplococci and \"dimpled\" colonies are supporting clues.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 2: Salmonella and Shigella look the same on MacConkey<\/h3>\n<p>Both are <b>lactose negative<\/b> (colorless). To tell them apart, use <b>motility<\/b> and <b>H2S<\/b>: Salmonella is motile and H2S positive (black on Hektoen, XLD or TSI); Shigella is non-motile and H2S negative. Remember: Salmonella \"swims and stinks\".<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 3: \"acid-fast = Mycobacterium\"<\/h3>\n<p>Acid-fast stains also pick up other organisms. <b>Nocardia<\/b> is <b>weakly<\/b> (partially) acid-fast with the modified stain. <b>Cryptosporidium<\/b>, <b>Cyclospora<\/b> and <b>Cystoisospora<\/b> oocysts in stool are acid-fast too. Always consider the specimen and the organism's size and shape: a branching rod in a lung abscess is likely Nocardia, and round 4-6 µm oocysts in stool are Cryptosporidium.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 4: the thick smear names the malaria species<\/h3>\n<p>It doesn't. The <b>thick smear<\/b> concentrates blood to <b>find<\/b> parasites, but the red cells are lysed, so you can't judge cell size, Schüffner dots or cell shape. The <b>thin smear<\/b> keeps red cells intact and is used for <b>species<\/b> and <b>parasite percentage<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>More microbiology traps<\/h3>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Over-decolorized Gram stain<\/b>Gram-positive organisms that look pink may just be over-decolorized, old, or antibiotic-treated. Check the slide's control organisms before trusting a surprising Gram reaction.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Catalase from blood agar<\/b>Red cells carried over from blood agar contain catalase and can make a streptococcus look catalase positive.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Nichrome loop for oxidase<\/b>Iron-containing loops oxidise the reagent and give false positives. Use a platinum, plastic or wooden applicator.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Refrigerated CSF<\/b>Fastidious meningitis organisms die in the cold, giving a false-negative culture. CSF stays at room temperature and is processed at once.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Coagulase-negative staph in one bottle<\/b>Usually a skin contaminant, not sepsis. Look for the same organism in more than one set before calling it significant.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Enterococcus and cephalosporins<\/b>Intrinsically resistant. Never report a cephalosporin as an option for Enterococcus.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>B. anthracis vs B. cereus<\/b>Anthracis is non-motile and non-hemolytic; cereus is motile and beta-hemolytic. A non-motile, non-hemolytic large Gram-positive rod means stop and refer.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Cryptococcus antigen negative but India ink full of yeast<\/b>Extremely high antigen can cause a prozone (false negative). Dilute the CSF and repeat.<\/div>\n\n<h3>How to approach a microbiology trap<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Start from the Gram stain and the key first-split tests (catalase, oxidase, lactose, hemolysis). Most traps live at those splits.<\/li>\n<li>Ask what else shares that feature, and which single test separates them.<\/li>\n<li>Check the specimen and how it was handled: temperature, delay, container and contamination.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>An alpha-hemolytic coccus shows a small optochin zone after CO2 incubation. Next step?<\/summary><p>Bile solubility test. If the colony dissolves, it is S. pneumoniae.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A colorless MacConkey colony from stool is motile and turns TSI black. Salmonella or Shigella?<\/summary><p>Salmonella (motile, H2S positive).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A modified acid-fast stain of a lung abscess shows thin branching rods that are partly red. Likely organism?<\/summary><p>Nocardia.<\/p><\/details>"},"M2":{"min":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Gram-positive cocci are some of the most common organisms you will meet: staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci. Identifying them is a decision tree. Each test splits the group in two, and you only need a few branches to name almost any isolate. Learn the order of the questions and the answers follow.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Step 1: clusters or chains? Catalase answers it<\/h3>\n<p>On a Gram stain, <b>staphylococci<\/b> grow in grape-like <b>clusters<\/b>, and <b>streptococci and enterococci<\/b> grow in <b>pairs and chains<\/b>. Shapes on a smear can be unclear, so confirm with <b>catalase<\/b>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Catalase positive<\/b> (bubbles) → <b>Staphylococcus<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Catalase negative<\/b> → <b>Streptococcus<\/b> or <b>Enterococcus<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>The staphylococci<\/h3>\n<p>Next question: <b>coagulase<\/b>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>S. aureus: coagulase positive.<\/b> The big pathogen: skin abscesses, wound infections, pneumonia, bone and blood infections, toxic shock and food poisoning. Also <b>mannitol positive<\/b> (yellow on mannitol salt agar), often beta-hemolytic with golden colonies, and DNase positive. <b>MRSA<\/b> carries the <b>mecA<\/b> gene, which codes for an altered penicillin-binding protein (PBP2a) that beta-lactam drugs can't bind.<\/li>\n<li><b>Coagulase-negative staphylococci (CoNS)<\/b> are mostly skin flora. To split the two you need to know, use <b>novobiocin<\/b>:\n <ul>\n <li><b>S. epidermidis: novobiocin sensitive.<\/b> Lives on skin; the usual blood culture <b>contaminant<\/b>, but a real pathogen on plastic devices (IV lines, prosthetic joints, heart valves) because it forms a biofilm.<\/li>\n <li><b>S. saprophyticus: novobiocin resistant.<\/b> Causes <b>urinary tract infections in young, sexually active women<\/b>.<\/li>\n <\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b>\"<b>S<\/b>aprophyticus is <b>S<\/b>tubborn\": it resists novobiocin. \"<b>E<\/b>pidermidis is <b>E<\/b>asy\": it is sensitive.<\/div>\n\n<h3>The streptococci: start with hemolysis<\/h3>\n<p>Look at the plate first. The type of hemolysis on sheep blood agar splits strep into groups:<\/p>\n<p><b>Beta-hemolytic (clear zones):<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>S. pyogenes (group A)<\/b>: <b>bacitracin sensitive<\/b> and <b>PYR positive<\/b>. Causes strep throat, impetigo, cellulitis, necrotising fasciitis, and later complications: <b>rheumatic fever<\/b> and <b>post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>S. agalactiae (group B)<\/b>: <b>bacitracin resistant<\/b>, <b>CAMP positive<\/b> (its CAMP factor teams up with S. aureus beta-toxin to make an arrowhead-shaped zone of hemolysis), and <b>hippurate positive<\/b>. Lives in the vagina and causes <b>newborn sepsis and meningitis<\/b>, which is why pregnant women are screened at 35-37 weeks.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Alpha-hemolytic (green):<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>S. pneumoniae<\/b>: <b>optochin sensitive<\/b> and <b>bile soluble<\/b>. Gram stain shows <b>lancet-shaped diplococci<\/b> (pointed pairs); colonies may look \"dimpled\" in the middle as the cells break themselves down. The top cause of pneumonia, otitis media and adult meningitis. Its capsule causes disease and swells with specific antiserum (the <b>Quellung reaction<\/b>).<\/li>\n<li><b>Viridans streptococci<\/b>: <b>optochin resistant<\/b> and <b>bile insoluble<\/b>. Normal mouth flora; after dental work they can seed damaged heart valves and cause <b>subacute endocarditis<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why optochin and bile solubility both work for S. pneumoniae<\/b>S. pneumoniae carries an <b>autolysin<\/b>, a self-destruct enzyme. Bile salts trigger it, so the colony dissolves. Optochin disrupts the organism's membrane enzyme (ATPase), so it is killed around the disk. Viridans strep lack both weak spots.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: optochin in CO2<\/b>Incubating the optochin disk in CO2 can shrink the zone, so a real pneumococcus may look resistant. When the zone is borderline, confirm with <b>bile solubility<\/b>.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Enterococcus vs group D strep<\/h3>\n<p>Both are usually non-hemolytic (gamma) and both are <b>bile esculin positive<\/b> (they grow in bile and turn esculin agar black). Two tests separate them:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Enterococcus<\/b> (E. faecalis, E. faecium): grows in <b>6.5% NaCl<\/b> and is <b>PYR positive<\/b>. Causes UTIs, wound infections and endocarditis, and is naturally resistant to many drugs. <b>VRE<\/b> = vancomycin-resistant enterococci, a major hospital problem.<\/li>\n<li><b>S. bovis group<\/b> (S. gallolyticus): bile esculin positive but <b>no growth in 6.5% NaCl<\/b>. A blood infection with S. bovis is linked to <b>colon cancer<\/b>, so the patient needs a colonoscopy.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>The whole tree in one view<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Catalase +<\/b> → Staph → coagulase + = <b>S. aureus<\/b>; coagulase − → novobiocin S = <b>S. epidermidis<\/b>, R = <b>S. saprophyticus<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Catalase −<\/b> → look at hemolysis:\n <ul>\n <li><b>Beta<\/b> → bacitracin S / PYR + = <b>group A<\/b>; CAMP + / hippurate + = <b>group B<\/b>.<\/li>\n <li><b>Alpha<\/b> → optochin S / bile soluble = <b>S. pneumoniae<\/b>; resistant = <b>viridans<\/b>.<\/li>\n <li><b>Gamma<\/b> → bile esculin + → 6.5% NaCl + / PYR + = <b>Enterococcus<\/b>; NaCl − = <b>S. bovis group<\/b>.<\/li>\n <\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Urine from a 20-year-old woman grows catalase-positive, coagulase-negative cocci resistant to novobiocin. Identify.<\/summary><p>Staphylococcus saprophyticus.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A beta-hemolytic coccus from a newborn's blood is bacitracin resistant and CAMP positive. Identify.<\/summary><p>Streptococcus agalactiae (group B).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A gamma-hemolytic, bile esculin-positive coccus grows in 6.5% NaCl. Identify.<\/summary><p>Enterococcus.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>S. bovis group is isolated from a blood culture. What should the clinician consider?<\/summary><p>Colon cancer or other bowel disease; a colonoscopy is recommended.<\/p><\/details>"},"M3":{"min":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The Enterobacterales (once called Enterobacteriaceae) are the gut's Gram-negative rods: E. coli, Klebsiella, Salmonella, Shigella, Proteus and their relatives. They are the most common cause of urinary infections and many hospital infections. They look identical on a Gram stain, so you identify them by what they <i>do<\/i>: which sugars they ferment and which products they make.<\/p>\n\n<h3>What all Enterobacterales share<\/h3>\n<p>Every member is a Gram-negative rod that:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>is <b>oxidase negative<\/b> (this separates them from Pseudomonas, Vibrio and others),<\/li>\n<li><b>ferments glucose<\/b>,<\/li>\n<li><b>reduces nitrate to nitrite<\/b> (the reason a urine nitrite test detects them),<\/li>\n<li>grows well on MacConkey agar.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Step 1: lactose fermenter or not?<\/h3>\n<p>On <b>MacConkey<\/b> agar, lactose fermenters produce acid and turn <b>pink<\/b>; non-fermenters stay <b>colorless<\/b>. This is the first big split:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Lactose fermenters (pink):<\/b> E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterobacter, (Citrobacter, variable).<\/li>\n<li><b>Non-fermenters (colorless):<\/b> Salmonella, Shigella, Proteus, Yersinia, Serratia (late), and the non-Enterobacterales Pseudomonas.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The stool pathogens <b>Salmonella and Shigella are both lactose negative<\/b>, which is why stool cultures use lactose-containing plates to spot them among the pink E. coli.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The TSI tube: reading sugar fermentation<\/h3>\n<p>The triple sugar iron (TSI) slant has a little <b>glucose<\/b>, lots of <b>lactose and sucrose<\/b>, a pH indicator (acid = yellow, alkaline = red) and iron to detect H2S. Read the <b>slant / butt<\/b>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>A/A (yellow/yellow)<\/b>: ferments glucose <i>and<\/i> lactose or sucrose. There is so much sugar that the acid lasts everywhere (E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterobacter).<\/li>\n<li><b>K/A (red/yellow)<\/b>: ferments <b>glucose only<\/b>. The small amount of glucose is used up; the slant (with air) then breaks down protein and turns alkaline (red), while the butt stays acid (Salmonella, Shigella, Proteus).<\/li>\n<li><b>K/K (red/red)<\/b>: ferments nothing, so it is a <b>non-fermenter<\/b> such as Pseudomonas, and not an Enterobacterales.<\/li>\n<li><b>Black<\/b> butt = <b>H2S<\/b> (Salmonella, Proteus, Citrobacter). <b>Cracks or bubbles<\/b> = <b>gas<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>IMViC: four classic tests<\/h3>\n<p><b>I<\/b>ndole, <b>M<\/b>ethyl red, <b>V<\/b>oges-Proskauer, <b>C<\/b>itrate (the \"i\" is just for pronunciation):<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>E. coli = + + − −<\/b> (indole +, MR +, VP −, citrate −).<\/li>\n<li><b>Klebsiella and Enterobacter = − − + +<\/b> (the exact opposite).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why MR and VP are opposites<\/b>Both tests look at how glucose is fermented. <b>Methyl red<\/b> detects strong acids from \"mixed acid\" fermentation (E. coli). <b>VP<\/b> detects acetoin, a neutral product of the \"butylene glycol\" pathway (Klebsiella, Enterobacter). An organism usually uses one pathway or the other.<\/div>\n\n<h3>The main organisms<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>E. coli<\/b>: lactose +, indole +, citrate −. The <b>most common cause of UTIs<\/b>, plus neonatal meningitis and diarrhoea (see below). Green metallic sheen on EMB.<\/li>\n<li><b>Klebsiella pneumoniae<\/b>: very <b>mucoid<\/b> colonies (big capsule), lactose +, <b>non-motile<\/b>, indole −, VP +. Pneumonia (often in alcoholics), UTIs, hospital infections and carbapenemase producers (KPC).<\/li>\n<li><b>Enterobacter<\/b>: like Klebsiella but <b>motile<\/b>; citrate +, VP +. Often resistant through inducible AmpC beta-lactamase.<\/li>\n<li><b>Salmonella<\/b>: lactose −, <b>H2S +<\/b>, <b>motile<\/b>. Food poisoning (eggs, poultry); S. Typhi causes typhoid fever.<\/li>\n<li><b>Shigella<\/b>: lactose −, <b>non-motile<\/b>, <b>no H2S<\/b>, no gas. Bloody diarrhoea (dysentery) from a very small number of organisms.<\/li>\n<li><b>Proteus<\/b>: <b>swarms<\/b> across blood agar in waves, <b>urease +<\/b> (fast), H2S +, <b>PAD +<\/b> (phenylalanine deaminase). The urease raises urine pH and helps form struvite stones. P. mirabilis is indole −; P. vulgaris is indole +.<\/li>\n<li><b>Serratia<\/b>: may make a <b>red pigment<\/b>; DNase +, gelatinase +; hospital infections.<\/li>\n<li><b>Citrobacter<\/b>: H2S +, citrate +, lactose variable. Can be confused with Salmonella.<\/li>\n<li><b>Yersinia<\/b>: <b>Y. pestis<\/b> (plague) shows <b>bipolar \"safety pin\"<\/b> staining. <b>Y. enterocolitica<\/b> grows in the <b>cold<\/b> (survives refrigerated blood products and foods), is urease +, and gives bull's-eye colonies on CIN agar; causes diarrhoea and pain that mimics appendicitis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: Salmonella vs Shigella<\/b><b>S<\/b>almonella <b>S<\/b>wims (motile) and <b>S<\/b>tinks (H2S +). Shigella does neither: non-motile, H2S negative.<\/div>\n\n<h3>E. coli types that cause diarrhoea<\/h3>\n<p>Different E. coli strains carry different toxins or invasion tools, so they cause different illnesses:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>ETEC<\/b> (enterotoxigenic): <b>traveler's diarrhoea<\/b>. Heat-labile and heat-stable toxins pull water into the gut, giving watery diarrhoea.<\/li>\n<li><b>EPEC<\/b> (enteropathogenic): <b>infant diarrhoea<\/b> in developing countries.<\/li>\n<li><b>EIEC<\/b> (enteroinvasive): invades the gut wall like Shigella; often a slow lactose fermenter and non-motile, so it can be mistaken for Shigella.<\/li>\n<li><b>EHEC / STEC<\/b> (enterohemorrhagic, Shiga toxin-producing, mainly <b>O157:H7<\/b>): undercooked hamburger; <b>bloody diarrhoea without fever<\/b>; the Shiga toxin can cause <b>hemolytic uremic syndrome<\/b> (HUS) in children. Unlike most E. coli, O157:H7 <b>doesn't ferment sorbitol<\/b>, so it forms <b>colorless colonies on sorbitol-MacConkey (SMAC)<\/b>. Shiga toxin assays or PCR detect non-O157 strains.<\/li>\n<li><b>EAEC<\/b> (enteroaggregative): long-lasting diarrhoea.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A stool isolate is lactose negative, H2S negative, non-motile, and K/A on TSI with no gas. Identify.<\/summary><p>Shigella.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>An organism gives K/K on TSI. Is it an Enterobacterales?<\/summary><p>No. K/K means no glucose fermentation, which suggests a non-fermenter such as Pseudomonas. All Enterobacterales ferment glucose.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which E. coli is detected by colorless colonies on sorbitol-MacConkey?<\/summary><p>E. coli O157:H7 (EHEC/STEC), which doesn't ferment sorbitol.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A swarming, urease-positive urine isolate is indole negative. Which species?<\/summary><p>Proteus mirabilis (P. vulgaris is indole positive).<\/p><\/details>"},"M4":{"min":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">This lesson covers the Gram-negative organisms that are <i>not<\/i> Enterobacterales. That includes the non-fermenters (which don't ferment sugar at all), the fastidious rods that need special conditions to grow, and the Gram-negative cocci (Neisseria and Moraxella). The key skill is matching each organism to the special clue or culture condition that gives it away.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Non-fermenters: Pseudomonas and friends<\/h3>\n<p>Non-fermenters get energy by breathing oxygen, not by fermenting sugar, so they give <b>K/K on TSI<\/b>. Many are tough, environmental organisms that thrive in hospitals and resist antibiotics.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Pseudomonas aeruginosa<\/b>: <b>oxidase positive<\/b>, grows at <b>42 °C<\/b>, smells like <b>grapes<\/b> (or corn tortillas), and makes pigments: <b>pyocyanin<\/b> (blue) plus pyoverdin (yellow-green fluorescent), together <b>blue-green<\/b>. Often metallic, flat colonies with beta-hemolysis. It loves water and moist places, and infects <b>burns<\/b>, the lungs in <b>cystic fibrosis<\/b> (mucoid strains), ventilators, the eyes of contact lens wearers and swimmer's ear. It is naturally resistant to many antibiotics.<\/li>\n<li><b>Stenotrophomonas maltophilia<\/b>: <b>oxidase negative<\/b> (unusual for a non-fermenter), oxidises <b>maltose<\/b> (hence the name), and is naturally <b>resistant to carbapenems<\/b>. Usually treated with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole.<\/li>\n<li><b>Acinetobacter baumannii<\/b>: <b>oxidase negative<\/b>, <b>non-motile<\/b>, plump coccobacilli that can look Gram-variable. A notorious <b>multidrug-resistant<\/b> hospital organism (ICUs, ventilators, wounds).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: oxidase-negative non-fermenters<\/b>\"<b>S<\/b>teno and <b>A<\/b>cinetobacter <b>S<\/b>ay <b>no<\/b>\" to oxidase. Pseudomonas says yes.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Fastidious Gram-negative rods: each needs something special<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Haemophilus influenzae<\/b>: needs <b>X factor (hemin)<\/b> and <b>V factor (NAD)<\/b>. It grows on chocolate agar, and on blood agar only right next to S. aureus, which lyses red cells and releases V factor: <b>satellite growth<\/b>. Type b capsule (Hib) caused childhood meningitis before vaccination. H. ducreyi (chancroid) needs only X; H. parainfluenzae needs only V.<\/li>\n<li><b>Legionella pneumophila<\/b>: stains faintly or not at all with Gram stain, and grows only on <b>BCYE agar<\/b> (buffered charcoal yeast extract), which supplies <b>L-cysteine and iron<\/b>. It lives in water systems (cooling towers, showers) and causes Legionnaires' pneumonia. The <b>urine antigen test<\/b> detects serogroup 1, the most common.<\/li>\n<li><b>Bordetella pertussis<\/b>: whooping cough. Grows on <b>Regan-Lowe<\/b> (charcoal) or <b>Bordet-Gengou<\/b> agar. Collect a <b>nasopharyngeal swab with Dacron or calcium alginate<\/b>, because cotton contains fatty acids that kill it. PCR is now the main test.<\/li>\n<li><b>Campylobacter jejuni<\/b>: <b>curved, \"gull-wing\"<\/b> rods; oxidase +; hippurate +. Needs <b>42 °C<\/b> and <b>microaerophilic<\/b> conditions (about 5% O2). A leading cause of bacterial diarrhoea (undercooked poultry), linked to Guillain-Barré syndrome.<\/li>\n<li><b>Helicobacter pylori<\/b>: curved rod in the stomach. Very strong <b>urease<\/b> activity: it splits urea into ammonia to neutralise stomach acid around itself. That is the basis of the <b>urea breath test<\/b> (patient swallows labeled urea and labeled CO2 is breathed out). Stool antigen and biopsy tests also exist. Causes ulcers and stomach cancer.<\/li>\n<li><b>Vibrio cholerae<\/b>: curved rod, <b>oxidase +<\/b>, grows in alkaline conditions and salt-tolerant. Gives <b>yellow<\/b> colonies on <b>TCBS<\/b> (ferments sucrose). Cholera toxin causes massive watery \"<b>rice-water<\/b>\" stools. V. parahaemolyticus (seafood) is green on TCBS; V. vulnificus causes wound infections from seawater.<\/li>\n<li><b>Brucella<\/b> and <b>Francisella tularensis<\/b>: tiny coccobacilli from animals (unpasteurised dairy, rabbits). They are among the most common causes of <b>lab-acquired infection<\/b>, so suspected isolates must be handled in a <b>biological safety cabinet<\/b> and referred.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>The Gram-negative cocci<\/h3>\n<p>All are <b>oxidase positive<\/b> kidney-bean-shaped <b>diplococci<\/b>. Identify them by which sugars they use (they oxidise rather than ferment them, but the tests are traditionally called \"fermentation\"):<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>N. gonorrhoeae<\/b>: uses <b>glucose only<\/b>. Seen as diplococci <b>inside neutrophils<\/b> on a urethral smear. Grows on <b>Thayer-Martin<\/b> or Martin-Lewis (selective chocolate agar) in CO2. Very fragile: plate quickly, keep warm. NAAT is the usual test now.<\/li>\n<li><b>N. meningitidis<\/b>: uses <b>glucose and maltose<\/b>. Has a capsule; causes meningitis and sepsis with a purple rash, spreading in dorms and barracks. Handle cultures in a safety cabinet, since lab infections are dangerous.<\/li>\n<li><b>Moraxella catarrhalis<\/b>: uses no sugars; <b>DNase +<\/b>, <b>butyrate esterase +<\/b>; colonies slide across the agar like a \"hockey puck\". Causes ear infections and bronchitis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b><b>M<\/b>eningitidis uses <b>M<\/b>altose (and glucose). <b>G<\/b>onorrhoeae uses only <b>G<\/b>lucose.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A burn-wound isolate is oxidase positive, smells like grapes and grows at 42 °C. Identify.<\/summary><p>Pseudomonas aeruginosa.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A pneumonia patient's sputum Gram stain shows few organisms, but a urine antigen test is positive. Which organism, and what agar does it need?<\/summary><p>Legionella pneumophila (serogroup 1); BCYE agar with L-cysteine and iron.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why must a pertussis swab not be cotton?<\/summary><p>Cotton contains fatty acids that are toxic to Bordetella pertussis. Use Dacron, rayon or calcium alginate.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>An oxidase-positive diplococcus from CSF uses glucose and maltose. Identify.<\/summary><p>Neisseria meningitidis.<\/p><\/details>"},"M5":{"min":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Anaerobes are bacteria that can't survive in oxygen. They live in the gut, mouth and vagina, and cause infections when they get into deep tissue: abscesses, wound infections after bowel surgery, gas gangrene. This lesson also covers the Gram-positive rods (Listeria, Corynebacterium, Bacillus and the branching rods), which are easy to mix up if you don't anchor each one to its key clue.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Working with anaerobes<\/h3>\n<p>Because oxygen kills them, everything depends on the specimen:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Send <b>tissue or aspirated fluid<\/b> (pus drawn up in a syringe) in an <b>anaerobic transport<\/b> container. Swabs are poor.<\/li>\n<li><b>Reject<\/b> specimens that are always contaminated by normal anaerobic flora: expectorated sputum, voided urine, stool (except for C. difficile), and superficial skin swabs.<\/li>\n<li>Culture in an anaerobic jar, pouch or chamber.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Gram-negative anaerobes: Bacteroides fragilis<\/h3>\n<p><b>Bacteroides fragilis<\/b> is the most important anaerobe in clinical labs. It is the main anaerobe in abdominal abscesses after bowel leakage. Two clues identify it:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>It <b>grows in 20% bile<\/b> (bile resistant), which fits its home in the bowel.<\/li>\n<li>With special-potency identification disks, it is <b>resistant to all three: kanamycin, vancomycin and colistin<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Like all anaerobes, it is naturally resistant to <b>aminoglycosides<\/b>, because those drugs need oxygen-dependent transport to enter the cell. It often makes beta-lactamase.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The clostridia: spore-forming Gram-positive rods<\/h3>\n<p>Clostridia make <b>spores<\/b> that survive heat and drying, and most of their diseases come from powerful <b>toxins<\/b>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>C. perfringens<\/b>: large, <b>boxcar-shaped<\/b> rods that rarely show spores in the lab. Two clues: a <b>double zone of hemolysis<\/b> on blood agar (inner complete, outer partial) and a positive <b>Nagler (lecithinase) test<\/b>, from its alpha toxin, which breaks down cell membranes. \"<b>Stormy fermentation<\/b>\" in litmus milk. Causes <b>gas gangrene<\/b> and food poisoning.<\/li>\n<li><b>C. difficile<\/b>: makes <b>toxin A and toxin B<\/b> and causes <b>antibiotic-associated colitis<\/b> (antibiotics wipe out the normal gut flora and C. diff takes over). Test only <b>liquid or unformed stool<\/b>, using a toxin/GDH assay or <b>PCR for the toxin gene<\/b>. Formed stool is rejected: the patient may just be colonised.<\/li>\n<li><b>C. botulinum<\/b>: makes <b>botulinum neurotoxin<\/b>, which blocks acetylcholine release at nerve endings and causes <b>flaccid (floppy) paralysis<\/b>. Sources: home-canned food; honey in infants.<\/li>\n<li><b>C. tetani<\/b>: <b>terminal round spores<\/b> that make the cell look like a <b>drumstick or tennis racket<\/b>. Tetanus toxin causes <b>spastic<\/b> paralysis and lockjaw. Enters through puncture wounds.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: two paralysis toxins<\/b><b>B<\/b>otulinum makes you <b>B<\/b>loppy (flaccid). <b>T<\/b>etanus makes you <b>T<\/b>ight (spastic).<\/div>\n\n<h3>Other Gram-positive rods<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Listeria monocytogenes<\/b>: small rods with <b>tumbling motility at 25 °C<\/b> (room temperature), plus \"<b>umbrella<\/b>\" motility in semi-solid agar. It is weakly <b>beta-hemolytic<\/b> and catalase positive, and it <b>grows in the fridge (4 °C)<\/b>, so \"cold enrichment\" helps isolate it. That also explains how it spreads: refrigerated deli meats, soft cheeses and unpasteurised milk. It threatens <b>pregnant women, newborns, the elderly and the immunocompromised<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Corynebacterium diphtheriae<\/b>: <b>club-shaped<\/b> rods in \"<b>Chinese letter<\/b>\" arrangements, with metachromatic granules. Grows on <b>Loeffler<\/b> serum and <b>tellurite<\/b> media (black colonies). Disease comes from <b>diphtheria toxin<\/b> (made only by strains carrying a phage). Confirm toxin with the <b>Elek test<\/b> or PCR. Other Corynebacterium species are usually skin flora (\"diphtheroids\").<\/li>\n<li><b>Bacillus anthracis<\/b>: large boxcar rods; <b>non-motile<\/b>, <b>non-hemolytic<\/b>, with sticky \"<b>medusa head<\/b>\" colonies. A select agent (bioterrorism); the lab must refer suspected isolates.<\/li>\n<li><b>Bacillus cereus<\/b>: <b>motile<\/b> and <b>beta-hemolytic<\/b>; food poisoning from reheated rice.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: B. anthracis vs B. cereus<\/b>Opposites: anthracis is <b>non<\/b>-motile and <b>non<\/b>-hemolytic; cereus is motile and beta-hemolytic.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Branching rods: Nocardia and Actinomyces<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Nocardia<\/b>: thin, <b>branching<\/b> Gram-positive rods that are <b>partially (weakly) acid-fast<\/b> with the modified acid-fast stain. Aerobic, found in soil. Causes lung disease and brain abscesses in immunocompromised patients.<\/li>\n<li><b>Actinomyces<\/b>: also branching, but <b>not<\/b> acid-fast and <b>anaerobic<\/b>. Part of mouth flora. Forms abscesses that drain yellow \"<b>sulfur granules<\/b>\" (clumps of organisms, not real sulfur), often after dental problems.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A Gram-negative anaerobe grows in 20% bile and resists vancomycin, kanamycin and colistin. Identify.<\/summary><p>Bacteroides fragilis group.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A boxcar-shaped Gram-positive rod shows double-zone hemolysis and a positive Nagler test. Identify.<\/summary><p>Clostridium perfringens.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is Listeria a risk in refrigerated foods?<\/summary><p>It can grow at fridge temperatures (about 4 °C), unlike most food-borne bacteria.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A formed stool is sent for C. difficile testing. What should the lab do?<\/summary><p>Reject it and ask for an unformed (liquid) stool. Testing formed stool detects colonisation, not disease.<\/p><\/details>"},"M6":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Mycobacteria and fungi are slow, tough organisms that need their own methods. Mycobacteria have a waxy wall that resists ordinary stains and lets them survive harsh treatments. Fungi are identified mostly by how they look, both the colony and the microscopic structures. This lesson explains the classification schemes so the details make sense.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Mycobacteria: the waxy-walled rods<\/h3>\n<p>The mycobacterial cell wall is packed with <b>mycolic acids<\/b>, long waxy fats. This explains almost everything about them:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>They resist Gram stain but, once stained with carbolfuchsin, hold the dye against acid-alcohol: <b>acid-fast<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>They survive harsh chemicals, which lets the lab <b>digest and decontaminate<\/b> specimens (with <b>NALC-NaOH<\/b>). The NALC thins the mucus; NaOH kills faster-growing contaminating bacteria, which would otherwise overgrow the slow mycobacteria.<\/li>\n<li>They grow slowly (M. tuberculosis takes about 2-8 weeks), because nutrients pass slowly through the waxy wall.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>M. tuberculosis<\/h3>\n<p>The cause of TB, spread by coughed-up droplets. Identification clues:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Slow grower<\/b> with <b>rough, buff (\"cauliflower\") colonies<\/b> on Löwenstein-Jensen.<\/li>\n<li><b>Niacin positive<\/b> and <b>nitrate positive<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cording<\/b>: cells line up in rope-like strands (cord factor).<\/li>\n<li>Today, isolates are usually identified by nucleic acid probes, PCR (which also detects rifampin resistance) or MALDI-TOF.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Cultures are handled in <b>BSL-3<\/b> conditions because aerosols spread infection. TB testing of patients (skin test, IGRA) is in the immunology lessons.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Non-tuberculous mycobacteria: the Runyon groups<\/h3>\n<p>Other mycobacteria are grouped by <b>growth speed<\/b> and <b>pigment<\/b>. Test pigment by growing two cultures, one in the dark and one exposed to light:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Group I, photochromogens<\/b>: yellow pigment <b>only after light exposure<\/b>. <b>M. kansasii<\/b> (TB-like lung disease) and <b>M. marinum<\/b> (skin lesions from fish tanks or pools; prefers <b>30 °C<\/b>, because it lives in cooler skin).<\/li>\n<li><b>Group II, scotochromogens<\/b>: pigment <b>even in the dark<\/b> (\"scoto\" = dark). M. gordonae (tap water, usually a contaminant), M. scrofulaceum (neck lymph nodes in children).<\/li>\n<li><b>Group III, nonphotochromogens<\/b>: no pigment. <b>M. avium complex (MAC)<\/b>, the classic infection in advanced <b>HIV/AIDS<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Group IV, rapid growers<\/b>: grow in under 7 days. M. fortuitum, M. chelonae, M. abscessus (skin and surgical-site infections).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>M. leprae<\/b> (leprosy) <b>can't be grown in lab culture<\/b>; it is diagnosed from skin biopsies, and grows in armadillos and mouse footpads.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b><b>Photo<\/b>chromogens need <b>photo<\/b>ns (light). <b>Scoto<\/b>chromogens color in the dark (\"<b>s<\/b>coto\" = <b>s<\/b>hadow). Group III is \"<b>n<\/b>on\" (no color).<\/div>\n\n<h3>Fungi: yeasts, molds and dimorphs<\/h3>\n<p>Fungi come in two basic forms:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Yeasts<\/b>: single round cells that reproduce by budding. Colonies look creamy, like bacteria.<\/li>\n<li><b>Molds<\/b>: long threads (<b>hyphae<\/b>) forming fuzzy colonies. Hyphae may have cross-walls (<b>septate<\/b>) or not (<b>aseptate<\/b>). Molds are identified by their spores (<b>conidia<\/b>) and how they are arranged.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Dimorphic fungi<\/b> switch between the two depending on temperature: <b>mold at 25 °C<\/b> (in soil) and <b>yeast at 37 °C<\/b> (in the body). Remember \"<b>mold in the cold, yeast in the heat<\/b>.\"<\/p>\n\n<h3>The dimorphic fungi (and where they live)<\/h3>\n<p>These cause lung infections when spores are inhaled, and each has a home region and a signature structure:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Histoplasma capsulatum<\/b>: Ohio and Mississippi river valleys, <b>bird and bat droppings<\/b> (caves, chicken coops). Mold shows <b>tuberculate macroconidia<\/b> (spiky balls). In the body it is a <b>tiny yeast hiding inside macrophages<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Blastomyces dermatitidis<\/b>: also eastern and central US. In tissue, large yeast with <b>broad-based budding<\/b> and a thick wall.<\/li>\n<li><b>Coccidioides immitis<\/b>: the deserts of the southwestern US (\"Valley fever\"). The mold makes barrel-shaped <b>arthroconidia<\/b> that are <b>highly infectious<\/b>; plates must be handled only in a biosafety cabinet. In tissue it forms <b>spherules filled with endospores<\/b>, not a budding yeast.<\/li>\n<li><b>Sporothrix schenckii<\/b>: \"<b>rose gardener's disease<\/b>\", entering through thorn pricks and spreading up the lymph channels. Cigar-shaped yeast in tissue.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Yeasts<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Candida albicans<\/b>: the most common yeast infection (thrush, vaginitis, line infections). Its quick ID test is the <b>germ tube<\/b>: in serum at 37 °C for 2-3 hours it sprouts small tubes with no pinch at the base. It also forms chlamydospores on cornmeal agar.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cryptococcus neoformans<\/b>: a yeast with a thick <b>polysaccharide capsule<\/b> (seen with India ink), <b>urease positive<\/b>, found in <b>pigeon droppings<\/b>. Causes meningitis in AIDS patients. The <b>cryptococcal antigen test<\/b> on CSF or serum is the most sensitive diagnosis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Molds you must recognise<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Aspergillus<\/b>: <b>septate<\/b> hyphae with <b>acute-angle (about 45°) branching<\/b>. Causes allergic disease, fungus balls in old lung cavities, and invasive infection in patients with low neutrophils.<\/li>\n<li><b>Mucor and Rhizopus<\/b> (Mucorales): <b>broad, ribbon-like hyphae with few or no septa<\/b> and <b>right-angle branching<\/b>. Invade blood vessels and destroy tissue, classically in <b>uncontrolled diabetics (ketoacidosis)<\/b>, starting in the sinuses.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Dermatophytes: skin, hair and nails<\/h3>\n<p>Dermatophytes feed on keratin, so they infect only skin, hair and nails (ringworm, athlete's foot):<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Trichophyton<\/b>: skin, hair and nails (all three).<\/li>\n<li><b>Microsporum<\/b>: skin and hair (not nails).<\/li>\n<li><b>Epidermophyton<\/b>: skin and nails (not hair).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: which dermatophyte skips what<\/b><b>Micro<\/b>sporum skips the nails; <b>Epiderm<\/b>ophyton skips the hair; <b>Tricho<\/b>phyton does all three.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>An AFB isolate turns yellow only after exposure to light. Which Runyon group, and a likely species?<\/summary><p>Group I photochromogen, such as M. kansasii (or M. marinum).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why are respiratory samples treated with NALC-NaOH before mycobacterial culture?<\/summary><p>NALC thins mucus and NaOH kills faster-growing contaminating bacteria. Mycobacteria survive because of their waxy wall.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Tissue shows spherules containing endospores in a patient from Arizona. Organism?<\/summary><p>Coccidioides immitis.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Broad aseptate hyphae with right-angle branching in a diabetic's sinus biopsy. Which group?<\/summary><p>Mucorales (Mucor or Rhizopus).<\/p><\/details>"},"M7":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">This lesson collects the organisms that don't fit the bacterial culture workflow: parasites (found mostly by looking through a microscope), viruses (found mostly by molecular tests), and a few unusual bacteria that can't be grown on ordinary plates. For parasites, the trick is to tie each one to the shape or structure that identifies it.<\/p>\n\n<h3>How parasites are found<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Stool ova and parasites (O&amp;P):<\/b> the stool is concentrated (<b>formalin-ethyl acetate<\/b> sedimentation, which collects eggs and cysts at the bottom), then looked at wet and with a permanent stain. <b>Trichrome<\/b> is the permanent stain for intestinal protozoa.<\/li>\n<li><b>Pinworm (Enterobius):<\/b> the female lays eggs around the anus at night, so press clear tape there first thing in the morning (the <b>Scotch tape test<\/b>), before bathing.<\/li>\n<li><b>Blood parasites:<\/b> Giemsa-stained thick and thin smears.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Intestinal and genital protozoa<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Entamoeba histolytica<\/b>: the disease-causing amoeba (bloody diarrhoea, liver abscess). The proof is a <b>trophozoite with ingested red cells<\/b>. Cysts have up to <b>4 nuclei<\/b> and cigar-shaped chromatoidal bars. (Harmless E. coli the amoeba has cysts with up to 8 nuclei.)<\/li>\n<li><b>Giardia<\/b>: <b>pear-shaped<\/b> trophozoite with two nuclei that look like eyes (a \"smiling face\") and a \"<b>falling leaf<\/b>\" motility. Causes greasy, foul diarrhoea, often from untreated stream water.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cryptosporidium<\/b> and <b>Cyclospora<\/b>: tiny oocysts that are <b>acid-fast<\/b> with the modified stain. Watery diarrhoea, severe in AIDS patients. Crypto survives chlorine (swimming pool outbreaks).<\/li>\n<li><b>Trichomonas vaginalis<\/b>: pear-shaped flagellate with <b>jerky motility<\/b> in a fresh wet mount; sexually transmitted vaginitis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Malaria: thick and thin smears<\/h3>\n<p>The <b>thick smear<\/b> piles up many layers of lysed blood, so it is sensitive for <b>finding<\/b> parasites. The <b>thin smear<\/b> keeps red cells intact, so you can see <b>what the parasite looks like and what it does to the cell<\/b>, which identifies the species and lets you count the percentage parasitemia.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>P. falciparum<\/b>: the deadliest. Look for <b>many small rings<\/b>, often <b>several in one red cell<\/b>, rings at the cell edge, <b>banana-shaped gametocytes<\/b>, and red cells of <b>normal size<\/b>. It infects red cells of any age, so parasitemia can be very high.<\/li>\n<li><b>P. vivax<\/b>: infects young red cells, which become <b>enlarged<\/b> with pink <b>Schüffner dots<\/b>; trophozoites look ragged and <b>ameboid<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>P. ovale<\/b>: also enlarged with Schüffner dots, but cells are <b>oval<\/b> with <b>fringed<\/b> edges.<\/li>\n<li><b>P. malariae<\/b>: infects old red cells (normal or small). <b>Band-shaped<\/b> trophozoites across the cell, \"<b>rosette<\/b>\" schizonts, and a <b>72-hour<\/b> fever cycle.<\/li>\n<li><b>P. knowlesi<\/b>: monkey malaria in Southeast Asia; looks like malariae but has a <b>24-hour<\/b> cycle and can be severe.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: Babesia vs falciparum<\/b>Both show small rings in red cells. <b>Babesia<\/b> (tick-borne, northeastern US) can form a \"<b>Maltese cross<\/b>\" (four parasites in a tetrad) and has <b>no pigment and no gametocytes<\/b>.<\/div>\n<p>Other blood and tissue parasites: <b>Trypanosoma<\/b> (long wavy flagellates: sleeping sickness, Chagas disease), <b>Leishmania<\/b> (small <b>amastigotes inside macrophages<\/b>), and <b>microfilariae<\/b> (worm larvae in blood).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Worm eggs: each has a signature<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Ascaris<\/b>: round egg with a thick, bumpy (<b>mammillated<\/b>) coat.<\/li>\n<li><b>Trichuris<\/b> (whipworm): <b>barrel<\/b> or football shape with a <b>plug at each end<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hookworm<\/b>: oval with a <b>thin, clear shell<\/b>; adult worms suck blood and cause <b>iron deficiency anemia<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Enterobius<\/b> (pinworm): <b>flattened on one side<\/b> (\"D\" shape).<\/li>\n<li><b>Diphyllobothrium latum<\/b> (fish tapeworm): egg with a lid (<b>operculum<\/b>); the worm absorbs <b>vitamin B12<\/b>, causing megaloblastic anemia.<\/li>\n<li><b>Schistosoma<\/b> (blood fluke) eggs have a <b>spine<\/b> whose position names the species: <b>lateral<\/b> (side) spine = <b>S. mansoni<\/b> (gut, liver); <b>terminal<\/b> spine = <b>S. haematobium<\/b> (bladder; found in urine); a tiny side <b>knob<\/b> = <b>S. japonicum<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: Schistosoma<\/b><b>H<\/b>aematobium has its spine at the <b>H<\/b>ead end (terminal) and lives near the bladder, so look in urine. <b>M<\/b>ansoni's spine points <b>M<\/b>idway (lateral).<\/div>\n\n<h3>Unusual bacteria<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Chlamydia trachomatis<\/b>: an <b>obligate intracellular<\/b> bacterium; it can only grow inside human cells, so it won't grow on agar. The common STI. Diagnosed by <b>NAAT<\/b> on urine or swabs. Giemsa can show inclusions in cells.<\/li>\n<li><b>Mycoplasma pneumoniae<\/b>: has <b>no cell wall<\/b>, so it doesn't Gram stain and beta-lactam drugs (which attack cell walls) don't work. \"Walking pneumonia\"; associated with <b>cold agglutinins<\/b> (anti-I).<\/li>\n<li><b>Treponema pallidum<\/b> (syphilis): too thin to see on a Gram stain and can't be cultured; seen as corkscrew organisms by <b>darkfield microscopy<\/b> of a chancre, but usually diagnosed by serology.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Viruses to know<\/h3>\n<p>Viruses are now mostly detected by <b>PCR<\/b> and antigen tests. A few old-fashioned clues are still tested because they show <i>what the virus does to cells<\/i>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Herpes simplex<\/b>: <b>multinucleated giant cells<\/b> on a Tzanck smear (cells fuse together). <b>PCR<\/b> is the best test (especially on CSF for encephalitis).<\/li>\n<li><b>Varicella-zoster<\/b>: chickenpox, and later shingles when it reactivates in a nerve.<\/li>\n<li><b>CMV<\/b>: enlarged cells with large \"<b>owl's eye<\/b>\" nuclear inclusions. Dangerous in <b>transplant patients<\/b> and <b>newborns<\/b> (congenital infection). That is why CMV-safe (leukoreduced or CMV-negative) blood is used for at-risk patients.<\/li>\n<li><b>EBV<\/b>: infectious mononucleosis; also linked to Burkitt lymphoma and nasopharyngeal cancer.<\/li>\n<li><b>Adenovirus<\/b>: pink eye (conjunctivitis), colds and diarrhoea in children.<\/li>\n<li><b>RSV<\/b>: <b>bronchiolitis<\/b> in infants; in cell culture it makes cells fuse into <b>syncytia<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Influenza<\/b>: seasonal; rapid antigen tests (less sensitive) and PCR.<\/li>\n<li><b>Rabies<\/b>: <b>Negri bodies<\/b> in brain neurons.<\/li>\n<li><b>Parvovirus B19<\/b>: \"fifth disease\" (slapped-cheek rash). It attacks red cell precursors, so in people whose red cells already die early (sickle cell, hereditary spherocytosis) it causes an <b>aplastic crisis<\/b>. It can also cause hydrops in a fetus.<\/li>\n<li><b>Norovirus<\/b> (outbreaks on cruise ships, in nursing homes) and <b>rotavirus<\/b> (infants): viral gastroenteritis.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A thin smear shows several small rings in single red cells of normal size, plus a crescent-shaped gametocyte. Species?<\/summary><p>Plasmodium falciparum.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>An egg with a terminal spine is found in urine. Which parasite?<\/summary><p>Schistosoma haematobium.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why doesn't Chlamydia grow on routine culture plates?<\/summary><p>It is an obligate intracellular organism and needs living host cells. NAAT is the test of choice.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A child with sickle cell disease suddenly has a very low hemoglobin and almost no reticulocytes. Which virus?<\/summary><p>Parvovirus B19, causing an aplastic crisis.<\/p><\/details>"},"M8":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Identifying a pathogen is only half the job. The doctor also needs to know <b>which antibiotics will work<\/b>. This lesson explains how susceptibility testing works, how bacteria resist drugs, which resistance is \"built in\", and the modern machines that now do much of the identification. It ends with the dangerous agents a routine lab must recognise and hand on.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Disk diffusion (Kirby-Bauer)<\/h3>\n<p>A standard amount of bacteria is spread over a plate and paper disks soaked in antibiotics are placed on top. Each drug spreads out into the agar, forming a gradient that is strongest near the disk. Where the drug is strong enough, the bacteria can't grow, leaving a clear <b>zone of inhibition<\/b>. The zone diameter is compared with <b>CLSI breakpoints<\/b> to report <b>S<\/b> (susceptible), <b>I<\/b> (intermediate) or <b>R<\/b> (resistant).<\/p>\n<p>Because the zone size depends on many things, every condition is standardised:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mueller-Hinton agar<\/b> (low in substances that block sulfonamides and trimethoprim), poured to <b>4 mm<\/b> depth, pH 7.2-7.4.<\/li>\n<li>Inoculum matched to a <b>0.5 McFarland standard<\/b> (about <b>1.5 × 10⁸ CFU/mL<\/b>).<\/li>\n<li>Incubate at <b>35 °C<\/b> in room air for <b>16-18 hours<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why each variable matters<\/b>Think about how each one changes the zone:<br>• <b>Too heavy an inoculum<\/b> → more bacteria to overcome the drug → <b>smaller zones<\/b> → false resistance.<br>• <b>Too light<\/b> → <b>larger zones<\/b> → false susceptibility.<br>• <b>Agar too thick<\/b> → drug spreads downward instead of outward → <b>smaller<\/b> zones. <b>Too thin<\/b> → larger zones.<br>• <b>Old, weak disks<\/b> → smaller zones.<\/div>\n\n<h3>MIC: the more precise answer<\/h3>\n<p>The <b>MIC (minimum inhibitory concentration)<\/b> is the <b>lowest drug concentration that stops visible growth<\/b>. In <b>broth dilution<\/b>, the organism is grown in a row of wells with doubling drug concentrations; the first clear well is the MIC. An <b>Etest<\/b> strip carries a gradient of drug on a plate; the MIC is read where the ellipse of inhibition meets the strip. The <b>MBC<\/b> (minimum bactericidal concentration) is the lowest concentration that kills 99.9%.<\/p>\n<p><b>QC strains<\/b> with known results check every run: <b>E. coli ATCC 25922<\/b>, <b>S. aureus ATCC 25923<\/b> (disk) or <b>29213<\/b> (MIC), <b>P. aeruginosa ATCC 27853<\/b>, and <b>E. faecalis ATCC 29212<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>How bacteria resist, and how the lab detects it<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>MRSA<\/b>: the <b>mecA<\/b> gene makes <b>PBP2a<\/b>, a cell-wall-building enzyme that beta-lactams can't bind. Detected with a <b>cefoxitin disk<\/b> (the best surrogate; it strongly triggers mecA), oxacillin MIC, PBP2a latex or PCR. MRSA is reported resistant to all beta-lactams except ceftaroline.<\/li>\n<li><b>VRE<\/b>: <b>vanA or vanB<\/b> genes change the target of vancomycin (the end of the cell-wall building block) so the drug can't stick.<\/li>\n<li><b>ESBL<\/b> (extended-spectrum beta-lactamase): enzymes that destroy penicillins, <b>third-generation cephalosporins<\/b> and aztreonam. They are blocked by <b>clavulanate<\/b>, so a bigger zone when clavulanate is added confirms an ESBL. Carbapenems still work.<\/li>\n<li><b>Carbapenemases<\/b> (KPC, NDM, OXA-48): destroy even carbapenems, the \"last-resort\" drugs. Detected by the <b>modified carbapenem inactivation method (mCIM)<\/b> or molecular tests. These are serious infection-control alerts.<\/li>\n<li><b>Inducible clindamycin resistance<\/b>: some staph and strep carry a dormant gene (erm) that erythromycin switches on, which then makes them resistant to clindamycin too. The <b>D-test<\/b> places the two disks near each other; if the clindamycin zone is <b>flattened into a \"D\"<\/b> on the erythromycin side, report clindamycin resistant.<\/li>\n<li><b>Beta-lactamase<\/b>: the <b>nitrocefin (cefinase)<\/b> disk turns from yellow to red when the enzyme breaks it. Useful for Haemophilus, Neisseria, Moraxella and anaerobes.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Built-in (intrinsic) resistance<\/h3>\n<p>Some organisms are naturally resistant to certain drugs, so the lab doesn't report those drugs as options. These are favourite exam questions:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Klebsiella<\/b> → <b>ampicillin<\/b> (it naturally makes a beta-lactamase).<\/li>\n<li><b>Pseudomonas aeruginosa<\/b> → many drugs, including ampicillin, first-generation cephalosporins and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (pumps and a tight outer membrane).<\/li>\n<li><b>Enterococcus<\/b> → <b>cephalosporins<\/b> and low-level aminoglycosides (its PBPs don't bind cephalosporins well).<\/li>\n<li><b>Stenotrophomonas<\/b> → <b>carbapenems<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Anaerobes<\/b> → <b>aminoglycosides<\/b> (uptake needs oxygen).<\/li>\n<li><b>Gram-negative rods<\/b> → <b>vancomycin<\/b> (too big to pass the outer membrane).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: Enterococcus and cephalosporins<\/b>Even if a cephalosporin appears to work in the lab, it never works clinically against Enterococcus. Never report it as susceptible.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Modern identification methods<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry<\/b>: a bit of colony is mixed with a matrix and blasted with a laser; its proteins (mostly ribosomal) fly through a tube, and the pattern of their masses is matched to a database. It identifies most bacteria and yeasts in minutes.<\/li>\n<li><b>Multiplex PCR panels<\/b>: test for many organisms and resistance genes (like mecA, vanA, KPC) at once, straight from a positive blood culture, stool, CSF or respiratory swab.<\/li>\n<li><b>16S rRNA gene sequencing<\/b>: reads a gene every bacterium has, to name organisms that routine methods can't.<\/li>\n<li><b>Automated ID/AST systems<\/b> (Vitek, Phoenix, MicroScan): miniature biochemical and antibiotic panels read by machine.<\/li>\n<li><b>Continuous-monitoring blood culture systems<\/b> (BACTEC, BacT/Alert): sensors detect the <b>CO2<\/b> that growing organisms produce, flagging positive bottles automatically.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Select agents and the sentinel lab<\/h3>\n<p>Some organisms could be used as bioweapons and are dangerous to lab staff. A routine hospital lab acts as a <b>sentinel lab<\/b>: its job is only to <b>recognise and rule out<\/b>, not to fully identify. If an isolate might be one of these, the lab works in a <b>biosafety cabinet<\/b>, <b>never sniffs plates<\/b>, stops further testing, notifies public health, and refers the isolate to the <b>Laboratory Response Network (LRN)<\/b>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Bacillus anthracis<\/b>: large Gram-positive rod, non-hemolytic, non-motile, \"medusa head\" colonies.<\/li>\n<li><b>Yersinia pestis<\/b>: Gram-negative rod with bipolar \"safety pin\" staining, slow growth.<\/li>\n<li><b>Francisella tularensis<\/b>: tiny Gram-negative coccobacillus that needs cysteine (grows on chocolate), extremely infectious.<\/li>\n<li><b>Brucella<\/b>: tiny coccobacillus, slow growth, urease positive; from unpasteurised dairy.<\/li>\n<li><b>Burkholderia pseudomallei and mallei<\/b>: oxidase positive; automated systems can misidentify them.<\/li>\n<li><b>Clostridium botulinum<\/b>: its neurotoxin is the danger.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b>Tiny Gram-negative coccobacilli that grow slowly and came from animals → think <b>Brucella or Francisella<\/b> → put it in the hood and call it in.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A Kirby-Bauer plate was inoculated with a suspension much heavier than 0.5 McFarland. Effect?<\/summary><p>Smaller zones, so resistance may be falsely reported. Repeat with a correct inoculum.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A staph isolate is erythromycin resistant and clindamycin susceptible, and the clindamycin zone is flattened next to the erythromycin disk. How do you report clindamycin?<\/summary><p>Resistant. The positive D-test shows inducible clindamycin resistance.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which disk best detects mecA-mediated resistance in S. aureus?<\/summary><p>Cefoxitin.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why is vancomycin never tested on E. coli?<\/summary><p>Gram-negative rods are intrinsically resistant: the large vancomycin molecule can't cross their outer membrane.<\/p><\/details>"},"M9":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A culture can only be as good as the specimen. A badly collected sample grows the wrong organisms, misses the right ones, or sends the doctor down the wrong path. This lesson covers how specimens should be collected and judged, which organisms to expect at each body site, and how results are reported. Exam questions here are often about <b>what to do<\/b>, not just what grows.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Why the specimen rules exist<\/h3>\n<p><b>Blood cultures.<\/b> Skin bacteria can fall into the bottle during the draw. To separate real infection from contamination:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clean the skin well (<b>chlorhexidine<\/b>, let it dry) and disinfect the bottle tops.<\/li>\n<li>Draw <b>two sets from two separate sites<\/b> (each set is an aerobic plus an anaerobic bottle), with enough blood. Volume is the biggest factor in detecting bacteremia.<\/li>\n<li>Interpretation: <b>coagulase-negative staph in one bottle out of four<\/b> is usually a <b>contaminant<\/b>. The same organism in multiple sets suggests real infection. <b>S. aureus, E. coli, S. pneumoniae or Candida<\/b> are treated as significant even in one bottle.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>CSF.<\/b> The most urgent specimen in micro. Deliver and process <b>immediately<\/b> at <b>room temperature<\/b>, and <b>never refrigerate<\/b> it: N. meningitidis, H. influenzae and S. pneumoniae die in the cold.<\/p>\n<p><b>Urine.<\/b> Urine picks up skin and urethral bacteria on the way out, so collect a <b>midstream clean catch<\/b>. Quantity separates infection from contamination: <b>≥100,000 CFU/mL<\/b> of one organism is the traditional cut-off (lower counts can matter in symptomatic patients or catheter samples). With a 0.001 mL calibrated loop, each colony equals 1,000 CFU/mL. In a <b>suprapubic aspirate<\/b> (needle through the abdominal wall into the bladder), <b>any<\/b> growth is significant, because it bypasses the urethra. Refrigerate if delayed, or use a <b>boric acid<\/b> preservative tube.<\/p>\n<p><b>Sputum quality.<\/b> A \"sputum\" that is mostly saliva is useless. Screen the Gram stain under low power: <b>more than 25 white cells and fewer than 10 squamous epithelial cells per field<\/b> means the sample came from the lungs. Many squamous cells means mouth contamination, so reject it and ask for a new one.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Expected organisms by body site<\/h3>\n<p>Knowing what usually causes infection at each site helps you judge whether a result makes sense:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Blood<\/b>: S. aureus, E. coli, Klebsiella, streptococci, enterococci, Candida. (CoNS is usually a contaminant.)<\/li>\n<li><b>Urine<\/b>: <b>E. coli<\/b> by far the most common, then S. saprophyticus (young women), Klebsiella, Proteus, Enterococcus, Pseudomonas (catheters, hospital), Candida.<\/li>\n<li><b>Upper respiratory<\/b>: S. pyogenes (strep throat), C. diphtheriae, B. pertussis.<\/li>\n<li><b>Lower respiratory<\/b>: S. pneumoniae (most common bacterial pneumonia), H. influenzae, Moraxella, Klebsiella (alcoholics), Legionella, Mycoplasma, M. tuberculosis, Pneumocystis (AIDS), Pseudomonas in cystic fibrosis.<\/li>\n<li><b>Stool<\/b>: Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter, E. coli pathotypes (O157), Yersinia, Vibrio, C. difficile (after antibiotics).<\/li>\n<li><b>Genital<\/b>: N. gonorrhoeae, C. trachomatis, T. pallidum, Gardnerella, Trichomonas, Candida, HSV, H. ducreyi (chancroid).<\/li>\n<li><b>Skin and soft tissue<\/b>: S. aureus, S. pyogenes, Pseudomonas (<b>hot tubs<\/b>), <b>Pasteurella<\/b> (cat and dog bites), <b>Vibrio vulnificus<\/b> (seawater wounds).<\/li>\n<li><b>Bone<\/b>: S. aureus usually; <b>Salmonella<\/b> in sickle cell disease.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Meningitis: the cause depends on age<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Newborns<\/b>: <b>group B strep<\/b>, <b>E. coli<\/b>, <b>Listeria<\/b>. These come from the mother's vagina and gut at birth.<\/li>\n<li><b>Children<\/b>: S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, H. influenzae (rare now thanks to the Hib vaccine).<\/li>\n<li><b>Adults<\/b>: S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis.<\/li>\n<li><b>Older adults<\/b>: S. pneumoniae, <b>Listeria<\/b> (weaker cell-mediated immunity), Gram-negative rods.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b>Listeria shows up at both ends of life: newborns and the elderly (and in pregnancy and immunosuppression).<\/div>\n\n<h3>Vaginitis: three causes, three patterns<\/h3>\n<p>Healthy vaginal pH is about 3.8-4.5, kept acidic by lactobacilli. The pH and a wet mount sort out the causes:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Bacterial vaginosis<\/b> (Gardnerella and anaerobes replace lactobacilli): <b>pH above 4.5<\/b>, a fishy smell when KOH is added (positive \"<b>whiff test<\/b>\", from amines), <b>clue cells<\/b> (epithelial cells coated with bacteria so their edges look fuzzy), and few white cells (it isn't really inflammatory).<\/li>\n<li><b>Candida<\/b>: <b>normal pH<\/b>, thick white discharge, <b>budding yeast and pseudohyphae<\/b> on KOH.<\/li>\n<li><b>Trichomonas<\/b>: <b>pH above 4.5<\/b>, <b>motile flagellates<\/b> on a fresh wet mount (check quickly, they stop moving as the slide cools), many white cells.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Rejecting specimens<\/h3>\n<p>A lab must refuse specimens that would give misleading results. Reject and ask for a new sample if it is:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>unlabeled or mislabeled<\/b>, <b>leaking<\/b>, or in the <b>wrong container<\/b> or transport medium,<\/li>\n<li><b>too old<\/b> or stored at the wrong temperature,<\/li>\n<li>a type that can't be interpreted: a <b>24-hour sputum<\/b> (overgrown with mouth flora), a <b>Foley catheter tip<\/b> (always contaminated by urine), a <b>swab of a surface ulcer<\/b> for anaerobes.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Transport rules to remember: <b>anaerobic transport<\/b> for anaerobe cultures; <b>don't refrigerate<\/b> CSF or gonorrhoea specimens; test stool promptly or use the right preservative.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Reporting<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Critical values<\/b> (call the clinician immediately): <b>positive blood culture<\/b>, <b>positive CSF Gram stain or culture<\/b>, and a <b>suspected select agent<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Reportable diseases<\/b> must go to public health (the list varies by state): TB, Salmonella, Shigella, measles, syphilis, gonorrhoea, Chlamydia, hepatitis, and the bioterror agents.<\/li>\n<li><b>Corrected reports<\/b> are clearly marked as corrected, the original result is kept on record, and the clinician is told.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Infection-control screening<\/b> looks for carriers rather than disease: <b>MRSA<\/b> (nasal swab, by mecA PCR or chromogenic agar), <b>VRE<\/b> (rectal swab, bile esculin agar with vancomycin), and <b>ESBL/CRE<\/b> (rectal swab, selective agar or PCR).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A sputum Gram stain shows 5 white cells and 40 squamous cells per low-power field. What do you do?<\/summary><p>Reject it. It is mostly saliva. Request a new deep-cough specimen.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A urine plated with a 0.001 mL loop grows 45 colonies. What is the count?<\/summary><p>45 ÷ 0.001 = 45,000 CFU/mL.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Vaginal pH 5.0, fishy odor with KOH, clue cells. Cause?<\/summary><p>Bacterial vaginosis (Gardnerella and anaerobes).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which three bacteria cause most newborn meningitis?<\/summary><p>Group B strep, E. coli and Listeria monocytogenes.<\/p><\/details>"},"U1":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A routine urinalysis has three parts: the <b>physical<\/b> exam (color, clarity, specific gravity), the <b>chemical<\/b> exam (the dipstick), and the <b>microscopic<\/b> exam (next lesson). Urine is easy to collect but changes quickly, and each dipstick pad has its own chemistry with known interferences. This lesson explains why each result happens, which is how the exam asks about it.<\/p>\n\n<h3>The 8 big ideas, explained<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Test within 2 hours or refrigerate.<\/b> Bacteria keep growing and metabolising, so urine changes on the bench (see below).<\/li>\n<li><b>Blood pad positive but no red cells seen<\/b> = free <b>hemoglobin or myoglobin<\/b> (the pad detects heme, not intact cells).<\/li>\n<li><b>Nitrite + leukocyte esterase<\/b> together point to a <b>bacterial UTI<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>The protein pad mainly sees albumin.<\/b> Sulfosalicylic acid (SSA) precipitates <b>all<\/b> proteins, including Bence Jones light chains.<\/li>\n<li><b>Casts tell you where in the kidney the problem is<\/b>: RBC cast = glomerulonephritis, WBC cast = pyelonephritis, fatty cast = nephrotic syndrome, waxy cast = chronic kidney failure.<\/li>\n<li><b>Crystals depend on pH<\/b>: acid urine gives uric acid, calcium oxalate, cystine; alkaline urine gives triple phosphate, ammonium biurate, amorphous phosphate.<\/li>\n<li><b>CSF tubes<\/b>: tube 1 chemistry, tube 2 microbiology, tube 3 cell count.<\/li>\n<li><b>Joint crystals<\/b>: gout (urate) = needles, strongly negatively birefringent; pseudogout (CPPD) = rhomboids, weakly positively birefringent.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Collecting the right specimen<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>First morning<\/b>: the most <b>concentrated<\/b>, so best for detecting small amounts of protein, nitrite (urine sat in the bladder all night) and hCG (pregnancy).<\/li>\n<li><b>Random<\/b>: routine screening.<\/li>\n<li><b>Midstream clean catch<\/b>: for <b>culture<\/b>. Cleaning and letting the first part go flushes out urethral bacteria.<\/li>\n<li><b>Timed 24-hour<\/b>: for things released unevenly through the day (protein, creatinine clearance, hormones). Start by <b>emptying the bladder and discarding<\/b> that urine, collect everything for 24 hours, and <b>include the final void<\/b> at the end. (The first void belongs to the previous period.)<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Why urine changes if left standing<\/h3>\n<p>Picture bacteria living in the warm urine:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bacteria <b>multiply<\/b> (false high counts on culture) and eat <b>glucose<\/b>, so glucose falls.<\/li>\n<li>Many bacteria split <b>urea into ammonia<\/b>, so the <b>pH rises<\/b>. Alkaline urine then dissolves <b>casts<\/b> and lyses <b>cells<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Ketones<\/b> evaporate (acetone) or are broken down, so they fall.<\/li>\n<li><b>Bilirubin<\/b> breaks down in light; <b>urobilinogen<\/b> oxidises to urobilin, so both fall.<\/li>\n<li><b>Nitrite<\/b> can rise (more bacteria converting nitrate) and <b>turbidity<\/b> increases.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Refrigeration slows this but can cause <b>amorphous crystals<\/b> to precipitate. Preservatives for special purposes: <b>boric acid<\/b> (culture transport), <b>formalin<\/b> (keeps cells and casts), <b>thymol<\/b>, <b>sodium fluoride<\/b> (stops glycolysis).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Physical examination: color and clarity<\/h3>\n<p>Color clues are about <b>what pigment is present<\/b>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Dark yellow-amber with yellow foam when shaken<\/b>: <b>bilirubin<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Red and cloudy<\/b>: intact <b>red cells<\/b> (they scatter light). <b>Red and clear<\/b>: a dissolved pigment: <b>hemoglobin<\/b>, <b>myoglobin<\/b>, <b>porphyrins<\/b> or <b>beets<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Port-wine<\/b>: porphyrins. <b>Brown or black<\/b>: old blood (methemoglobin), melanin, or <b>homogentisic acid<\/b> in alkaptonuria (darkens on standing).<\/li>\n<li><b>Blue-green<\/b>: Pseudomonas infection or methylene blue dye.<\/li>\n<li><b>Milky<\/b>: fat (chyluria), pus or amorphous phosphates.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cloudy<\/b> from cells, bacteria, mucus, or amorphous crystals: <b>urates<\/b> (pink \"brick dust\", acid urine, dissolve with heat) or <b>phosphates<\/b> (white, alkaline urine, dissolve with acetic acid).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Odor<\/b>: fruity = <b>ketones<\/b>; maple syrup = <b>maple syrup urine disease<\/b>; mousy = <b>PKU<\/b>; ammonia = old sample or infection.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Specific gravity: how concentrated is the urine?<\/h3>\n<p>Specific gravity (SG) compares urine's density with water's. It shows whether the kidney can concentrate and dilute urine. Normal random urine is about <b>1.003-1.035<\/b>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>refractometer<\/b> measures how much light bends, which reflects all dissolved solids. <b>Glucose and protein<\/b> raise the reading, and so does X-ray contrast dye (giving impossible values above 1.040).<\/li>\n<li>The <b>dipstick<\/b> SG pad responds only to <b>ions<\/b> (it releases H+ in proportion to ionic strength), so glucose and protein don't affect it.<\/li>\n<li><b>Osmolality<\/b> (number of particles) is the best true measure of concentrating ability.<\/li>\n<li>A SG stuck around <b>1.010<\/b> on repeated samples (<b>isosthenuria<\/b>) means the kidney can neither concentrate nor dilute, a sign of severe kidney damage. 1.010 is the SG of the plasma filtrate.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>The dipstick: each pad's chemistry and its traps<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Glucose<\/b> (glucose oxidase-peroxidase): specific for glucose. Appears when blood glucose exceeds the <b>renal threshold (about 160-180 mg/dL)<\/b>, or with a low threshold from a tubular defect. <b>Vitamin C<\/b> (a reducing agent) gives false negatives. <b>Clinitest<\/b> (copper reduction) detects any reducing sugar, such as <b>galactose<\/b> in infants with galactosemia.<\/li>\n<li><b>Ketones<\/b> (nitroprusside): detects acetoacetate (and acetone), <b>not beta-hydroxybutyrate<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Protein<\/b> (\"protein error of indicators\"): the dye changes color when protein binds at a fixed pH. It is <b>most sensitive to albumin<\/b>. <b>Very alkaline urine<\/b> (pH 9) overwhelms the buffer and gives a <b>false positive<\/b>. It <b>misses Bence Jones<\/b> proteins.<\/li>\n<li><b>Blood<\/b> (pseudoperoxidase): the heme in red cells, hemoglobin and myoglobin acts like an enzyme on the pad. Intact red cells give <b>speckles<\/b>; free hemoglobin gives uniform color. <b>Vitamin C<\/b> gives false negatives.<\/li>\n<li><b>Bilirubin<\/b> (diazo): detects only <b>conjugated<\/b> bilirubin (the water-soluble form). Light destroys it.<\/li>\n<li><b>Urobilinogen<\/b> (Ehrlich reagent): high in <b>hemolysis<\/b> and <b>liver disease<\/b>; absent in complete <b>bile duct obstruction<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Nitrite<\/b> (Griess reaction): many Gram-negative bacteria (especially Enterobacterales) convert dietary nitrate to nitrite. A positive needs bacteria that can do this <b>and<\/b> about <b>4 hours<\/b> in the bladder. False negatives: Gram-positives like Enterococcus, urine not held long enough, vitamin C.<\/li>\n<li><b>Leukocyte esterase<\/b>: an enzyme from <b>neutrophils<\/b> (and monocytes), so it detects <b>pyuria<\/b> even if the cells have lysed. It does <b>not<\/b> detect lymphocytes.<\/li>\n<li><b>pH<\/b> (methyl red and bromthymol blue): normal range <b>4.5-8.0<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: vitamin C<\/b>Ascorbic acid is a strong reducing agent. It interferes with the reactions on the <b>glucose, blood, bilirubin, nitrite and leukocyte esterase<\/b> pads, giving false negatives. Ask about vitamin C if results don't fit.<\/div>\n<p><b>Hemoglobin or myoglobin?<\/b> Both make the blood pad positive with no red cells. Check the patient's <b>plasma<\/b>: <b>pink plasma = hemoglobin<\/b> (hemolysis; haptoglobin binds a lot first, and the excess colors the plasma). <b>Clear plasma = myoglobin<\/b> (it is small and cleared into urine quickly). Myoglobinuria comes with a very high <b>CK<\/b> after muscle injury.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Confirmatory tests<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>SSA (sulfosalicylic acid)<\/b>: precipitates <b>all proteins<\/b>, including globulins and <b>Bence Jones<\/b>. Dipstick negative + SSA positive → think light chains.<\/li>\n<li><b>Clinitest<\/b>: reducing sugars (galactose, lactose, fructose). Positive Clinitest + negative glucose pad in a baby → galactosemia.<\/li>\n<li><b>Ictotest<\/b>: confirms bilirubin (more sensitive than the pad). <b>Acetest<\/b>: ketones.<\/li>\n<li><b>Ferric chloride<\/b>: green color in <b>PKU<\/b> (phenylketones) and with other metabolites.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hoesch or Watson-Schwartz<\/b>: porphobilinogen (acute intermittent porphyria).<\/li>\n<li><b>Cyanide-nitroprusside<\/b>: <b>cystine<\/b> (cystinuria).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A urine sat at room temperature for 4 hours. Which results change, and how?<\/summary><p>pH rises (ammonia from urea), glucose and ketones fall, bilirubin and urobilinogen fall, bacteria increase, and cells and casts break down.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Dipstick protein negative, SSA strongly positive. What protein might be present?<\/summary><p>Bence Jones protein (free light chains), which the albumin-sensitive pad misses.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Positive blood pad, no red cells, clear plasma, CK 25,000. Cause?<\/summary><p>Myoglobinuria from rhabdomyolysis.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient with an Enterococcus UTI has a negative nitrite. Why?<\/summary><p>Enterococcus (a Gram-positive) doesn't reduce nitrate to nitrite.<\/p><\/details>"},"U2":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The microscope shows what the dipstick can only hint at: actual cells, casts and crystals. <b>Casts<\/b> are the most valuable finding, because they can only form inside the kidney's tubules, so whatever is trapped in a cast tells you what is happening <i>inside the kidney<\/i>. This lesson explains how to prepare the sediment and how to interpret each element.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Preparing the sediment<\/h3>\n<p>Standardisation matters, because results are reported as counts per field:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Mix the urine and spin <b>10-12 mL<\/b> at about <b>400 RCF for 5 minutes<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Pour off the supernatant, leaving a fixed volume (about 0.5-1 mL), and resuspend gently.<\/li>\n<li>Examine under <b>low power<\/b> to scan for <b>casts<\/b> (report per LPF; they collect near the coverslip edges) and under <b>high power<\/b> to count <b>cells<\/b> (per HPF).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Reduce the light (or use <b>phase contrast<\/b>) to see pale <b>hyaline casts<\/b> and mucus. <b>Polarised light<\/b> shows crystals and the <b>Maltese cross<\/b> pattern of cholesterol droplets. Useful stains: <b>Sternheimer-Malbin<\/b> (general cells and casts), <b>Hansel<\/b> (eosinophils, for drug-induced interstitial nephritis), <b>Sudan III / Oil Red O<\/b> (fat; stains triglyceride, not cholesterol), and <b>Prussian blue<\/b> (hemosiderin).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Cells<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Red cells<\/b>: small, pale discs without a nucleus. In concentrated urine they shrink (<b>crenated<\/b>); in dilute urine they swell and lose hemoglobin (<b>ghost cells<\/b>). <b>Dysmorphic<\/b> red cells (odd shapes, blebs) have squeezed through the glomerulus and point to <b>glomerular<\/b> bleeding. Normal-shaped cells suggest bleeding lower down (stones, tumours, infection). Don't confuse red cells with <b>yeast<\/b> (which bud) or oil droplets (which vary in size). Acetic acid lyses red cells but not yeast.<\/li>\n<li><b>White cells<\/b>: mostly neutrophils, with a granular, lobed look. More than a few per HPF = infection or inflammation. In dilute urine they swell and their granules move (Brownian motion), sparkling as \"<b>glitter cells<\/b>\".<\/li>\n<li><b>Squamous epithelial cells<\/b>: large, flat, with a small nucleus. They come from the urethra and vagina, so many of them simply mean the specimen is <b>contaminated<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Transitional (urothelial) cells<\/b>: round or pear-shaped, from the renal pelvis, ureters and <b>bladder<\/b>. Increased after catheterisation; clusters with odd nuclei may suggest a tumour.<\/li>\n<li><b>Renal tubular epithelial (RTE) cells<\/b>: the most significant epithelial cell. They come from inside the kidney, so many of them mean <b>tubular injury<\/b> (acute tubular necrosis, toxins, rejection).<\/li>\n<li><b>Oval fat bodies<\/b>: RTE cells (or macrophages) <b>filled with fat droplets<\/b>, which show a Maltese cross under polarised light. They mean fat is leaking into urine: <b>nephrotic syndrome<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Casts: moulds of the tubule<\/h3>\n<p>Casts form in the <b>distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct<\/b>, where the tubular cells secrete <b>Tamm-Horsfall protein (uromodulin)<\/b>. When urine flows slowly, is acidic and concentrated, this protein gels into a cylinder shaped like the tubule. Anything floating nearby (cells, fat, debris) gets trapped inside. When the cast washes out, it carries a sample of what was in the kidney.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box why\"><b>Why casts locate the problem<\/b>A red cell in urine could have come from anywhere: kidney, ureter, bladder or contamination. A red cell <b>inside a cast<\/b> must have been in the renal tubule when the cast formed, so the bleeding is from the <b>kidney<\/b> (glomerulus). That is why casts are so valuable.<\/div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hyaline<\/b>: just protein, no cells. A few are <b>normal<\/b>, especially after exercise, dehydration, fever or stress.<\/li>\n<li><b>Red cell cast<\/b>: <b>glomerular bleeding<\/b>, the hallmark of <b>glomerulonephritis<\/b>. Always significant.<\/li>\n<li><b>White cell cast<\/b>: inflammation or infection <b>in the kidney<\/b>: <b>pyelonephritis<\/b>, interstitial nephritis. Separates kidney infection from simple cystitis.<\/li>\n<li><b>Epithelial (RTE) cell cast<\/b>: <b>tubular damage<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Granular cast<\/b>: cells inside a cast that broke down over time; non-specific. Many coarse \"muddy brown\" granular casts suggest <b>acute tubular necrosis<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Waxy cast<\/b>: the final stage of breakdown. Brittle, glassy, with cracked edges, formed after <b>prolonged stasis<\/b>: <b>chronic kidney failure<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Fatty cast<\/b>: contains fat droplets (Maltese crosses): <b>nephrotic syndrome<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Broad cast<\/b>: formed in widened, damaged tubules or collecting ducts: <b>chronic kidney failure<\/b> (\"renal failure casts\").<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook: the cast ladder<\/b>Cellular cast → (time and stasis) → coarse granular → fine granular → <b>waxy<\/b>. The further down the ladder, the longer the urine sat still, and the worse the kidney.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Crystals: let pH guide you<\/h3>\n<p>Crystals form when a substance is concentrated beyond its solubility, and solubility depends on pH. So always check the pH first.<\/p>\n<p><b>In acid urine:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Uric acid<\/b>: yellow-brown, rhombic plates or rosettes; strongly polarise. Seen in gout and chemotherapy (cell breakdown).<\/li>\n<li><b>Calcium oxalate<\/b>: the \"<b>envelope<\/b>\" (dihydrate, colorless square with an X) is common and usually harmless (diet: spinach, vitamin C). The <b>oval or dumbbell<\/b> (monohydrate) form in large numbers suggests <b>ethylene glycol poisoning<\/b>. Also seen in neutral urine.<\/li>\n<li><b>Abnormal crystals<\/b>, always reported: <b>cystine<\/b> (colorless <b>hexagons<\/b>; inherited <b>cystinuria<\/b>; confirm with cyanide-nitroprusside), <b>leucine<\/b> (spheres with concentric rings) and <b>tyrosine<\/b> (fine needles in sheaves), both in severe <b>liver disease<\/b>; <b>cholesterol<\/b> (flat plates with a <b>notched corner<\/b>; nephrotic syndrome); <b>bilirubin<\/b> (yellow needles); <b>sulfonamide<\/b> (drug crystals, sheaves).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>In alkaline urine:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Triple phosphate (struvite)<\/b>: colorless \"<b>coffin lid<\/b>\" prisms. Formed when <b>urea-splitting bacteria (Proteus)<\/b> make urine alkaline; linked to infection stones.<\/li>\n<li><b>Ammonium biurate<\/b>: yellow-brown \"<b>thorny apple<\/b>\" spheres, often in old specimens.<\/li>\n<li><b>Calcium phosphate<\/b> (prisms, plates) and <b>calcium carbonate<\/b> (small dumbbells; <b>fizz with acetic acid<\/b>).<\/li>\n<li><b>Amorphous phosphate<\/b>: white granular precipitate (dissolves in acetic acid). Compare <b>amorphous urates<\/b> in acid urine: pink \"brick dust\" that dissolves with heat.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Exam trap: crystal look-alikes<\/b>Cystine and uric acid can both look hexagonal. Uric acid is <b>yellow-brown and strongly polarises<\/b>; cystine is <b>colorless<\/b>, with weak polarisation. Confirm cystine with cyanide-nitroprusside.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Other findings<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Yeast<\/b>: oval, <b>budding<\/b>, no central pallor; may form pseudohyphae (common in diabetics).<\/li>\n<li><b>Trichomonas<\/b>: pear-shaped with <b>jerky motility<\/b>; must be seen moving to identify confidently.<\/li>\n<li><b>Sperm<\/b>, <b>mucus<\/b> threads (normal), and <b>Schistosoma haematobium<\/b> eggs (terminal spine) in travellers with blood in their urine.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why does a red cell cast prove bleeding comes from the kidney?<\/summary><p>Casts form only inside the renal tubules. A red cell inside a cast had to be in the tubule, so it came from the glomerulus.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Which cast separates pyelonephritis from cystitis?<\/summary><p>The white cell cast, which shows inflammation inside the kidney.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Colorless hexagonal crystals in acid urine. Which crystal, and how do you confirm it?<\/summary><p>Cystine; confirm with the cyanide-nitroprusside test.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Coffin-lid crystals in alkaline urine with a positive nitrite. What does this suggest?<\/summary><p>Triple phosphate (struvite) crystals from an infection with urea-splitting bacteria such as Proteus.<\/p><\/details>"},"U3":{"min":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Urine findings make sense only when you know how a healthy kidney works. This lesson starts with how the nephron filters and fine-tunes urine, then shows how the main kidney diseases produce their urine patterns, then covers kidney function testing and a few special urine tests.<\/p>\n\n<h3>How the nephron works<\/h3>\n<p>Each kidney has about a million nephrons. Think of each as a filter followed by a long processing tube:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Glomerulus (the filter)<\/b>: blood pressure pushes plasma through a fine sieve at about <b>125 mL/min<\/b> (the GFR). Water, salts, glucose and small molecules pass; <b>cells and large proteins (like albumin) stay in the blood<\/b>. A damaged filter lets protein and red cells through.<\/li>\n<li><b>Proximal tubule (the big reclaimer)<\/b>: takes back about <b>two-thirds<\/b> of the filtrate, including nearly <b>all glucose and amino acids<\/b>, plus sodium, bicarbonate and water. It has a limited number of glucose carriers. Once blood glucose rises above about <b>160-180 mg/dL<\/b> (the <b>renal threshold<\/b>), the carriers are full and glucose spills into urine.<\/li>\n<li><b>Loop of Henle (the concentrator)<\/b>: builds a salty gradient in the kidney's inner layer (the <b>countercurrent mechanism<\/b>) so that water can later be pulled out of the urine.<\/li>\n<li><b>Distal tubule and collecting duct (fine control)<\/b>: hormones adjust the final urine. <b>ADH<\/b> (antidiuretic hormone) opens water channels so water is reabsorbed and urine gets <b>concentrated<\/b>. <b>Aldosterone<\/b> saves sodium and gets rid of potassium.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Diabetes insipidus<\/b> is a failure of ADH (none is made, or the kidney ignores it). The patient makes large volumes of very <b>dilute urine with a low specific gravity<\/b>, even when dehydrated. Don't confuse it with diabetes mellitus, where urine contains glucose.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Kidney disease patterns: match the clue to the site<\/h3>\n<p><b>Nephrotic syndrome: a leaky filter for protein.<\/b> The glomerulus lets large amounts of protein through, but not many red cells.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Heavy proteinuria, <b>above 3.5 g/day<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Low blood albumin, so fluid leaks into tissues: <b>edema<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>The liver overworks making proteins, and lipoproteins with them: <b>high cholesterol<\/b>, and fat appears in urine as <b>oval fat bodies<\/b> and <b>fatty casts<\/b> (Maltese crosses).<\/li>\n<li>Common causes: minimal change disease (children), diabetic kidney disease, membranous nephropathy.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Nephritic syndrome (glomerulonephritis): an inflamed, bleeding filter.<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Blood<\/b> in urine (often cola-colored), <b>dysmorphic red cells<\/b> and <b>red cell casts<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Only <b>mild<\/b> proteinuria; <b>high blood pressure<\/b>, low urine output, rising creatinine.<\/li>\n<li>Causes: post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (after strep throat), IgA nephropathy, lupus, Goodpasture.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b>Ne<b>ph-r-o-tic<\/b> = <b>pr<\/b>otein (heavy) and fat. Ne<b>ph-r-i-tic<\/b> = <b>bl<\/b>eeding and <b>i<\/b>nflammation (red cell casts, high blood pressure).<\/div>\n<p><b>Infections:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cystitis<\/b> (bladder): white cells and bacteria, positive nitrite and leukocyte esterase, <b>no casts<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Pyelonephritis<\/b> (kidney): the same plus <b>white cell casts<\/b>, fever and flank pain.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Acute tubular necrosis (ATN):<\/b> tubular cells die from low blood flow (shock, surgery) or toxins (aminoglycosides, contrast dye, myoglobin). Urine shows <b>RTE cells<\/b> and \"<b>muddy brown<\/b>\" <b>granular casts<\/b>. Damaged tubules can't reclaim sodium or concentrate urine, so urine sodium is high and urine osmolality is low. That helps separate ATN from <b>prerenal<\/b> problems (dehydration), where healthy tubules hold on to sodium (urine Na under 20) and concentrate the urine (osmolality over 500).<\/p>\n<p><b>Chronic kidney disease<\/b>: <b>waxy and broad casts<\/b>, and a specific gravity fixed at about 1.010 (<b>isosthenuria<\/b>): the kidney can no longer concentrate or dilute.<\/p>\n<p><b>Orthostatic proteinuria<\/b>: protein in urine collected while upright (daytime) but not in the <b>first morning<\/b> sample. Common in young people and usually harmless. Compare a first-morning specimen with a daytime one to diagnose it.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Measuring kidney function: creatinine clearance<\/h3>\n<p>\"Clearance\" is the volume of plasma the kidneys clean of a substance per minute. Creatinine is filtered and barely reabsorbed, so its clearance approximates the GFR.<\/p>\n<p><b>CrCl (mL/min) = (U × V) ÷ P<\/b>, then corrected to a standard body size: × (1.73 ÷ patient's body surface area).<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>U = urine creatinine, P = plasma creatinine (same units), V = urine volume per <b>minute<\/b> (24-hour volume ÷ 1440).<\/li>\n<li>Normal is about <b>90-120 mL/min<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Urine creatinine 100 mg/dL, plasma creatinine 1.0 mg/dL, 24-hour volume 1440 mL (V = 1 mL/min), BSA 1.73 m². CrCl = 100 × 1 ÷ 1.0 = <b>100 mL/min<\/b>. BSA is already 1.73, so no correction is needed.<\/div>\n<p>Most labs now report an <b>eGFR<\/b> calculated from serum creatinine, age and sex (and sometimes <b>cystatin C<\/b>, which doesn't depend on muscle mass). The biggest error in a 24-hour collection is an <b>incomplete collection<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Inherited metabolic disorders seen in urine<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Phenylketonuria (PKU)<\/b>: can't break down phenylalanine; mousy odor; <b>ferric chloride turns green<\/b>. Detected by newborn screening.<\/li>\n<li><b>Alkaptonuria<\/b>: homogentisic acid builds up; urine <b>turns black on standing<\/b> (as it becomes alkaline and oxidises).<\/li>\n<li><b>Maple syrup urine disease<\/b>: branched-chain amino acids build up; sweet maple-syrup smell.<\/li>\n<li><b>Cystinuria<\/b>: a transport defect causes cystine stones and hexagonal crystals.<\/li>\n<li><b>Porphyrias<\/b>: Ehrlich-reacting porphobilinogen (Watson-Schwartz, Hoesch tests).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Urine pregnancy and drug testing<\/h3>\n<p><b>Pregnancy tests<\/b> detect <b>hCG<\/b>. Use <b>first-morning urine<\/b> (most concentrated) early in pregnancy. Very high hCG (molar pregnancy, later pregnancy) can cause a <b>hook effect<\/b> false negative; dilute and retest.<\/p>\n<p><b>Drug testing<\/b>: an immunoassay screen, then <b>GC-MS or LC-MS/MS confirmation<\/b> of positives. To detect tampering (adulteration or dilution), check that the sample's <b>temperature is 90-100 °F within 4 minutes<\/b> of collection, and check <b>specific gravity, creatinine and pH<\/b> (very dilute or odd values suggest tampering). Forensic tests require an unbroken <b>chain of custody<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Protein 5 g/day, oval fat bodies, low albumin and edema in a child. Diagnosis?<\/summary><p>Nephrotic syndrome (minimal change disease is the most common cause in children).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Hematuria, dysmorphic red cells, red cell casts and hypertension two weeks after strep throat. Diagnosis?<\/summary><p>Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (a nephritic syndrome).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Why does glucose appear in urine when blood glucose is above about 180 mg/dL?<\/summary><p>The proximal tubule's glucose carriers are saturated (the renal threshold is exceeded), so the extra glucose isn't reabsorbed.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A patient produces large volumes of urine with SG 1.002, even after water restriction. What is suggested?<\/summary><p>Diabetes insipidus (lack of ADH or kidney resistance to it).<\/p><\/details>"},"U4":{"min":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Body fluids other than blood and urine (CSF, the fluids around the lungs, heart, gut and joints, plus amniotic fluid, semen and stool) are tested to answer one main question: <b>what is causing this fluid to be abnormal: infection, inflammation, bleeding, cancer, or just fluid leaking under pressure?<\/b> Each fluid has a few classic tests that answer it.<\/p>\n\n<h3>CSF: collection and the three tubes<\/h3>\n<p>CSF is collected by lumbar puncture into numbered tubes, each sent to a different department:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tube 1: chemistry and serology<\/b>. It may contain a little blood or skin cells from the needle, which matters least for chemistry.<\/li>\n<li><b>Tube 2: microbiology<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Tube 3: cell count<\/b>. It has the least contamination from blood picked up during the puncture, so counts are most accurate.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>CSF is precious (it can't easily be recollected) and urgent: process it immediately, and keep microbiology samples at room temperature.<\/p>\n<p><b>Normal CSF<\/b>: clear and colorless; <b>0-5 white cells/µL<\/b> in adults (mostly lymphocytes and monocytes; newborns have more); <b>glucose about 60-70% of plasma glucose<\/b> (so always compare with a blood glucose); <b>protein 15-45 mg/dL<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Traumatic tap or real bleeding?<\/h3>\n<p>Sometimes the needle nicks a blood vessel. That blood should be distinguished from a real <b>subarachnoid hemorrhage<\/b> (SAH):<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Traumatic tap<\/b>: blood is heaviest in tube 1 and <b>clears by tube 3<\/b>; the supernatant (after spinning) is <b>clear<\/b>; the fluid may <b>clot<\/b> (fresh blood has fibrinogen).<\/li>\n<li><b>Subarachnoid hemorrhage<\/b>: <b>equal blood in all tubes<\/b>; the supernatant is <b>yellow (xanthochromia)<\/b>, because red cells that have been there for a few hours break down into oxyhemoglobin and then bilirubin. Old bleeding doesn't clot.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Meningitis patterns: think about what each germ does<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Bacterial<\/b>: bacteria eat glucose and attract <b>neutrophils<\/b>; inflammation leaks protein. So: <b>cloudy<\/b> fluid, <b>very high neutrophils<\/b>, <b>high protein<\/b>, <b>low glucose<\/b>. A medical emergency.<\/li>\n<li><b>Viral<\/b>: viruses don't consume glucose. So: <b>clear<\/b> fluid, <b>lymphocytes<\/b>, <b>normal or slightly high protein<\/b>, <b>normal glucose<\/b>. Usually milder.<\/li>\n<li><b>Fungal (Cryptococcus) or tuberculous<\/b>: slow, chronic. <b>Lymphocytes and monocytes<\/b>, <b>high protein<\/b>, <b>low glucose<\/b>. A fine \"pellicle\" or web may form in TB CSF.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b><b>Low glucose<\/b> = something is eating it: bacteria, fungi or TB. <b>Normal glucose with lymphocytes<\/b> = viral.<\/div>\n<p>Other CSF tests: <b>India ink<\/b> and cryptococcal antigen (Cryptococcus), Gram stain and culture, <b>VDRL<\/b> (the only test accepted on CSF for neurosyphilis), and <b>oligoclonal bands and the IgG index<\/b> (immunoglobulin made inside the brain, supporting <b>multiple sclerosis<\/b>).<\/p>\n\n<h3>Counting cells with a hemocytometer<\/h3>\n<p>Fluid cell counts are often done manually in a <b>Neubauer counting chamber<\/b>, which holds a known volume: each large square is 1 mm × 1 mm, and the chamber is 0.1 mm deep. The formula converts what you count into cells per µL:<\/p>\n<p><b>Cells/µL = (cells counted × dilution) ÷ (area counted in mm² × 0.1 mm depth)<\/b><\/p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Undiluted CSF, 45 cells counted in all 9 large squares. Volume counted = 9 mm² × 0.1 mm = 0.9 µL. Count = 45 ÷ 0.9 = <b>50 cells/µL<\/b>.<\/div>\n<p>For a <b>red cell count<\/b>, dilute with saline. For a <b>white cell count<\/b>, use <b>3% acetic acid (with methylene blue)<\/b>, which <b>lyses the red cells<\/b> so they don't get in the way, and stains the white cell nuclei. Count both sides of the chamber; they should agree closely. The <b>differential<\/b> is done on a <b>cytospin<\/b> slide, which concentrates the few cells onto a small spot.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Serous fluids: transudate or exudate?<\/h3>\n<p>Pleural, pericardial and peritoneal fluids build up in two ways:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Transudate<\/b>: fluid <b>pushed out by pressure<\/b> or pulled out by low protein, with healthy membranes. Low protein, few cells. Causes: <b>heart failure<\/b>, <b>cirrhosis<\/b>, <b>nephrotic syndrome<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Exudate<\/b>: fluid <b>leaking from inflamed or damaged<\/b> membranes. High protein and LD, many cells. Causes: <b>infection<\/b>, <b>cancer<\/b>, inflammation.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><b>Light's criteria<\/b>: the fluid is an <b>exudate if any one<\/b> is true:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>fluid protein ÷ serum protein <b>above 0.5<\/b>,<\/li>\n<li>fluid LD ÷ serum LD <b>above 0.6<\/b>,<\/li>\n<li>fluid LD <b>above two-thirds<\/b> of the upper limit of normal for serum LD.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>More clues: many <b>lymphocytes<\/b> suggest TB or cancer; a pleural <b>pH below 7.2<\/b> suggests an <b>empyema<\/b> (pus) that needs draining; milky fluid with <b>triglycerides above 110 mg/dL<\/b> is <b>chylous<\/b> (leaking lymph duct), while milky fluid rich in cholesterol is <b>pseudochylous<\/b> (long-standing effusion).<\/p>\n<p><b>Peritoneal (ascitic) fluid<\/b> uses the <b>SAAG<\/b> (serum-ascites albumin gradient) = serum albumin − fluid albumin. <b>SAAG ≥ 1.1 g/dL<\/b> means <b>portal hypertension<\/b> (cirrhosis, heart failure); lower values suggest other causes (cancer, TB, pancreatitis). More than <b>250 neutrophils/µL<\/b> in ascites means <b>spontaneous bacterial peritonitis<\/b>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><b>Worked example<\/b>Serum albumin 3.5, ascitic albumin 1.5. SAAG = <b>2.0 g/dL<\/b> (≥ 1.1) → portal hypertension, such as cirrhosis.<\/div>\n\n<h3>Synovial (joint) fluid<\/h3>\n<p>Normal joint fluid is <b>clear, pale yellow and very viscous<\/b> (hyaluronic acid makes it stringy), with <b>under 200 white cells/µL<\/b>. Disease groups:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Non-inflammatory<\/b> (osteoarthritis): slightly raised cells, good viscosity.<\/li>\n<li><b>Inflammatory<\/b> (rheumatoid arthritis, <b>gout<\/b>, pseudogout): cloudy, poor viscosity, raised neutrophils.<\/li>\n<li><b>Septic<\/b> (bacterial infection): very high white cells (often over 50,000), mostly neutrophils, <b>low glucose<\/b>, positive Gram stain and culture. An emergency.<\/li>\n<li><b>Hemorrhagic<\/b> (trauma, hemophilia).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Very viscous fluid can be treated with <b>hyaluronidase<\/b> before counting. Use saline (not acetic acid) as a diluent for synovial white counts, because acetic acid makes the hyaluronic acid clot into a mucin clump.<\/p>\n<p><b>Crystals<\/b> are identified with a <b>polarising microscope plus a red compensator<\/b>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Monosodium urate (gout)<\/b>: <b>needle-shaped<\/b>, <b>strongly negatively birefringent<\/b>, <b>yellow when parallel<\/b> to the compensator's slow axis.<\/li>\n<li><b>Calcium pyrophosphate (pseudogout)<\/b>: <b>rhomboid or rod<\/b>, <b>weakly positively birefringent<\/b>, <b>blue when parallel<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><b>Memory hook<\/b>Gout: \"<b>Y<\/b>ellow, <b>N<\/b>eedle, <b>N<\/b>egative\" (YNN). Pseudogout: \"<b>B<\/b>lue, <b>P<\/b>ositive, rhomboid\" (\"<b>BP<\/b>\").<\/div>\n\n<h3>Amniotic fluid, semen and stool<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Amniotic fluid<\/b>: <b>L/S ratio ≥ 2.0<\/b> (lecithin/sphingomyelin) and the presence of <b>phosphatidylglycerol<\/b> show <b>fetal lung maturity<\/b> (enough surfactant to breathe). High <b>AFP<\/b> suggests a <b>neural tube defect<\/b>. Bilirubin measurement (for HDFN) needs the sample <b>protected from light<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Semen<\/b>: collected after 2-7 days of abstinence and examined within <b>1 hour<\/b>; it should liquefy in 30-60 minutes. WHO lower reference limits (2021): volume about <b>1.4 mL<\/b>, concentration <b>16 million/mL<\/b>, total motility <b>42%<\/b>, normal forms (strict criteria) <b>4%<\/b>. (The older 2010 limits were 1.5 mL, 15 million/mL and 40%.) <b>Fructose<\/b> comes from the seminal vesicles, so it is absent if the ducts are blocked. <b>Eosin-nigrosin<\/b> stain checks if sperm are alive. After a <b>vasectomy<\/b>, success means <b>no sperm<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Stool<\/b>: the <b>fecal immunochemical test (FIT)<\/b> detects <b>human globin<\/b>, so it is more specific than the older <b>guaiac<\/b> test, which reacts with red meat and is affected by vitamin C. <b>Sudan III<\/b> screens for fat (steatorrhea, malabsorption); the quantitative 72-hour fecal fat is under 7 g/day normally. <b>Fecal elastase<\/b> tests pancreatic function.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>CSF: cloudy, 2,000 neutrophils/µL, protein 250 mg/dL, glucose 15 mg/dL. Cause?<\/summary><p>Bacterial meningitis.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Blood in CSF tubes 1 to 3 decreases steadily and the supernatant is clear. Traumatic tap or SAH?<\/summary><p>Traumatic tap.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Pleural fluid/serum protein ratio 0.6. Transudate or exudate?<\/summary><p>Exudate (ratio above 0.5 meets Light's criteria).<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Needle-shaped crystals that are yellow when parallel to the compensator. Which disease?<\/summary><p>Gout (monosodium urate, strong negative birefringence).<\/p><\/details>"},"U5":{"min":3,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Urinalysis and body fluid traps nearly always involve a test that <b>misses<\/b> something (a false negative) or two things that <b>look alike<\/b> under the microscope. Each listed trap is explained below, followed by more classics.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 1: \"a negative nitrite rules out UTI\"<\/h3>\n<p>The nitrite test only works if three things are true: the bacteria can <b>convert nitrate to nitrite<\/b> (mostly Gram-negative Enterobacterales), the urine sat in the bladder long enough (<b>about 4 hours<\/b>), and there was nitrate from the diet to convert. So nitrite is <b>falsely negative<\/b> with <b>Enterococcus<\/b>, staph and other Gram-positives, with frequent urination, with a low-vegetable diet, and with <b>vitamin C<\/b> (which interferes with the reaction). Use leukocyte esterase, microscopy and culture too.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 2: \"a negative dipstick protein means no protein\"<\/h3>\n<p>The protein pad reacts mainly with <b>albumin<\/b>. It misses <b>Bence Jones protein<\/b> (free light chains) and most globulins. If myeloma is suspected, use <b>sulfosalicylic acid (SSA)<\/b>, which precipitates all proteins, and specific tests (immunofixation, free light chains). The opposite trap: a very <b>alkaline<\/b> urine can make the pad <b>falsely positive<\/b>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 3: blood in CSF always means hemorrhage<\/h3>\n<p>Blood from a <b>traumatic tap<\/b> (the needle nicked a vessel) <b>clears<\/b> from tube 1 to tube 3, and the supernatant is clear. A true <b>subarachnoid hemorrhage<\/b> shows the same amount of blood in every tube and a <b>yellow (xanthochromic)<\/b> supernatant from broken-down red cells.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Trap 4: yeast counted as red cells<\/h3>\n<p>Yeast and red cells are a similar size and both look round and pale. Clues: <b>yeast buds<\/b>, has no central pallor, and may form pseudohyphae; red cells don't. Add <b>dilute acetic acid<\/b>: <b>red cells lyse<\/b>, yeast doesn't. Oil droplets are another mimic: they vary in size and are very refractile.<\/p>\n\n<h3>More urinalysis and fluid traps<\/h3>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Old urine<\/b>A sample left out for hours shows high pH, high bacteria, low glucose and ketones, low bilirubin, and dissolved casts. Don't report it as if fresh.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Vitamin C<\/b>It falsely lowers the glucose, blood, bilirubin, nitrite and leukocyte esterase pads.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Dipstick ketones in DKA<\/b>The pad misses beta-hydroxybutyrate, the main ketone in severe DKA.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Refractometer and contrast dye<\/b>Glucose, protein and X-ray contrast raise refractometer SG. A value above 1.040 suggests contrast dye, not real concentration.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Hemoglobin vs myoglobin<\/b>Both give a positive blood pad without red cells. Check the plasma color (pink = hemoglobin) and the CK (very high = myoglobin).<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Synovial fluid diluent<\/b>Acetic acid forms a mucin clot with synovial fluid. Use saline (or hyaluronidase) for cell counts.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Crystal birefringence<\/b>Gout crystals are yellow when parallel (strong negative); pseudogout crystals are blue when parallel (weak positive). Swapping the colors is a classic error.<\/div>\n<div class=\"box trap\"><b>Refrigerated CSF for culture<\/b>Never refrigerate CSF for bacterial culture; fastidious organisms die.<\/div>\n\n<h3>How to approach a urinalysis trap<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Check the specimen first: age, storage, collection type and contamination (squamous cells).<\/li>\n<li>Ask what each pad or test actually detects, and what it misses.<\/li>\n<li>Confirm with a second method: microscopy, SSA, Clinitest, culture or plasma color.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>Check your understanding<\/h3>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Leukocyte esterase positive, nitrite negative, many white cells, culture grows Enterococcus. Why was nitrite negative?<\/summary><p>Enterococcus doesn't reduce nitrate to nitrite.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>Round bodies in urine sediment don't dissolve with acetic acid and some are budding. What are they?<\/summary><p>Yeast.<\/p><\/details>\n<details class=\"sc\"><summary>A urine SG reads 1.050 on the refractometer after a CT scan. Why?<\/summary><p>X-ray contrast dye in the urine raises the refractometer reading. The dipstick or osmolality gives a truer value.<\/p><\/details>"}};
